Camille Auer wanted to correct her gender, was diagnosed as mentally ill, and now she's making art on the subject - "A person should have the right to their own body"
Dozens of patient stories hang from the ceiling of the Academy of Fine Arts' Project room gallery.
Each of them says Camille Auer's name.
The papers tell a multi-year story of how outside people have written down their interpretation of Auer and his identity.
Decided for him who he is.
"I wanted to tell my personal treatment history so that the violence in the process would become visible," Auer says.
In the process, he refers to the gender reassignment process.
It is often a year-long period of research that includes appointments with psychologists, psychiatrists, nurses and doctors.
The process will be launched with a referral for "gender identity disorder" diagnostic studies, which will be carried out in Tampere and Helsinki.
In Auer's case, the process started four years ago.
Even before this, he had used the estrogen he had ordered via the internet and was also very far advanced in his own repair process.
She felt that she was a woman, but according to Finnish law, a person must go through the repair process so that the gender can also be corrected legally.
The Trans Act, i.e. the Act on the Confirmation of the Transsexual Sex (553/2002), obliges transgender people to be forced to undergo forced sterilisation in order to obtain the right gender, for example, in the Population Register Centre and passports.
At this time, a psychiatric diagnosis and proof of infertility are required for each gender-remedial.
A person has the right to control their own body.
"This situation is intolerable and violates human rights," says Auer.
There has been a long-terment of reforming the Trans Act in Finland
The UN Human Rights Council has issued eight comments on the Act and the European Court of Human Rights has a preliminary ruling requiring the Parties to the European Convention on Human Rights to comply with self-determination.
Finland's previous government prepared an amendment to the Act under the leadership of Susanne Huovinen, Minister of Social Affairs and Health of Social Affairs and Health
There were elections and the Finns Party rose to the government.
Now the change in the law is stalled again.
It's about domestic political disputes that aren't even related to this law.
"The government doesn't want to put it on the table, because there are disagreements within the board about changing the law," says Frank Johansson, Executive Director of Amnesty International's Finnish department.
Two major installations dominate the front of the exhibition space.
One of them consists of the packaging and instructions for use of the drugs used by Auer.
In the Patient Records installation, any visitor can, for example, read the interpretation of Tampere University Hospital's transpolic psychologist from Auer.
The psychologist met Camille Auer twice and based on these wrote a report that would define Auer's right to receive treatment and assistance.
The subject's sense of reality appears vulnerable to faltering.
The researcher's perception of oneself appears fragmented at the boundary level, and the unstable traits related to personality are emphasized.
Auer didn't recognize himself from these descriptions.
"It was a shock to hear that I was diagnosed with mental illness," Auer says.
It was an even greater shock when Auer was not granted gender reassignment treatment.
According to the nursing staff, his resources were not sufficient to start the process.
He was denied his own identity.
That's February 11, 2015 ­Auer walked out of the meeting and kicked the TV in the transpole's anticipation lobby.
Later on that television a work of art called Monument for the Excluded was created, in which Auer reflects his experience of power and how to use it.
“It’s crazy that anyone at birth as a woman can magnify their breasts, but you need a diagnosis to remove your breasts,” Auer says.
Your body is not your own if you feel that you are anything other than the amount of gender defined at birth.
Auer's experience isn't the only one.
For many, the gender reassignment process is harder for mental reasons than purely because of medical operations.
Those who go through it meet dozens of people who need to be justified of their own existence and their right to their own sexual experience.
"It's crazy that people in need of support and help are denied care, claiming they don't have the resources," Auer says.
Many people who have applied for the process have psychological symptoms: depression, anxiety, possible bullying experiences.
On the basis of these, the medical staff may prescribe that treatment cannot be started.
"Preventing treatment and getting help can put a person in an even worse situation than it was before the start of the process," Auer says.
Auer's personal exhibition also deals with anxiety and thoughts about suicide.
In the text of the exhibition, Auer writes: "I've had suicidal thoughts throughout my adult life and it's something that people usually find it difficult to digest."
Depression has been treated with medication and when Auer started using hormones, I felt relieved.
He had finally been able to express the sensations that had already existed in his childhood and adolescence.
Why the wide jaw didn't seem to fit your own body and why the chest muscles had to be round instead of a flat chest.
The most important thing for Auer has been that there are people close to him who see him.
Being seen is a basic need.
"You have to justify yourself so much by talking, dressing, using sound and movements," she says.
Today Auer already feels seen, she exists as a woman.
However, in the early stages of the process, random comments from loved ones could hurt a lot.
It hurts the most when people close to you abuse.
"They should know and they know who I am, but they won't or won't internalize it," he continues.
However, not all trans people need physical changes.
They do not suffer from body dysphoria, i.e. body conflict, where the body or its parts can cause anxiety and even physical pain.
However, without the use of hormones and a long research process, it is not possible to obtain official reinforcement for one's own gender.
"There is no medical justification for these studies," Auer says.
And it's true.
In Denmark, for example, in June 2014, Parliament adopted a new procedure for the recognition of the legal gender.
The new legislation builds on self-determination in relation to legal gender.
An adult applicant submits a written application for a new personal identity code, indicating that he or she feels that he or she is of the other sex.
After a six-month period of reflection, the applicant confirms his/her application in writing and the Ministry assigns the applicant a new personal identity code.
This self-determination right is also demanded in Finland, and Auer's exhibition is crystallized in this requirement.
It has not been a therapeutic experience for the artist, but the exhibition is a political and artistic collection of works.
I want to believe that art continues to play a role and that it can bring about political changes, at a time when politicians have forgotten about human rights.
Camille Auer's exhibition Antiphallic Dick 4.-28.7.2017 at the Academy of Fine Arts Project Room.
A civil rights organization issued a travel warning to Missouri
NAACP, a national agency that promotes African-American civil rights, has issued a travel warning for Missouri due to discriminating practices in the state and the racist attacks that took place there.
‘NAACP’s travel advisory for Missouri comes into force on 28.8.2017 and urges African-American tourists, visitors and locals to pay special attention and to exercise extreme caution when traveling in a state where there have recently been numerous questionable race-based events,” the organization said in a statement.
The law, which recently came into force in Missouri, complicates the admission of cases of discrimination to justice. In addition, the implementation of state laws is disproportionately directed at minorities. According to the NAACP, these factors led to a travel warning.
‘People’s civil rights are violated.
“They will be stopped on the basis of their skin color and abused or killed,” Rod Chapel, president of NAACP’s Missouri, told Kansas City Star.
“We’ve never heard of as many cases in such a short period of time.”
A travel advisory for Missouri is the organization's first U.S. state warning.
For example, the organization cited racist comments against African-American students at the University of Missouri and the death of Tory Sanders, a 28-year-old black man from Tennessee.
Sanders died under questionable circumstances earlier this year. He was about to drive through the state when his car ran out of fuel. He was arrested by the Missouri police, even though he wasn't charged with the crime.
A travel bulletin also referred to a report by the Missouri Attorney General that police are 75 percent more likely to stop a black driver than white.
“The purpose of the travel bulletin is to urge people to be on guard and to warn their families, friends and co-workers about what could happen in Missouri,” Chapel said.
“People need to prepare, for example, by making money to pass bail or telling relatives that they’re going to travel through the state.”
According to the FBI, one hundred hate crimes were recorded in Missouri in 2015, the sixteenth most of the U.S. states.
A travel warning is also a response to Missouri's new law, which makes it difficult for companies to sue for discrimination in housing and employment.
ACLU recently issued travel warnings to Texas and Arizona after accepting immigration laws that oblige the police to detain people for immigration violations. According to the organization, this increases racial-based profiling.
As a rule, travel warnings are issued by the Department of Foreign Affairs and apply to other countries. However, recently, NGOs have published them in response to certain laws and developments in the United States.
Lintilä asked Finnair for a report on the CEO's supplementary pension
Minister of Economic Affairs Mika Lintilä (Centre Party), who is responsible for state ownership steering, has asked Finnair for a report on the supplementary pension contribution received by the airline's CEO Pekka Vauramo, Yle says.
According to the minister, it contradicts the state's declaration of intent.
Vauramo's additional pension of 130 000 euros was highlighted this week when Helsingin Sanomat reported on the remuneration development of the directors of listed companies.
Suomen Kuvalehti reported on Vauramo's supplementary pension in February.
Finnair said it would phase out supplementary pensions in 2013.
The state has stated that supplementary pensions will not be used for remuneration.
The State owns more than half of Finnair.
According to the company, the decision is in line with last year's decision-in-principle on ownership steering.
Sanctions immediately loudeness speeches
Until recently it seemed that relations between the United States and Russia were on their way from the cold to the warm.
U.S
Since then, the mood has cooled considerably.
New economic sanctions, approved by the U.S. congress last week and signed by Trump on Wednesday, reacted bitterly in Russia.
The most stunning response has so far been heard from Prime Minister Dmitry Medvedev, according to a "full-fledged trade war".
Even if Russian reactions were only offset in political rhetorical escalations and excesses, tensions between the United States and Russia are a fact after a short month of honeymoon following Trump's election.
In the United States, it was not simple to pass the Sanctions Act on Russia, Iran and North Korea.
However, Congress accepted the law with considerable consensus, but for the President, the adoption of the law was considerably more difficult, according to reports.
Trump recently criticized the bill he signed as "significantly flawed."
It must have been Russia's wrinkle, in particular, even thought that it must have been disgusted by the adoption of a law restrictinging one's own powers.
As is well known, there will be an investigation in the United States, both of Russia's possible interference in the presidential election and the Trump campaign's ties to Russia.
The tension between the United States and Russia is a fact after a short honeymoon.
As White House staff have switched frequently and various scandalous twists have been almost updated, it increasingly seems that foreign policy in the United States under the leadership of the president, but despite the president.
Such a situation is by no means desirable.
If the relationship with Russia is complicated in the United States, the same applies to the European Union.
In addition to economic sanctions, the tense security situation in the Union's core area in the Baltic Sea will also tighten relations.
The EU has prominently highlighted the situation in Ukraine in its relations with Russia and called for the implementation of the Minsk agreement.
Even though the Union has so far remained at least on the surface in its Russia policy, the interests of the Member States are quite different.
For a member state that does a lot of trade with Russia, economic sanctions have been a real nuisance.
The effects of the recent U.S. Sanctification Act are still unclear.
There are fears in the EU that sanctions will hamper the activities of many European companies.
In Finland, the effects may be felt, for example, in the Nord Stream 2 gas pipeline project and possibly in Fennovoima's nuclear power plant project.
Acidity in Europe has been caused by the fact that, for example, the U.S. liquid natural gas lng, which is important for the US economy, has been excluded from the sanctions.
Trade sanctions are a means in world politics as a way to show resentment.
However, economic sanctions must be well-thoughttered and sacrificial.
By the way, they are easily more harmful than useful.
Isn't anything sacred anymore?
Trump's White House is now aiming for the Statue of Liberty
Intervening in statues is the surest sign of a change of power.
Americans know it just as well as everyone else: Iraqis, together with American soldiers, toppled Saddam Hussein's Statue in Firdos Square in 2003.
Stephen Miller, one of the chief ideologues of the Trump administration, was well aware of what he was doing during a heated debate with CNN channel Jim Acosta
Miller didn't really criticize the statue itself.
If he had done so, he would have been attacked by huge herds of cruise organizers and figures imitating the Statue of Liberty.
His attack focused on the significance of the statue and, in particular, the fact that in 1903 an Emma Lazarus poem was added to the pedestal – 17 years after the completion of the statue.
“I don’t want to go into history right now, but the Statue of Liberty is... a symbol of American freedom that illuminates the whole world.
The poem was later added and is not part of the original Statue of Liberty.”
Miller wasn't wrong, but it'sn't just history, as he said.
Lazarus's touching words are essential, the only ones in which the state invites not only immigrants, but “the poorest of the poors”: “Your tired, your poor, ‘Your huddled masses yearning to breathe free,’ ‘The wretched refuse of your teeming shore.’
Poetry is now more topical than ever.
Miller didn’t engage in literary criticism. He made it clear that “the poorest of the poors” aren’t welcome in Trump’s United States.
The controversy over the significance of the Statue of Liberty is part of a wider political and cultural controversy over the importance of the United States.
Lazarus's friends campaigned after his death that the poem would be attached to the pedestal of the statue. They knew very well that the act was strongly political.
The purpose of the statue was to embody the connections of French and American republicanism with a classical metaphor, the feminine embodiment of freedom.
Lazarus changed its meaning: in his poem, the female figure is no longer abstract, but has a voice.
And it gives itself a new name: the mother of the deportees.
The inclusion of the poem was an ingenious feminist coup and a destructive attack on the American nationalism.
Lazarus changed the meaning of the statue, and the right has long wanted to turn it back into the original.
Rush Limbaugh – a mouth-writter who can now be regarded as some kind of prognostic about Trump – argued in 2010 that the Stature of Liberty doesn’t invite anyone, but it’s an early pioneer of the neo-Conservatists, which will take the U.S. torch into a world covered in darkness:
He takes the torch of freedom from the United States to the rest of the world.
“The torch doesn’t lead the way to the United States
Rarely, Limbaugh was partially right.
The significance of the Statue of Liberty depends entirely on the point of view.
Lazarus saw it as poor and exhausted but hopeful immigrants from the deck of the ship after a long and trying journey.
He saw it from the point of view of people looking for shelter and a better life.
His Statue of Liberty is comforted by giving hope and welcoming.
And that's what Miller and Trump don't want under any circumstances.
Their torch does not show the way, but incites the flames of wrath, their only source of power.
Athletics World Championships 2017: Neil Black praises the Scottish members of the British national team
Eilidh Doyle remembers a time when only two Scots reached the British national team
Neil Black, head of the UK Athletics Federation, says the 16 Scot members of the national team participating in the World Cup are “significant confirmation.”
A record number of Scottish athletes have been selected for the Athletics World Championships starting in London on Friday.
According to Black, Scotland “has something special going on,” of which British athletics must learn from.
“This is welcome, and we will continue to explore it until we understand what’s going on,” Black said.
‘I had a conversation with representatives of the Scottish Institute of Sport, and they thought it had anything to do with the Institute.
The Scottish Athletics Federation, on the other hand, believes that it relates to the union's activities,
The British Athletics Federation believes it is based on their activities.
And an ordinary streetwalker thinks he must have something to do with it.
But it’s something amazing, whatever it’s about.”
Many Scottish athletes compete in London in earnest for medals, including Laura Muir and Andrew Butchart, who competes with Sir Mo Farah in the 5,000-meter race, while Eilidh Doyle was selected as captain of the national team.
‘Eilidh is too modest.
His captain's speech was unbelievably wonderful," Black says.
‘Athletes left the event proudly of their achievements and strongly motivated, no matter where they happened to live and practice.
Eilidh’s words had a genuine feeling and a passion for sports.”
Eilidh Doyle is a silver medalist in the 400-meter hurdles of the Commonwealth Championships, and these are his fifth World Championship. He thinks it’s “unbelievable” that there are 16 Scots in the national team.
The previous record was seven from 1983 and 2015.
Middle-distance runner Laura Muir has run five British and two European records during the year.
He will compete in London in the 1,500 meters and 5 000 meters.
Chris O’Hare will run 1,500 metres in London. He should also be kept in mind: he has an excellent season behind him, which includes victories in the UK World Cup qualifiers and Anniversary Games.
Doyle remembers his first World Cup, when Lee McConnell was the only Scotsman in the national team.
The captain of the national team says he is ‘hugely proud’ that there are so many Scots involved, some of whom are seriously competing for places and medals in the final.
‘Becaising captaincy is a great honour anyway, but it’s even more special because the members of the national team voted for the captain,” Doyle said.
“Of course, I’m very proud of all the Scots that made it to the national team.
It shows how far we've come.
They are involved as distinguished athletes competing for podiums.
Captain's Speech?
I thought it went just fine.
I focused on how lucky we are when we're at home.
It's important to enjoy the support of the home crowd and that we're back at the Olympic Stadium.
I get a second chance to compete there and I know that the atmosphere is going to be amazing.”
Preventing critics on Facebook: Don’t run for a public office if you can’t handle criticism
Our country's father knew what bad publicity was.
The Americans loved George Washington, but newspapers soon began to criticize him for all possible domestic politics all the way to his political principles.
Criticism must have annoyed,
But he didn't silence his critics.
“If the freedom of speech is abolished, we may be led stupid and silent like sheep to be slaughtered,” Washington said in 1783.
That's how we'll get to Maryland Governor Larry Hogan, who's supposed to learn from Washington.
Hogan's staff have removed at least 450 comments from his official Facebook page and blocked their authors.
Hogan was sued by the U.S. Civil Rights Association earlier this week.
His staff considers the challenge unfounded, and the attitude was also dismissive online.
“What does it matter? It’s just Facebook,” many commented.
But that matters,
Especially in Hogan's case.
Hogan is an astonishingly popular Republican governor in a state dominated by Democrats.
He has received the second-bestest ratings among the 50 governors.
Hogan isn't easily responsive to hothead.
He has steadfastly led the state and adhered strictly to the principles.
She has also competently used Facebook as her main channel of contact with her constituents and posted a picture of her bald end in chemotherapy.
That’s why it’s unnecessary and even dangerous to block users who appear on the governor’s Facebook page. The governor’s page is a public and official forum, which is maintained by his staff with tax funds.
Hogan spokesman Amelia Chasse defended the governor's actions in an interview with the Washington Post.
However, labeling commenters as trolls, spammers or haters is too easy a solution.
The Washington Post interviewed users blocked by Hogan on Facebook.
They turned out to be ordinary people who wanted to talk to the leaders of their choice. They were not trolls, but, for example, teachers, entrepreneurs and pastors.
They said that their comments were respectful, thoughtful and matter-of-fact.
The pastor quoted the Bible in his comment; he wanted to appeal to Hogan's Catholic faith.
Attorney Lakshmi Sarma Ramani of Maryland's Bowie was not hostile and asked a question about hate crimes.
“Politely, I’m politely disappointed that Hogan hadn’t commented on hate crimes and other recent news,” he tweeted.
‘I also cannot accept the fact that when several people comment on the same issue, they are immediately regarded as an organized group in some way and ignored as a large group of concerned citizens.’
The governor’s staff called the comments a “centralized attack.” Someone else might call them to promote what’s considered important.
In the age of Facebook, it's easy to manage communication by blocking the reviewer or deleting a critical comment.
In the old days, staff should have been sent to collect and burn newspapers where critical editorials had been published, or arrested and silenced by demonstrators.
It’s really the same thing, now it’s just digitally.
Hogan is not the first public official to be accused of shacking through Facebook and other social media channels.
Twitter users blocked by President Trump have sued him.
The first major decision was made last week in Virginia.
According to Alexandria District Court judge James C. Cacheris, the Loudoun county government violated the U.S
And the U.S. People's Rights League went on a conversation with Kentucky's Republican Governor Matt Bevin about the use of Twitter and Facebook.
It shouldn't be difficult.
In Washington's time, inexpensive postage had the same effect as the internet today.
The number of newspapers quadrupled from 1776 to 1800, with anonymous writers hammering Washington.
There were trolls at that time.
Juricola, Valerius, Belisarius and Portius wrote letters to the newspapers in which they slammed Washington's decisions.
The president's office was flooded with petitions criticizing Jay's agreement, i.e
However, he did not silence his critics.
Freedom of expression, freedom of opinion and freedom of discussion are the cornerstones of our nation.
True leadership means accepting this fact.
It is advisable to stop feeding the birds immediately
Samples have come from about half a dozen places, as well as online advertisements about a couple, three.
Most of them have come from Southern and Southwest Finland, but then also individuals from Haapavesi and Seinäjoki, says Senior Researcher Marja Isomursu from Evira.
Greenfinches have died in a parasitice called trichomonosis.
It is caused by Trichomonas gallinae a protozoa.
It has previously been known to hit pigeons, and the pigeon in particular.
However, in the 21st century, the disease also began to be detected in finches.
In 2009, it really worked out.
At that time, trichomonosis caused a major collapse in Finland's greenfinch population, after which the species' strains have been recovering.
There have now been sick greenfinches, but at least not yet on a large scale," says Juha Honkala, Museum Master of the Finnish Museum of Natural History (Luomus) at the University of Helsinki.
It can be feared that the previous epidemic of greenfinches will recur again if something is not done.
What needs to be done now is a very simple thing that can be done by everyone: to stop feeding the birds immediately.
More on the subject later.
Ronan Rafferty hopes to take advantage of home-court advantage at Renaissance Club
Former Europe's number one Ronan Rafferty hopes to take advantage of the home advantage for the next three days when the Renaissance Club in East-Lothian hosts the 25th Scottish Senior Open tournament.
Rafferty from Northern Ireland left last year in a tournament held next to Archerfield Links, but eventually lost to Paul Eales. Rafferty is attracted to a new place and serves as the tournament's ambassador on behalf of the club's founder and CEO, Jerry Sarvad.
‘I followed the construction of the Renaissance Club, and with this event, it will have its full glory,’ Rafferty says.
‘Players can see the field at its best. It’s in great shape.
Jerry's been doing a great job with this place.
He's an avid ambassador of the game, and he's proud of what he's accomplished here.
The tournament is a great presentation for this field.”
Eales is defending the championship for the first time, even though he has won both the European Tour and the European Challenger Tour in his 32-year career.
“This feels like a really special opportunity,” says a Southport player.
“I didn’t get to defend my Extremadura Open victory because the tournament is not part of the tour this year.
It'll be great to come back here.
It's a great place to play golf.
Last year's memories come to mind: a tournament win was a great thing for me and my wife Sharon.
It was a great time that we will always remember.”
Gary Orr will return to action on his own soil. He went on tour earlier this year after turning 50.
This is his first appearance in Scotland since the 2013 Aberdeen Asset Management Scottish Open.
‘Playing at home is always more meaningful than usual,” says Orr, who lives in Helensburgh.
“You want to succeed, but you’ll also feel extra pressure.
I've enjoyed playing again and I've been playing quite strongly.
I’m happy with my game so far.”
The trio will be joined by former Masters winner Ian Woosnam and senior major tournament winners Paul Broadhurst, Roger Chapman, Mark Jams and Mark McNulty.
The tournament will be attended by six Scots: Orr and former Ryder Cup captain Sam Torrance, Andrew Oldcorn, Stephen McAllister, Bill Longmuir and Ross Drummond.
It is free of access to the tournament. Car parking costs five pounds if the car has only the driver, and ten pounds if there are at least two people in the car.
All proceeds will be donated to Prostate Cancer UK.
Possible indoor air problems in the barracks were investigated in Säkylä
No indoor air problems have been found in the barracks building of the Pori Brigade's Relay Company in Säkylä, at least for the time being, the brigade said on Thursday.
Indoor air surveys are still ongoing.
Investigations began when a man who interrupted his military service for health reasons thought his health had deteriorated due to poor indoor air.
According to Lieutenant Colonel Mika Holma from the Pori Brigade, no problems have been encountered in the health situation of other conscripts.
It turned out that this was an isolated incident, and there have been no health problems among those who served in that unit.
Every announcement is taken seriously and we wanted to find out this matter thoroughly.
At the moment, it is not known that we have any indoor air problems," Holma says.
The brigade's press release states that due to the arrival batch in July, goods have had to be transferred to the barracks, which is why the dustiness may have temporarily increased.
Because of that, the air may have seemed stuffy, Holma says.
Poverty and Death in the Indonesian Goldland
Bardina Degei doesn't use stove in cooking.
He rarely uses a boiler, too.
Degei lives in a wooden building in Enarotali, the capital of the Paniai region, located in Indonesia’s restless Papua province. When he prepares dinner, he usually puts the sweet potato – locally “nota” – directly into the fireplace.
After half an hour, the sweet potato that has been burnt on the horse is eaten greedily with bare, unwashed hands.
Degei's home doesn't have furniture. He sits on a muddy ground floor, where he usually also does his daily chores, such as laundry in turbid water from a nearby swamp.
The office of the toilet is provided by a bucket in a roofless room.
Degei is the youngest of her husband's four wives, and she has not been ordered to take care of the fields.
Polygamy is common here.
Working late can be dangerous: the majority of men in the village are unemployed, and many drink a lot. In addition, it is necessary to take into account soldiers.
‘No one dares to walk around the village after five in the evening,’ Degei says.
This is a rare glimpse into everyday life in the Papuan highlands, the former Dutch colony, which was annexed to Indonesia in 1996.
The local independence movement, which opposed the Dutch power, quickly turned its anger on the Jakarta government, which maintains strict control of the region and prevents foreign journalists and human rights observers from accessing it.
In 2003, the province was officially divided into Papua and Western Papua. The eastern part of the island belongs to Papua New Guinea.
Enarotali is secluded and dreary. The journey there begins with a one-and-a-half-hour flight from the provincial capital Jayapura to Nabire and continues with a five-hour drive with a rental car.
There's no public transport here.
In the city of about 19 000 inhabitants there are wooden houses surrounded by bamboo fences, the corrugated tin roofs of which have swirled yellow-brown.
Few Indonesians have been here, not to mention journalists and foreigners.
Prior to the arrival of Christian missionaries, Papua's Mee Pago tribes worshiped the god Uga Tamee.
There have also been other changes.
‘We didn’t have clothes like this,’ Degei says. She means her colourful, hand-woven wrapping hat, dark skirt, and brightly colored blouse.
“Before, we only had leaves covered our bodies.”
Papua is Indonesia's poorest province. 28% of its population lives below the poverty line, and infant mortality and illiteracy are among the highest in Asia.
But it's also Indonesia's gold country.
Paniai is roughly the size of New Jersey with a population of 153 000 inhabitants. Only about a hundred kilometers from Paniai is Grasberg, the world’s largest and most lucrative gold mine. It is owned by Freeport McMoran, a company based in Phoenix in the United States.
In 2015, the mine produced 3.1 billion dollars worth of gold and copper for Freeport.
In addition, Papua has an estimated 78-billion-dollar timber reserves.
For the Papuans, however, these riches bring misery, as they mean the suggestive presence of Indonesia's strong army.
In 2005, the New York Times revealed that Freeport paid local military personnel and local military units nearly US$20 million between 1998 and 2004. Individual officers were paid up to US$150,000.
Papua's demands for greater self-determination threaten this source of income and will be silenced mercilessly.
According to human rights activists, since 1969, the Indonesian army has killed more than 500 papuans and raped, tortured and captured thousands.
According to the Asian Human Rights Commission, massacres in the highlands of the Papua tribes in the 1970's reached genocide proportions.
Last year, Indonesian police arrested more than 3,900 people in peaceful protests.
The Archdiocese of Brisbane's We Will Lose Everything 2016 reports on previous year's atrocities, such as executions without trial, torture – rape and electrocution are particularly popular means – and from relentless attacks towards peaceful demonstrations.
“It’s difficult to count the number of casualties because there are cases every week,” says Andreas Harsono, an Indonesian researcher at Human Rights Watch.
The screw has been tightened as Papua's natural resources attract migrants from other parts of Indonesia.
83% of Province's 3.5 million inhabitants are Christians, but the demographic structure is changing as Muslims are moving Muslims from Indonesia's populous islands of Java, Borne, Sumatra and Sulawes to the region for a better livelihood.
Javese warung stalls sell fried chicken and gado-gado vegetable mix in peanut sauce.
It's hard for locals to compete.
“Migrants have started to sell chicken and vegetables in the market at a lower price than the locals,” says 24-year-old Abeth You, who is from Pania and moved to the provincial capital for work.
“Local women lost their clients.”
Indonesia’s President Joko Widodo – nicknamed ‘Yokowi’ – pledged in his 2014 election campaign to address Papua’s injustices and human rights violations.
Papua consists of 29 districts, and this former carpenter won a vote in 27 districts, including Paniai, on his way to the Presidential Fortress in Jakarta.
But little has changed in Papua, and the locals feel betrayed.
“Our hearts were shattered because we voted for Jokowi in 2014 and waited for him to fulfill our wishes and restore justice,” You said.
Paniai experienced some kind of wave base just two months after the inauguration of Jokowi in October.
Eleven children were singing Christmas carols by the campfire in Enarotali on 7.12.2014, when two Indonesian soldiers on motorcycles appeared from the dark.
Frightened children told the soldiers that they should use headlights.
According to local pastor Yavedt Tebai, the man returned with four soldiers.
According to the stories of the victims and witnesses, the drunken soldiers apprehended the children and assaulted them with rifles.
That's how one of the soldiers shot at the children.
One of the children, 16-year-old Yulianus Yeimo, fell into a coma as a result of the assault.
A couple of hours later, the building of the State Electoral Board, which was located near the scene, was set on fire, and the next day began.
Approximately a thousand Papuans – young men and women and children – gathered at the football field in front of a local police station and army command center to demand justice.
They were accompanied by hunting bows for celebrations, and they began to perform the traditional waita dance of the Papua Mee Pago tribe, where they danced in a ring and imitate birdsong.
Some of the demonstrators began stoning police and military buildings.
As the mood heated up, the soldiers were commanded in the internal radio system: “If crowds resist more than three times, shoot them to death.” The information is based on an official document seen by TIME magazine that has not been made public to the local media.
Yeremias Kayame, 56, resident leader of the Kego Koto area, anticipated that the situation would get out of hand and asked the crowd to calm down and return to their homes.
No one wanted to listen anymore.
‘When I turned around, I was shotted on my left wrist,’ he told TIME on the porch of his wooden house painted in bright color.
Kayame still doesn't know who shot him, but the bullet came from the soldiers' ranks.
‘Several shots were fired in the crowd.’
Alfius Youwi was shot three times. His cousin saw the shootings.
‘I ran to him and made sure it really was he,’ said Yohanes to the TIME newspaper with sadness. Like many Indonesians, he uses only one name.
‘I saw that he was dead, I kissed him.’
Yotje Mende told reporters that the police only “protected” the police station because it was under attack.
“We need to defend ourselves when people threaten to kill us,” Papua police spokesman Pudjo Sulistiyo said in 2015.
“It’s about life and death.”
According to Human Rights Watch, five young demonstrators were killed and many were injured.
Information about the killings came to Jakarta the next day.
Three weeks later, Jokowi gave an emotional speech in Jayapura expressing his condolences to the families of the victims and promised that Papua's violence would be tackled.
‘I want the case to be investigated immediately, so that nothing like this will ever happen again.’
Security Minister Wiranto said in October 2016 that he would initiate reports of human rights violations outside the justice system.
However, he immediately began to withdraw his speeches.
“Most violations occurred a long time ago,
In the 1990's and the early 2000's.
The most important thing is that we have decided to investigate the violations, but certain processes are needed for that,” he said.
In an interview with TIME-magazine in Jakarta 5.6.2017, Wiranto had already changed his mind. He said that he did not intend to start investigations in Papua.
‘Everything will be determined in court,’ he said.
Wiranto is accused by the UN of ‘crimes against humanity’. The crimes relate to the deaths of more than a thousand people in East Timor’s bloody independence referendum in 1999. Wiranto told TIME magazine that 11 cases have already been cleared about human rights violations in Papua, including the events of Paniai.
Families of Paniai's victims view the allegations with dark disbelience.
“I’ve been interviewed four times in the last three years, but things haven’t progressed in any way,” Yohanes says.
‘I’m already tired of this.’
He says he still lives in fear, even though it's been years ago.
‘I’m afraid,’ he said.
‘I’m afraid the army will arrest me, and I’m afraid I’ll be shot.’
His brother Yacobus confirms that the Paniists do not dare to discuss the event.
He says the soldiers had assaulted him when he had helped burial four of the victims.
“Soldiers were looking for me after the victims were buried,” he said.
Shooting hasn't stopped.
On Tuesday, Indonesian police shot villagers in Deiya, Neighbouring Paniai.
One was killed and 17 injured in a conflict between the villagers and the construction company's manager, which began when the manager refused to take the man who lost his consciousness to the hospital.
Ravianus Douw, a 24-year-old man in need of hospital treatment, had been stranded in water while fishing on a nearby river. He died on the way to the hospital.
Angried villagers demonstrated at a construction site in front of the company's office.
According to the police, the villagers stoned police officers who responded with warning shots.
According to the locals, however, a mobile police unit of the Indonesian paramilitary police opened fire towards the crowd, killing one villager.
‘We are terrified. We’re afraid of revenge,’ says 29-year-old Dominggu Badii, who lives near the hospital and saw the wounded being brought for treatment.
“I’ve been hiding at home for two days.”
Deiyai's parliament has demanded that the police officers who fired the shots be held accountable and that the mobile police unit be withdrawn from the area.
Paniai has always been a problem for the Indonesian government.
The lack of real development fuels discontent in the Mee, Moni, Dani and Damal tribes, who live in the lush central highlands of Papua.
Many have joined the Papua Liberation Movement (OPM), an army of rebels who claim to be defending the rights of Pawans with occasional attacks and kidnappings by Indonesian army soldiers.
Some of OPM's executives are from Paniai, such as Tadius Yogi and Daniel Yudas Kogoya.
Thousands of people have been arbitrarily restrained and imprisoned in Pania in the name of the “safeguarding of national sovereignty.”
Some of those arrested will never come back.
Among Papuans, Paniai is known as the “sad, forgotten place.”
Poverty feeds discontent.
There's only a small amount of rice for sale in Enarotal, and it's too expensive for the locals,
Just like the bread.
People grow their food themselves: mainly sweet potatoes and some fruits and leafy vegetables.
Cultivation is the work of women, and one woman can be responsible for four or five sweet potato fields.
Most of the harvest is for the family, and the rest is sold at the market.
Ten sweet potatoes costs only 10 000 rupees (75 cents).
Over time, differences in the livelihoods of locals and migrants have widened, and the number of migrants has increased since new routes were opened to Nabire Airport.
There are few jobs, and they usually go to better-trained and wealthy migrants than locals.
Few Papuans have enough capital and know-how to run their own business competitively.
“Young people don’t want to stay in the village because there’s no work or money here,” said John Gobai, chairman of the Paniai Tribal Council.
There is very little attention in the world.
In addition, restrictions on international news coverage are still strict.
Earlier this year, French journalists Franck Escudie and Basille Longchamp were expelled from Papua, although they had been granted a rare filming licence. The official reason was the inability to cooperate with local institutions.
Phelim Kine, Deputy Director of Human Rights Watch in Asia, says Jokowi hasn't kept his election promises. He hasn't increased transparency and hasn't promoted development by liftinging news restrictions.
‘Reporters reporting on Indonesia’s most troubled eastern provinces of Papua and Western Papua are facing new risks: refusing a visa and blacklisting,” Kine said in a statement.
The lack of freedom of the press means that the Indonesian government is pressured only by Papua's immediate neighbours.
In March, six Pacific states – Tonga, Nauru, Palau, Tuvalu, Marshall Islands and Solomon Islands – urged the UN Human Rights Council to investigate Papua’s ‘numerous and widespread violations’, including the Pania attack.
These states have traditionally also supported Papua's liberation movement.
Arrmanatha Nasir, a spokesman for the Indonesian Foreign Ministry, denied the accusations and told reporters in Jakarta that ‘Indonesia is still a democracy with freedom of the press, so it would be difficult to conceal evidence of human rights violations.’
The locals want foreign governments to intervene.
When an official Dutch delegation led by human rights envoy Kees Van Bar visited Jayapura on 4.5.2017, the locals violated their silence and expressed their desire for freedom, according to an anonymous fountain who attended the meeting.
There will be a presidential election in Indonesia in 2019, but Pawans say they are no longer likely to vote on Jokow.
‘Jokowi means well, but there are people around him who are entangled in Paniai’s shooting,’ said Gobai.
He wants Jokow to know the situation of the Paniists: they live under the power of a greedy and unpredictable army in deprivation and poverty, practically without health services and educational opportunities.
According to Gobai, the Paniates, like the other Pawans, consider the votes they have given Jokowi as a “debt”, which must be repaid.
‘They don’t want money. They just want justice,’ he says.
Despite threatening and intimidation, the relatives of the victims of Paniai's shooting once again showed metaphorical resistance: they buried one of the victims in the country opposite the police station and military office.
They know that they may never get justice, but at least they won't let those responsible forget their crimes.
‘My family members were killed,’ Yacobus said with a head.
‘What else could we do?’
Dereck Chisora: ‘Even his own fans don’t want to see
Heavyweight boxers Robert Helenius and Dereck Chisora were scheduled to fight on May 27 at the Hartwall Arena.
However, the match was canceled for commercial reasons.
In a recent IFL TV interview, Chisora swirls the reason for flopping the rematch completely on Helenius' shoulders.
My match in Finland flopped, but it wasn't my fault.
I don't live in Finland, I don't want to sell tickets there.
It was my Finnish opponent.
Even his fans don't want to see her fight because she's terrible.
Helenius and Chisora met for the first time in the Hartwall Arena in December 2011.
Helenius won the match with a controversial scoring.
Ticket sales for the rematch went sluggishly, and its promoter Nisse Sauerland told Ilta-Sanomat in May that the match had been postponed to the autumn.
There has been nothing new about the match.
However, the cancellation no longer bothers Chisora.
The match would probably have had to be cancelled after Chisora got sick anyway.
On Thursday, three days before the match, I woke up to severe pain.
My hernia was about to explode.
“The hospital said I needed surgery,” Chisora said.
The Briton says he is currently in about 45% condition.
By all accounts, he's not interested in a rematch with Helenius, he's aiming for Dillian Whyte.
Chisora suffered Whyte's loss in December.
The fire took over the Torch skyscraper in Dubai for the second time
Flames captured one of the world's tallest residential buildings in Dubai on Friday. Hundreds of residents were forced to escape from a 337-meter-high 79-storey building.
This was the second fire within two years in a building called Torch, a torch.
The building was emptied, and no one is known to have been injured. There was no idea of the cause of the fire immediately after the incident.
“We were sleeping and we woke up to the sound of the fire alarm and people’s screams.
“We ran down the stairs on the 50th floor, which took about ten minutes,” a resident named George told Reuters.
According to Dubai's rescue authorities, firefighting forces were able to extinguish the fire at four o'clock in the morning local time and remained to cool the building.
The government announced that it would arrange accommodation for those who left their homes.
The case may raise again questions about the safety of materials used on the exterior surfaces of tall buildings around the world.
After the 2015 fire, Torch's board commissioned a study to find that the boards used to cloak the façade and insulate suffered the most damage.
In the UK, police believe the insulation and cladding plates of the Grenfell Tower in London may have contributed to the rapid propagation of flames in a fire in which 80 people were killed in June.
A moped was stolen from a parking lot in Joensuu during the night
A moped was stolen from a parking lot in Joensuu between 21 o'clock on Tuesday and 5 o'clock on Wednesday.
The moped disappeared from Niinivaara at Tikkamäentie 2.
Moped by Sky Team Monkey.
It's red in color, and the registration number is 27-REV.
The moped was found later Thursday.
