In the United States, more than 20 percent of air passengers take banned items on board, according to a survey
A survey of air passengers reveals that more than 20 percent of passengers in the U.S. had deliberately or unknowingly exported prohibited items to aircraft past U.S. Transportation Safety Agency (TSA) checkpoints. At least six percent of passengers had knives or other bladed items that they didn’t know were prohibited.
Less than one per century said they only found out afterwards that they had mistakenly transported weapons, ammunition or explosives.
More than a thousand passengers responded to the survey and was carried out by Stratos Jet Charters. The results also reveal that young passengers are more likely to defy the rules.
19.7 per cent were representatives of Generation Y and less than 15 per cent belonged to Generation ‘X’.
The most common rules were breached by taking food and fluids to the aircraft.
More than three per centiments admitted to deliberately taking blade-mounted items onto the aircraft, and 2.2 per cent of women and 3.7 per cent of men said they deliberately exported banned drugs to the aircraft.
Passengers from all over the United States participated in the survey. The company that conducted the survey wanted to find out how many people had accidentally taken smuggling goods past the security inspection and how many had done so consciously.
It’s not surprising that the smuggling goods are also most likely to be taken away by the smuggling – consciously or unknowingly.
But as the company that conducted the survey states, TSA's instructions are quite complex and confusing.
Passengers often don't know what liquids are allowed to be taken to an airplane and how much.
So it's no surprise that the majority of TSA confiscations are liquid.
For a while, there was also the belief that books should not be taken to an airplane.
Fluids may be exported for domestic flights, but not for international flights.
Scott Pruitt, the head of the U.S. Environmental Protection Agency, swirled his decision to delay decisions made under Obama to reduce emissions
A day after 15 states sued Scott Pruitt, Director of the U.S. Environmental Protection Agency (EPA), Pruitt withdrew his previous decision to delay the execution of decisions made during Obama's administration that would reduce smoggling emissions.
According to Pruitt, the change shows that the agency under his leadership will take better account of the needs of decision-makers responsible for environmental matters in the Land.
Pruitt did not mention in a word the lawsuit that had been brought against his previous decision in the Federal Court of Petitions.
States should start complyinging with the 2015 ground surface ozone standard from 1 October.
Pruitt announced in June that he would postpone the implementation of the decisions by a year, so that his agency will have more time to familiarize itself with the plan because the agency “does not want to interfere with local decision-making or hamper economic growth.”
Pruitt was a public prosecutor in the state of Oklahoma before he was appointed head of EPA. Pruitt is known as an opponent of stricter environmental regulations.
Since coming to Washington, Pruitt has repeatedly attempted to prevent or delay the implementation of decisions opposed by the chemical industry and fossil fuel industries.
Wednesday's sudden mindal change is the latest setback for Pruitt's plan to relax the regulations.
Last month, a federal appeals tribunal in Washington issued a ruling that the EPA chief exceeded his mandate in an attempt to delay the Obama administration's decision on oil and gas companies. The decision obliged companies to monitor and reduce methane leaks.
In Wednesday evening's statement, Pruitt argued that his translation of ozone regulations merely strengthens EPA's cooperation with the Länders in a complex process aimed at meeting the new requirements in accordance with the timetable.
“During previous administrations, the EPA often failed to meet deadlines and then waited for activist groups and others to challenge it to the right to reach an agreement on timetables,” Pruitt said. In his previous position, he sued the Environment Agency more than a dozen times.
“We don’t believe in regulation through litigation and we take deadlines seriously.
We are also serious about the regulation concerning our activities and the powers conferred on us.”
However, the EPA opinion stated that Pruitt will continue to be able to exercise its ‘powers to delay the execution of decisions and other legal powers’ to ensure that the provisions ‘are justified and based on the best information available’.
Republicans are pushing Congress for a wider change in ozone regulations.
The aim of the bill adopted last month is to delay the implementation of 2015 decisions by at least eight years.
This proposal has not yet been voted on in the Senate.
Eric Schneiderman, the New York State Attorney in New York, which led the state consortium that sued the EPA this week, said the group intends to continue to exert pressure.
“The EPA’s change of mind as a result of our actions is a significant victory for the 6.7 million New Yorkers and more than 115 million Americans exposed to the direct effects of smog in their communities,” Schneiderman said.
In addition to New York, the lawsuit included California, Connecticut, Delaware, Illinois, Iowa, Maine, Massachusetts, Minnesota, New Mexico, Oregon, Pennsylvania, Rhode Island, Vermont, Washington and District of Columbia.
Ozone on the ground is created when, for example, emissions from cars, power plants, oil refineries and chemical plants react to sunlight in the atmosphere.
The resulting smog can cause serious breathing difficulties for sensitized groups of people. It is also part of the reason for thousands of premature deaths each year.
Public health promoters and environmentalists were delighted with Pruitt's sudden change of direction.
“It’s worrying how much pressure was required to use common sense from the guy responsible for our breathing air,” says Lori Ann of the Center for Biological Diversity.
“We still have to fight the Trump administration, for its ideological goal is to act on the terms of polluters and other self-interested groups.”
SUV coming from supercar manufacturer - "happens in Ferrari's style"
It also intends to invest in hybrid sports cars while trying to maintain its exclusive position.
“This is likely to happen, but it’s going to happen in Ferrari’s style,” CEO Sergio Marchionne said at a news conference.
The details of the new strategy will be unveiled next year when an Italian supercars manufacturer unveils its new five-year plan.
All cases of measles in Eastern Savo the same type of virus
Rantasalmi's measles infection is the same type of virus as the previous cases in Eastern Savo, THL announces.
On Thursday, the Virology Laboratory of the National Institute for Health and Welfare has ensured that Itä-Savo's fifth measles infection is the same strain with previous infections.
THL has ensured that the measles infection confirmed on Friday 28 July by a person from Rantasalmi is of the same type of virus as the previous cases in Eastern Savo.
Based on this information, all the infections detected in Itä-Savo are related to the first measles detected in the area in June.
The news is good, because it means that we haven't come up with any other types of measles viruses.
However, it is possible that new infections may still come up after the fifth patient until mid-August, and therefore the epidemic is not yet oven," says Mirkaleena Erkkilä, Sosteri's physician responsible for infectious diseases in basic health care.
There have been no new measles infections or their suspicions since the fifth confirmed incident.
However, it is still important that everyone takes care of the up-to-dateness of their own vaccination protection, Erkkilä reminds.
More on the topic: Three new measles infections confirmed in Pirkanmaa - "Additional infections or the epidemic very unlikely"
Finland prepares for a permissible scream
The soup was not even considered for legalization.
In the background, the criterion is that the process, along with liquor, produces methanol, which is toxic.
It'll be legal to make a squeal at home next year, but you won't even consider legalizing moonshine soup.
The scream is made by fermentation, the moonshine is distilled again.
Underlying the regulation of distillation, there is a reason that the process, along with liquor, produces methanol, which is toxic.
There has been no political will to enactuate moonship soup as everyman's right," says Ismo Tuominen, Senior Ministerial Adviser at the Ministry of Social Affairs and Health.
When the Ministry was preparing the reform of the Alcohol Act, the distillation of the moonshine was not considered for legalisation.
This was not brought to the political table either.
There was no discussion on the liberalisation of distillation, says MP Outi Mäkelä (National Coalition Party).
He is in the negotiating team of the parliamentary groups of the governing parties, who commented on the reform of the Alcohol Act.
In the future, you can't even grant permission to distillate the moth at home, even on application.
Commercial manufacturing will also remain subject to a licence.
Tuominen says that there will be a simple rule for the domestic production of alcoholic beverages: alcohol can be brewed at home through fermentation.
This means that in addition to beer, cider and wine, you can legally scream at home.
The government wanted to simplify the regulations.
The new law removes the requirements for flavourings and flavourings and enables the production of a scream, Mäkelä specifies.
The preparation still needs to be carried out in such a way that the raw materials or the flavouring and flavouring materials created during their fermentation are clearly noticeable in the drink.
According to the new law, drinks can be prepared as much as you want, and self-made alcohol can be given as a gift.
It should still not be put up for sale.
The act of Scream is no longer a public health problem in Finland, Tuominen believes.
A much bigger change took place in 2004, when passenger imports from Estonia and other EU countries were released.
75 per cent of the alcohol consumed in Finland is imported from Estonia.
The Alcohol Act is now under review by the Ministry of Justice.
The Alcohol Act will be processed by Parliament as soon as the autumn season begins.
The plenary session is 5.9.
The Act will enter into force at the beginning of 2018.
The law is well prepared.
It's as if Parliament has already taken a stand, Mäkelä rejoices.
Oscar Pistorius was rushed to a hospital accompanied by armed guards
Oscar Pistorius was rushed to a South African hospital on suspicion of a heart attack.
Pistorius has already been discharged from the hospital.
Oscar Pistorius is serving a prison sentence in Atteridgeville.
According to the British Mirror, Pistorius was taken from the prison to a hospital this morning, accompanied by armed guards.
Pistorius suffered chest pain, but he was discharged from the hospital this afternoon.
A prison spokesman confirmed that Pistorius was taken to the hospital for examination.
A spokesman for Pistorius's family said that the former top athlete is fine.
Last summer, the Supreme Court sentenced a six-time Paralympic gold medallist to six years in prison for the murder of his girlfriend, Reeva Steenkamp.
Pistorius shot Steenkamp in February 2013.
Tranned phone conversations reveal that Trump pressured Mexico to build a border wall
Transcriptions of telephone conversations between U.S. President Donald Trump and the leaders of Mexico and Australia have been leaked to the Washington Post.
In a telephone conversation on 27.1.2017, Trump urged Mexican President Enrique Peña Nieto to end his public speech that he was not going to pay the proposed border wall.
“You can’t say so to the press,” Trump said.
Trump launched his presidential campaign by promising to build a wall on the U.S. southern border at the expense of Mexico.
The Washington Post also published a transcript of Trump's first call to Australian Prime Minister Malcolm Turnbull.
Trump told Turnbull that the agreement that the U.S. would accept refugees on its way to Australia must be “ridiculous,” “miserable” and ‘stupid.’ According to Trump, a deal negotiated under Obama would make him look “idiotic” because he had proposed a ban on resettling refugees into the United States.
Trump ended the call by sayinging to Turnbull that “this was the most unpleasant phone call all day.”
Georgia massacre: Activists rescheduled racist lynching for justice
A Ford Mercury-branded car overtakes a crowd in Monroe’s small town in the Georgian countryside and continues its journey towards the small Moore’s Ford Bridge.
There are four black passengers and a white driver in the car. Four armed men block the road before the car gets to the bridge.
The leader of the group, a middle-aged man dressed in a striped suit, takes a deep breath from his cigars and watches through the windshield.
Passengers are scared.
‘Roger, ‘‘‘‘‘‘‘′′′
‘Out of the car!’
The screams break the silence when a young black man is pulled out of the front seat.
Fortunately, this shocking event is only a re-staging, which was presented on 22.7.2017.
However, the sighes of the shock are genuine when the noose is threaded into “Roger’s” neck.
Since 2005, human rights organizations have returned to Moore’s Ford’s Bridge to re-stage the events of 1946. At that time, the Ku Klux Klan lynched two black couples, Roger and Dorothy Malcom and George and Mae Murray Dorsey.
No one has been prosecuted for the murders.
‘It’s perplexing that no one has been arrested during these years – even though murderers live among us and many need to know who they are,’ says Tyrone Brooks, who will help arrange a train.
“It’s a stain in history, but above all, it’s a burden that weighs on our soul.”
Brooks is a 71-year-old civic rights activist and former congresswoman in Georgia.
For him, reloading is a startling invitation to action. It also reminds Monroe's residents that injustice has never been corrected.
“We want the culprits to go to court, we want things to finally come to an end, and we want to recover from grief and make peace, but above all, we want justice,” Brooks said.
“We can’t recover and reconcile until we get justice.”
Coroner covers the body of the deceased victim in the Georgia massacre 27.7.1946.
According to NBC news, more than 50 men were suspected of lynching about Monroe and the surrounding counties.
However, no one was charged with murder.
Although the culprits have been available for decades, according to activists, the motives for lynching have always been known to locals: politics and sex.
In 1946, Georgian state blacks were allowed to vote for the first time in the presidential election. The Supreme Court handed its offense in April, and elections were held in the summer.
According to the FBI, there was an incident at the time of the election in which Roger Malcom, a black lease farmer, stabbed white landowner Barnett Hester in a fighting apparently involving Malcom's wife Dorothy.
According to Brooks, there were rumours that Hester had an affair with Dorothy and that Dorothy was expecting his child.
According to FBI information, influential white landowner Loy Harrison paid Malcom's 600-dollar bailies on 25.7.1946, against which he was released from prison.
On the way home, Malcom and Dorothy were accompanied by their cousins Mae Murray and George Dorsey.
The crowd stopped their cars ahead of Moore’s Ford Bridge.
“It turns out Harrison commissioned and orchestrated the murders,” the FBI report said.
“It’s also obvious that there was a conspiracy between the state and the local authorities. Authorities were involved in the murders and destroyed the evidence and did not reveal the identity of the witnesses.”
Brooks expresses it more succinctively.
“It was a votical murder,” he said.
“They were murdered because they wanted to send black people a message that the same thing would happen to them if they were going to vote.”
Cassandra Greene has been directing the re-stamping of the Georgia massacre for almost a decade.
The performance is extremely realistic and captivating. It is a depiction of the shocking racism that many would like to believe have already disappeared from today's United States.
It's hard to watch the performance, but it's even more difficult not to look at what Greene says is supposed to do.
“We don’t want to talk about lynching, because we’d have to face our own dark sides,” the director says.
“But I can’t create a relationship with you if you don’t hear me or you don’t want to hear what I’ve been through.”
Rehearsals for this year's performance began in June. On the first day, about 30 actors – a dozen black and twenty whites – gathered to listen to Greene's routine summary of the performance.
Wade Marbaugh is Greene's co-director.
He has been portraying the local Ku Klux Klan leader for years, but the role is always equally difficult and disgusting.
‘I’ll feel dirty because I’m not myself,’ Marbaugh says.
‘However, it is important that this side of history should also be highlighted so that it will never happen again.’
Darrius Bradshaw and Nick Rascona, aka Roger Malcom and Barnett Hester, are practicing on the other side of the room.
The performance begins with a scene where Malcom is screaming and pushing Hester. He's furious that Hester's had an affair with Dorothy.
‘Didn’t you hear? I said get out of my land, ‘‘‘‘n
Rascona, or Hester, responds.
And then there's the punch.
And then everyone (at least all black actors) burst into laughter.
Greene's laughing the loudest.
‘What?’ Rascona asks.
She's confused.
‘Did I accidentally hit too hard?’ he asks.
‘Not,’ Greene said.
‘I just don’t think white people used the word ‘‘‘‘‘the whites used the word ��
‘That’s right,’ said another black actor.
“It’s slang. You have to use a longer version.”
The subject of the performance is unpleasant, but the room is occupied by a friend spirit.
Everyone wants to succeed, and not just because of the performance, and they want to honor the memory of those killed.
The scene starts from the beginning.
Rascona's words are slashing now.
No one's laughing.
The re-staging stops quickly and violently.
Shouts, gunshots – and silence.
Dressed in a black funeral costume, a woman stands next to the bodies and sings Billie Holiday's Strange Fruit.
His voice is shattered for every other word.
A few dozen meters away, fifty spectators wipe sweat and tears from their eyes.
Almost exactly half of the audience is black and half white.
There's a couple who've come to see a show from Ithaca in the state of New York, but most are local.
They've been guided by Brooks to the tombs of the victims in Georgia's oppressive humidity.
The murders have come true from the story. The atmosphere at the scene is heavy.
After the singing performance, Greene invites the crowd to inspect the bodies.
Children squat to look at artificial blood and immobile bodies and whisper to each other.
‘They’ren’t really dead,’ the little boy mutters as if to remind himself.
In a few minutes, Greene will thank the audience.
Actors pretending to lynches strip their robes and wipe their tears.
Bradshaw hugs Marbaugh, a man who played a local Ku Klux Klan leader.
Nick Rascona hugs two actors and then bursts into tears after a tough performance.
‘Don’t worry,’ said the woman.
“We know you’re not him.
“You did this for a good reason.”
Later, the audience and the actors gather for a joint dinner.
A white couple who have arrived from Altanta are busy discussing the performance with the black couple they've just met.
Denise Duplinski finds it difficult to find the words when she describes the feelings evoked by the performance.
“It’s hard to listen to terrible words and watch the terrible actions that people say and do, who look like you – precisely because they look the same as you,” he says.
‘The performance made me notice that lynchings are still taking place,’ he added. ‘They’re just called arrests.’
On the other side of the room, Brooks recalls the development of civil rights for listeners sitting at the same table.
He has already started planning next year's presentation.
‘Staging again is a powerful experience because it depicts the horrors we’re experiencing, but it’s also an instructive experience,” Brooks said.
“Its message is that this can happen again if we don’t learn from it.
“Those who don’t know their history are doomed to repeat it.”
It's a lot of "extra parts" to remove "extra parts" that it really saves
Is it permissible to remove cauliflower leaves already in the store before weighinging?
What about taking a big hat out of the pineapple - it weighs so much?
The responses strongly divide opinions: for one, it is an indication of extreme sluggishness, while others do not want to pay for those parts of vegetables that are not used, and which, however, go into the dumpster at home.
The phenomenon will be recognized in the shops.
For example, there are cardboard boxes in K-Citymarket in Ruoholahti, Helsinki, because many tears away, for example, cauliflower leaf parts.
From the box, the newspapers are taken to the store's biowaste.
According to a news release published by Taloussanomat, shopkeepers are amazed at sniffing at the vegetable counter.
But how much the removal of the extra parts of vegetables before weighinging actually saves money?
How much of a slug is it?
We Women tested how the price changes when pineapple, top artichoke, mushrooms, broccoli and cauliflower take the leaves or stems away.
Cauliflower: The price of a cauliflower costing 2,10 euros was 1,80 euros, when you peel all the leaves out of it.
Pineapple: The price of an entire pineapple costing 2.30 euros dropped to 1.95 euros when its stock was twisted away.
Top artichoke: 2.40 euros came out of a price of 2.60 euros, when the short-term stock was cut off.
Mushrooms: Two mushrooms cost a total of 75 centimetres.
Without strains, the price was 60 cents.
Broccoli: A medium-sized broccoli cost 1 euro and without a strain of 80 centimetres.
Result: If the leaves and stumps are plucked away, at best a few dozen centimetres are saved for one vegetable.
Considered in percentage terms, the price of vegetables fell by as much as 20 per cent.
A total of 1,20 euros would have been saved from these purchases!
Neymar was bought out of Barcelona - the brass star signed a five-year contract with PSG
Neymar has signed a five-year-long contract with the French football club PSG, the AFP news agency reported.
Earlier on Thursday, it was reported that Neymar's representatives had paid FC Barcelona 222 million euros in the Spanish league's buy-out price.
The 222-million-euro transfer is the largest in football history.
Earlier, the record was set by Manchester United, which paid 105 million euros last summer for the Frenchman Paul Pogba.
The Spanish league blocked the move earlier on Thursday when it rejected Neymar's first payment attempt.
According to the league's rules, the buying club cannot pay the purchase price directly to the seller. Instead, the player has to buy out his/her own contract, which is done by wageringing money through the league.
In practice, PSG has paid the sum to Neymar and his representatives, who delivered the money through the league to Barcelona.
Neymar is expected to arrive in Paris on Friday.
He may be presented to a new home crowd at PSG's season-opening against Amiens on Saturday.
UEFA examines details
Barcelona also said in a statement that it would provide information on the payment to the European Football Association (UEFA) in order to allow the union to determine any disciplinary measures resulting from the incident.
Javier Tebas, the chairman of the Spanish league, has suspected PSG's use of money to violate UEFA's financial fair play rules.
UEFA can punish a club that will make a loss of more than 30 million euros over a three-year period.
According to UEFA, Neymar's transfer does not automatically violate its economic clauses.
Neymar's transfer to PSG will affect the club's finances for several years, but the impact cannot be assessed in advance.
PSG, for example, may sell multiple players with significant amounts of money, Uefa announced.
PSG is owned by the state-owned Qatari company Qatar Sports Investment.
The club's president is Nasser al-Khelaifi, a former tennis professional.
