Pearson cuts 3 000 jobs and revitalizes the business by cutting dividends
The British educational group Pearson announced on Friday that it would cut 3 000 jobs and cut dividends by 72 per cent when starting a new restructuring programme in response to pressures on its business.
According to President and CEO John Fallon, job reductions will mainly be made at the end of 2018 and at the beginning of 2019. The company already reduced 4 000 jobs in connection with the previous structural arrangements.
Pearson announced that it would again cut costs by 300 million pounds in May as part of its third austerity programme, which aims to revitalize the business that has deteriorated as a result of rapid digitalisation.
Last month, Pearson decided to sell its share of Penguin Random House to its partner Bertelsmann. The company announced that it would keep its outlook for the current year unchanged. Its net sales grew by one per cent in the first half of the year and amounted to 2.05 billion pounds.
Convoy in Kabul: NATO Soldier Killed in Suicidal Attack
A suicidal bomber attacked a military bug in Kabul, the Afghan capital, and authorities said a NATO soldier was killed and several others were wounded.
According to NATO's statement, five soldiers and an interpreter were injured in the attack.
According to unconfirmed local reports, three civilians were also killed in the attack in the Qarabagh region of Kabul.
The explosion is the latest violent attack in Afghanistan this month. On Tuesday, 30 people were killed in a mosque explosion in Herat.
On Monday, two people were killed by a suicide bomber at the Iraqi embassy in Kabul.
According to NATO's statement, Thursday night's explosion occurred when a NATO-Afghan-Afghan-Afghan-Afghan army joint patrol was attacked by a "self-made explosive device carried by a person."
The statement said that the condition of all the injured was stable and they were treated at a U.S. military hospital at Bagram Airport.
According to correspondents, the strengthening of the Taliban and the presence of the ISIS terrorist organization in Qarabagh are significant concerns for NATO forces near Bagram.
According to the United Nations, at least 1,662 civilians were killed in Afghanistan in the first half of 2017, 20 per cent in the capital.
At the end of July, a car bomb killed at least 30 people in a suicide bombing in Kabul, a region based mainly in Shiite Muslims.
The Taliban said they were behind the attack.
On the last day of May, more than 150 people were killed in the centre of Kabul in a bombing, the country's deadliest militant staff attack after U.S.-led U.S.-led forces overthrew the Taliban in 2001.
Violence highlights the unstable situation in Afghans when President Donald Trump is considering whether the U.S. is sending more troops to help the local army and police.
According to correspondents, suicide bombings in Kabul in recent weeks have also significantly weakened people's confidence in the government, its policies and, in particular, President Ashraf Ghani.
EPA swirls its decision to postpone the implementation of smog regulations
The U.S. Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) swirled its decision to delay the execution of decisions to reduce smoggle during Obama's reign a day after 15 states sued the agency.
EPA announced its decision to implement so-called ‘ozone definitions’ in 2015 late on Wednesday. According to the agency, the decision demonstrates its commitment to cooperation with the Länders.
“We believe in the dialogue and we want to meet the needs of the states,” Scott Pruitt said in a statement.
