Average salary 5 300 euros, education what happens - the situation of the investment counselling was "wild and free"
There has been a lot of discussion about the quality of investment advice recently.
Most recently, Helsingin Sanomat reported about Ritva, 68, to whom an investment adviser from a banker's store spoke a complete bunny when selling investment products.
The rules for investment advisers and various sales of investment products will become stricter at the beginning of next year.
Tighteners are based on the new Financial Markets Directive, which will be applied from the beginning of next year.
At present, the assessment of the competency of investment advisers and employees selling investment products is mainly based on self-regulation.
There are no competence requirements defined by law.
The Financial Sector (FA) itself recommends that those working in the investment services complete their own APV1 or APV2 degree.
The financial sector has authorised the organisation of qualifications for a company called APV Investment Examinations Oy, whose degree working group includes experts from the Financial Supervisory Authority (FSA), universities and actors in the field.
The scope of the examinations is 12-15 credits, and their exams are prepared by Aalto EE, Aalto University Executive Education Oy, which provides management development services.
Its website states that, in general, both exams are "quite extensive in terms of requirements".
The learning material of the degrees consists of 10-15 books and numerous other guides and works.
Markku Savikko, CEO of APV Investment Degrees Ltd, says that the pass rate of the exams is roughly about 70.
Of the staff of the largest banks, almost all those providing investment services have completed either the APV1 or APV2 degree, because they are mandatory according to the company's own rules.
Then there are the smaller banker's shops without mentioning the names, whose staff seems to have avoided the completion of the examinations," Savikko says.
According to him, there are "a few bankers like this."
According to Savikko, since 2000, the APV1 degree has been completed by 13 000-14 000 people, and approximately 3 000 people have completed the more demanding APV2 degrees recommended for investment advisors.
Some bankers consider graduation to be an advantage.
Several other industries have significantly stricter competence requirements.
Savikko says that the Financial Sectoral Sector recommendation requires the completion of APV examinations, because it is then the competence related to the investment sector will be ensured.
According to Statistics Finland, financial and investment advisers earn, on average, several thousand euros per month, including commissions, fringe benefits and performance-based bonuses.
The average daily working hours of monthly employees are about 5 300 euros.
On average, men who work in positions earn more than 5 900 euros per month, while women earn more than 4 500 euros.
On average, the tenth of those who earn the most will earn about 8 100
The tenth of men who earn the most will earn more than 9 000 euros a month or more, one-tenth of the women who earn the most will earn 6,400 euros or more.
Investment advice in accordance with the Investment Services Act can only be provided by a licensed company.
Until now, self-regulation has been relied on at the employee and task level.
According to FIN-FSA, the audit it carried out involved individual cases in which the investment advisor's professional skills and qualifications were not sufficient on the basis of the advisor's activities.
In principle, you can also work as a seller of investment products, even if your professional skills and qualifications are at an easy level.
As a rule, the situation has been savage and free in this regard.
More other industries have significantly stricter competence requirements," Savikko says.
According to Savikko, from the beginning of next year, there will be tightening of this with the introduction of the new MiFID II (MiFID II).
It requires that each company is responsible for ensuring that the personnel providing the investment services are sufficiently competent.
The FIN-FSA, on the other hand, may be empowered to determine what to do.
MARKO Hovi, FIN-FSA's market supervisor, says that one of the clear changes to the current one is a regulation on the requirements concerning the competence and experience of investment advisers and investment product vendors.
According to Hovi, the new requirements apply both to the own staff of the investment firms and to tied agents who sell products on behalf of a company.
According to a government proposal submitted by the Ministry of Finance, FIN-FSA is likely to be empowered to prescribe expertise and experience.
From exactly what they are, we are expected to decide during the coming autumn.
In the future, the company must continue to take care of the necessary training and, if necessary, follow-up and continuing training.
All of this must be documented, and the company has a duty of proof that this has also been done.
The court specifies that, in practice, the company must, for example, map the job descriptions of the personnel at the individual level.
Depending on whether it is investment advice or the sale of products, there will certainly be different levels of requirements.
According to Hovi, it is also a new requirement to work with someone more experienced within the deadline if the competence or experience is not at a sufficient level.
In other words, if the company hires a new employee who does not quite meet the specified criteria, the employee must work with a person who meets the criteria for a certain period of time.
According to Hovi, with the change, the rules will tighten and become concrete.
Legislation on the sales of investment products will be tightened at the beginning of 2018.
