The new results of Swedish asylum seekers' age tests have been announced
The latest results of the medical age tests for asylum seekers in Sweden have been published. The results of tests carried out using the new assessment method reveal that more than 80 per cent of those tested were determined to be over 18 years of age. However, not everyone considers the tests to be reliable.
Sweden's National Bureau of Forensic Medicine started testing earlier this year.
Tests can be used to assess the age of the asylum seeker more accurately than before. The new test was introduced after the Swedish Immigration Service was criticised for having failed in determining the actual age of the applicants who had claimed to be minors.
The Finnish Immigration Service has commissioned 6 880 tests so far, and the Forensic Agency has now published the results of 2,481. The tests were carried out between mid-March and the end of July in 2017.
Of those tested, 80 %, i.e. in 2002, the asylum seeker was determined to be over 18 years of age. In addition, in 25 cases, the forensic Agency determined that the test subject was ‘possibly 18 years of age or older’.
In 432 cases, it was concluded that the asylum seeker is “possibly under the age of 18”.
Tests were carried out only in cases where there was reason to suspect the age indicated by the applicant.
Johan Göransson from the Department of Forensic Medicine says that the results are consistent with the results of previous months.
‘The figures are quite consistent. There are no major differences in the statistics of the previous months,’ he told The Local.
Although a large number of applicants were determined to be over the age of 18, this does not necessarily mean that applicants have lied about their ages.
In May, the Agency published the results of the 581 tests and stated that in 442 cases there were ‘indications that the subject is 18 years of age or older’.
When Svenska Dagbladet examined the same results, it turned out that in 243 cases the applicant had openly stated that they were turning 18 this year.
In the medical age assessment method used in the tests, X-rays of the wisdom teeth and MRI images of the knee joints are taken. Then the dentists and radiologists analyze the images. The method has also been criticized.
One of the skeptics is Claude Marcus, an endocrinologist at the Karolinska Institutet, whose work includes an assessment of people's biological maturity. The assessment can be used to find out whether a person's puberty has come earlier or later than usual.
‘I’ve had reason to think about it, and I don’t think it’s the right method.
Determining biological age in this way is very uncertain.
“If we can’t determine the age, we shouldn’t pretend we’re able to do it,” he told Metro.
However, Göransson of the Department of Forensic Medicine defended the accuracy of the tests: “In general, it can be said that the risk of assigning a child to an adult is small.
The risk is greatest in a situation where the test subject will soon turn 18 years old, and then the risk will be about 10 per cent.”
