Lightning sown damage - Thursday is becoming even the hardest thrift day of the summer
At the end of the week, there will also be more thunders in Southern Finland.
During the past week, three has been thrieving especially in the central and northern parts of the country and in Southwest Finland.
Between Wednesday and Thursday, lightning strikes started several fires.
According to the meteorologist, Thursday may be the toughest thunderstorm day of the summer so far.
Thunderstorm showers have finally found Finland as well.
During the past week, lightnings have occurred especially in the central and northern parts of the country and in Southwest Finland.
In Pöytyä, lightning hit the electricity center of a terraced house, causing a small fire.
The rescue department received a report of the fire about a quarter to midnight on Wednesday.
The electricity centre was located in the sauna at the end of the terraced house.
The rescue department dismantled the roof and wall to find the fires and quickly extinguished the fire.
A total of eleven apartments were left without electricity, but personal injuries were avoided.
In Loimaa, lightning ignited the outbuilding on Harjulantie before midnight.
When the rescue department arrived, the building was already in full fire phase, and the rescue department had no choice but to prevent the fire from spreading.
An outbuilding of about fifty square metres burned to the ground.
In the direction of Kuopio in Rääkkylä, lightning hit a detached house.
The occupants of the house were outside the building and witnessed a flammable lightning strike.
The rescue department went to the scene to check the incident, but this time the lightning didn't cause a fire - only a few fuses frowned.
In traffic, thunderstorm caused problems on the railway line between Seinäjoki and Vaasa, when a lightning strike damaged the traffic control system.
Information about the fault was received between eight and nine on Thursday morning, after which the trains in that distance were replaced by buses.
The problem was not fixed until shortly before five in the afternoon.
Since then, train traffic has continued normally also on the thunder-stroken section.
Lightnings have wreaked havoc in different parts of Finland.
Lightning has been relatively low this summer.
This summer's thundery stories have been absolutely terrible!" says Jari Tuovinen, meteorologist on duty at the Finnish Meteorological Institute.
Wednesday's and Thursday's thunderstorms have been predominantly moderate, but thanks to the low lightning balance of the summer, it also feels strong for many.
This might seem like a really hard thunderstorm, but that's what it's been compared to this summer's thunderfist, Tuovinen says.
On Wednesday, ground lightning flapped 1,700 times, and Thursday has had the potential for the toughest thunderous day of the summer: by about 16 o'clock, there were 1,790 earth lightnings in the placard.
If lightning still continues to the early evening, then more than two thousand lightnings can be reached, Tuovinen predicts.
The previous record-weak thundery year was experienced in 2015, when the year's lightning count was about 30 000 pieces.
By Wednesday, this year's lightning count had risen to 18,386 lightnings.
So there's still something to be arrested so that this year doesn't break the "record" two years ago.
However, thunderstorms can be expected to continue.
According to Tuovinen, the air mass over Finland will also remain warm and humid during the rest of the week, which is a favorable environment for thundrummers.
The main focus of thundress is likely to be shifted from the northern parts of the country to the south on Saturday, where it is possible to receive about 2 000 lightning days both on Saturday and Sunday.
There will be a more unified rain front in the central parts of the country.
According to Tuovinen, there is only sparsely thundering.
Coe praises Russia’s ‘cohabiting’ apology for doping scandal
Sebastian Coe, the world's athletics boss, praised the apologism he described as open-minded, which Russia made due to the doping scandal on Thursday, but Coe added that it was not yet time to lift Russia's ban.
On the eve of the World Championships, Dmitry Shlyahthin, Russia's Athletics Director, told the IAAF congress on Thursday that Russia's ban was appropriate and that he intends to fight doping decisively.
He said he felt sorry “on behalf of all the athletes who were deprived of a gold or silver medal in competitions.”
Despite his comments, the IAAF congress voted in favour of extending the ban. Russia was ruled out of athletics competitions in November 2015 after the World Anti-Doping Agency (WADA report) presented evidence of state-subsidized doping in Russia.
‘In my opinion, his response today was very direct. It was a very open-minded proposal,’ Coe, the IAAF president, told reporters.
“The entire Council and Congress were pleased that the Russian Athletics Federation admitted that the country had gone through difficult times and announced that it would do everything possible to reorganize its activities.”
Coe also said he was ‘satisfied’ with Russia’s acceptance of the terms of the ban.
“In my opinion, we had a very constructive day and progress has been made, but Congress supports the working group’s recommendation that now is not the right time to put an end to Russia’s ban.”
“In the coming months, we will have to do everything we can to normalize the situation.
The main principle has always been that we want to keep clean athletes separate from the depraved system,” he added.
Nineteen Russian athletes will compete on the status of a neutral athlete at the Athletics World Championships starting in London on Friday. They have met the IAAF's strict criteria.
Rune Andersen, the leader of the IAAF's working group, said on Monday that Russia still needs to meet a number of requirements before its status can be restored.
‘Testing is still inadequate, and coaches who have been banned continued to operate freely,” he told reporters on Friday to the IAAF Council following a proposal in London.
The production company was disappointed with the poor success of the Tom of Finland film
Tom of Finland did not turn out to be such success as the authors hoped, says Yle.
To be honest, I'm disappointed, admits Annika Sucksdordf, the CEO of Helsinki Film, in an interview with YLE.
I was afraid that the Finnish film audience might be conservative, and it looks like it is.
The film premiered in February 2017.
By the end of June, it had reached more than 100 000 viewers, compared to the film's budget of about five million euros.
It didn't turn out to be a lottery win, but now it's important for investors to get theirs back.
If the film had been more successful at home, they would've already received their money.
Now we have to wait for small streams to spread abroad, says Sucksdorff.
Tom of Finland is a film directed by Dome Karukoski.
How do football's financial rules affect Neymar's transfer to Paris
The regulations restricting the spending of European football clubs do not prevent Neymar's transfer from Barcelona, Spain, to Paris Saint-Germain in France, the most expensive player transfer ever.
Its possible consequences for Paris Saint-Germain (PSG) will be decided by UEFA regulating European football, which may prevent teams from participating in the prestigious Champions League.
In 2011, UEFA introduced the economic rules for fair play, according to which PSG must be able to prove that Neymar's transfer has been financed without major losses.
Barcelona were paid 222 million euros for the termination of the Brazilian contract, and Neymar's annual salary for the new club will amount to tens of millions of dollars.
Neymar's transfer to France involves a wide range of economic considerations.
PSG won't have any trouble paying Neymar's transfer amount and linking him to his star's guard, as the club is funded by Qatar's rich-enriched royal family.
Clubs are allowed to spend large sums of money to acquire players, but they need to be balanced with actual sources of income so that football-related business is not loss-making.
Fairplay's financial rules were drafted on the initiative of Michel Platini, a Frenchman elected president of UEFA in 2007.
As a former footballer, Platini was of the opinion that the pursuit of success by indebtedness was cheating and led to serious problems if banks or owners withdrew their support.
In 2011, the focus of the financial rules of fair play shifted from club debts to monitoring their losses.
The change meanted that Roman Abramovich's first business model used by Chelsea in 2003 became significantly more difficult. Abramovich bought a promising team, spent huge sums on player acquisitions and created a global brand.
The current three-year valuation period of the fair game's financial rules lasts until 2018, during which clubs are allowed to make a loss of up to 30 million euros.
Although Platini was forced to step down the UEFA president's duties scandalously in 2015, his successor, Aleksander Ceferin, also wanted to curb the reckless spending of clubs.
‘Fair game rules are still in use, and we will continue to apply them in the future,” Andrea Traverso, who is in charge of the fair game financial rules in UEFA, told the Associated Press news agency last week.
Although PSG passed Neymar out of its contract with a lump sum, it may appointate the amount in the accounts for the entire duration of his new contract.
Neymar has entered into a five-year contract, so the transfer amount can be recorded as an annual expense of 44.4 million euros for the next five years.
Based on the economic rules of the fair game, the first sanctions were handed out in 2014. The biggest sanctions were imposed on PSG and Manchester City, funded from Abu Dhabi.
PSG received a fine of 60 million euros, which was later reduced to 20 million euros. In addition, the club was ordered to limit its Champions League group to 21 players for the 2014–2015 season instead of the usual 25 players.
UEFA monitors clubs that may try to circumvent its financial regulations by increasing their income through companies associated with club owners.
For example, if a Qatari company supports PSG but the value of the aid is considered to be above the usual level on the market, UEFA does not count the aid as actual income.
This is what happened when UEFA decided in 2014 that support for PSG by the Qatari tourism authority was excessive.
It has also emerged that part of Neymar's merits for PSG could be financed by payments available from Qatar's 2022 FIFA World Cup Organizing Committee.
However, PSG and the organizing committee have denied that Neymar would be made an ambassador for the World Championships, and it would be very easy for UEFA to reveal such an arrangement.
Due to PSG's previous sanctions, UEFA is increasingly monitoring attempts to circumvent the rules.
On Thursday, sources said UEFA had not negotiated with PSG on Neymar's transfer.
Sources gave an interview to the Associated Press on the condition that their identities weren't disclosed because they don't have the authority to discuss PSG's situation.
According to the most recently published financial statements, PSG's net sales for the period 2015–2016 were MEUR 542.4 and the operating profit exceeds 10 million euros.
Last season's financial statements have not yet been published.
PSG expects Neymar's arrival to attract new sponsors and increase the sales of club products and jerseys.
The club estimates that its turnover will increase by at least 30 per cent with Neymar's agreement.
Clearly annoyed by the departure of one of the world's best players, the Spanish league tried to complicate Neymar's move, which allegedly violated the financial rules of fair play.
“This is a clear economic doping case for both the club and the state,” Javier Tebas, chairman of the Spanish League, told the Spanish newspaper AS.
Arsenal manager Arsene Wenger also has reservations about the state's financing of the football club's activities. Arsenal is owned by an American sports club.
“The incident is the result of ownership, which has completely changed the operating environment of football over the past 15 years,” Wenger said on Thursday.
“Anything is possible after the state owns a football team.
It becomes very difficult to comply with the fair game’s financial rules. There are so many kinds of interests related to the fact that the famous player represents the state.”
