The World Championship medal from London requires a miracle
The Athletics World Championships will start in London on Friday and will last just over a week ending on Sunday, August 13.
Athletics, cross-country skiing and ice hockey are the sports that Finnish bench athletes have the energy to be passionate about year after year, whether they are successful or not.
In the hearts of bench athletes, success is measured in medals.
Of course, there are other metrics.
Less attention will be paid to the beautiful growth stories of young athletes from one value competition to another, where gaining experience will increase the athlete's performance to the level that an adult medal is genuinely possible.
Now there are no such young athletes in Finland's small World Championships team, and there are no veterans either.
The World Championship medal for Finland requires a miracle.
Fortunately, the attractiveness and unexpectedness of the sport means that miracles can also happen.
It draws bench athletes to the TV screen and glues to follow the races, even though London's medals are not solved due to the time difference until late in the evening.
Finland received one bronze medal from the previous World Athletics Championships in 2015.
It was brought by javelin thrower Tero Pitkämäki.
Daring people are expecting a medal from Pitkämäki even now, even though the results of him and other throwers in recent times, as well as Pitkämäki's qualification in last summer's competition.
No matter what happens in these competitions, Pitkämäki will go down in history as a great Finnish sports hero.
Admission to other Finnish athletes in the final or setting their own record is a good enough performance.
Hope and faith in Finland's realistic medal hunt in the coming years of athletics will be given a great success in the youth international championships again this summer.
Refining the talents of young people and financially supporting their work is more important than maintaining a small elite group of representative athletes.
Success doesn't come in an instant, but requires years of sweaty work from athletes and patience from both coaching and bench athletes.
Finns are also able to appreciate international sports stars and enjoy their unique performances.
I hope the London Games will go down in history as Usain Bolt and Mo Farah, and not, for example, due to the deterrent of terrorism or cowardly attacks.
