Small circle nostalgia: Wet Hot American Summer and Comrade Detective
At the same time, the story yearns for both the 80's brilliance of the characters and the grunge of the 90's (at that time David Wain and Michael Showalter made a glorious sketch series for MTV) and their own time – before Elizabeth Banks, Amy Poehler, Paul Rudd, and the other stars of the series moved into bigger stories in their careers.
As a cultural product, it is both a parody and a description of the constant reminiscence to which we are stuck in the Facebook era.
As a commercial product, it already seems to have lived up to its time.
First Day of Camp worked surprisingly well, and not only because of its absurd humour (for example, the backstory of H. Jon Benjamin's vocal vegetable jar).
At the heart of the joke is that middle-aged actors play lustful teens approachinging adulthood. Emphasizing it makes the impossibility of reachining the past a part of a joke.
Ten Years Later, in which adults play adults, is less emotional, and its parody is more fragmented: it parodies, for example, 90's stalker movies, such as the horror movie Hand That Cradle.
At one point, one of the characters digs up Apple Powerbook, inline skates and “other stuff from 1991”.
Many of the stories are the same as before.
Good-hearted Coop (Michael Showalter) is now a writer but still hasn't gotten over Katie (Marguerite Moreau).
Victor (Ken Marino) is still a virgin.
And one of the crazy plots features Ronald Reagan (also Michael Showalter) as his accomplice George H.W. Bush (played by Michael Ian Black).
As is always the case in such series, the best are the side paths.
It’s especially fun to see Paul Rudd, who’s under his lower lip with a beard tassel (Matt Dillon’s ‘soulu patch’ from Singles.” Rudd’s Andy, the king of the camp, is starting to be an old guy.
Moments of random madness may take flight of this series as a kind of recursive Seven Up! series until it gets an apparent conclusion: Wet Hot American Summer – Twenty Years Later. In its final scene, a group of friends goes to the premiere of Wet Hot American Summer.
Amazon's Comrade Detective series, on the other hand, appeals to a different nostalgia: the Cold War spirit of The Americans, the Atomic Blonde movie, and even GLOW series wrestling imagery, in which the East meets the West.
Created by Brian Gatewood and Alessandro Tanaka and directed by Rhys Thomas. It has been made a Romanian 1980s police drama, in which a Bucharest stalk couple fight for truth, atheism, and Marxism-Leninism.
Florin Piersic Jr and Corneliu Ulici are starring Gregor and idealistic Iosefi. Their lines have been dubbed in English by Channing Tatum (the main producer of the series) and Joseph Gordon-Levitt.
It's a great idea,
But it’s not much else.
There are some good visual jokes in the series (cops spend time watching chess on TV in the same way Americans watch football).
There will be a number of famous voice actors, such as Jenny Slate, Nick Offerman, Mahershala Ali and Chloë Sevigny.
Comrade Detective, however, gets too caught imitating clumsy socialist drama – including, for example, a killer hiding behind a Reagan mask. That's why the series often begins to feel like clumsy propaganda.
Six long episodes are too long, and the humour of the series is neither fast enough nor plentiful.
In summary, the series might work better as an agitprop satire, for example, when Gregor and Iosef repeatedly visit the U.S. embassy and there are always two fat men devouring burgers in the lobby.
A message to an imagined communist audience is that Western culture can make everything into consumer goods and fast food.
The fictional Romanian creators of the series were not mistaken: we devoured our own recent history and their history.
