The Central Bank of England keeps interest rates at 0.25 per cent
The Bank of England is expected to announce that it will keep interest rates unchanged as economic growth has been impetuous and easing inflation has stopped speculation on interest rate hikes.
The Central Bank of England has warned that economic growth will continue to be sluggish. The Bank postponed interest rate hikes due to even more pressure on the income level of families.
The decision-makers of the Central Bank's Monetary Policy Committee decided by a vote of 6–2 to keep interest rates at 0.25 per cent. This month, the interest rate received even less support, as economic growth has been effortless.
In its quarterly inflation survey, the Bank lowered its growth forecast for 2017 to 1.7 per cent and its 2018 projections to 1.6 per cent. It also warned that there is increasing pressure on household income levels, as inflation is expected to continue to accelerate to close to three per cent in the autumn.
According to the bank, however, interest rate hikes will be needed in the next few years to curb inflation accelerated by Brexit. In addition, the bank announced that loan costs may have to be raised more than expected in the financial markets.
In addition, the members of the committee decided to withdraw part of the massive financial package, which began to be implemented a year ago in the aftermath of the Brexit referendum.
The Bank will discontinue the Term Funding Scheme in February 2018, although it is now expected to offer cheap financing to banks more than 15 million pounds, a total of 115 million.
‘According to a forecast by the Monetary Policy Committee, the slow development of GDP will continue in the next few years, as the decrease in households’ real income reduces consumption.’
With regard to interest rates, the Bank reiterated the need to ‘slightly increase market expectation ‘slightly more widely’ to curb inflation.
Markets are currently predicting the first increase in the third quarter of 2018 and the second in 2020.
However, the bank underlined that the possible increases are ‘gradual’ and ‘limited’.
In May, the bank predicted growth of 1.9 per cent for this year and 1.7 per cent for next year.
It now kept its 2018 growth forecast at 1.8 per cent.
The pound weakened against the dollar and the euro as a result of the news.
Compared to the dollar, the pound weakened by 0.5 per cent and was US$1.31. Compared to the euro, it weakened by 0.4 per cent and was 1.11 euros.
The decision to keep interest rates unchanged was made after the weaker-than-expected growth figures dampened the increased expectations of interest rate hikes. GDP grew by 0.3 per cent in the second quarter and by 0.2 per cent in the first quarter.
The Bank expects growth to remain at 0.3% in the third quarter, but to pick up slightly at the end of the year.
Its most recent inflation report gave little hope to households suffering from accelerating inflation and scarce wage increases. According to the bank, the situation will deteriorate even before it gets better.
The Bank added that monetary policy ‘can’t ‘do not prevent’ a decrease in revenue in the next few years; however, it expects that wage developments will ‘significantly’ recover at the end of the three-year forecast period.
The economy will also be strengthened by a strong increase in the demand for British products as a result of the weak pound. It will override part of the impact of the fall in private consumption.
The decision was made one year after interest rates were lowered to 0.25 per cent, following the shocked EU referendum, which plunged the pound into a decline.
The Bank estimates that the overall effects of the decision are likely to begin to be reflected in prices and the economy only after four years.
Before the central bank's most recent decision, interest rate hikes had begun to be demanded loudest when inflation tightened the pressure on households. Three decision-makers demanded an interest rate hike of 0.5 per cent in July.
Inflation relented to 2.6 per cent in June last month, but according to the bank, it was expected and inflation will tighten again in the coming months.
According to the bank, its two per cent target was ‘full’ due to a wealthy pound.
“Given the recent decline in swap rates, competition in the mortgage market seems to be intensifying as lenders feverishly seek customers and want to end the year strongly,” said Andrew Montlake, president of Coreco Mortgage Brokers.
‘The market looks particularly good for those whose current loan agreement is coming to an end. The renewal agreements are becoming increasingly popular, their interest rates are low, and there are also cost-free options.”
The NHS prescribes 10,000 controversial HIV drugs
As of September, patients can be prescribed a ‘reversal’ anti-HIV medicine. The Supreme Court ruled on a dispute concerning the financing of the medicine in the English public health system (NHS).
The NHS reported that pre-exposure inhibition therapy (PrEP) is prescribed to approximately 10 000 people during the first three-year trial. The pre-intercourse drug has been found to reduce the risk of infection by approximately 86% in high-risk individuals.
The NHS agreed to fund the experiment after the Court of Appeal upheld the Supreme Court's judgment last year. According to it, the NHS will be able to pay for PrEP, even thoughet that local authorities would have to fund it.
Simon Stevens, the head of the English NHS, called Thursday’s decision a “significant new intervention,” and the national AIDS organization described it as ‘a crucial moment’.
Sexual health clinics in London, Brighton, Manchester, Liverpool and Sheffield will be the first to receive registrations for the experiment, which will expand to other clinics in October. The experiment will reach its full extent in England by April 2018.
Pre-exposure prevention (PrEP) therapy (PrEP) is an HIV medicine prior to sexual intercourse that reduces the risk of infection.
Experiments have shown that PrEP significantly reduces HIV infections in at-risk groups.
A drug taken before intercourse means that there are enough drugs in the body during intercourse that prevent HIV infection.
The medicine can be taken daily or two hours before sex.
The target group of treatment includes gay men, bisexual men and transgenders, as well as people who have an HIV-positive partner who does not receive effective treatment.
The clinics will select candidates who meet the criteria for the experiment from men, women and transgenders, as well as those whose HIV-positive partners are not known to receive antiretroviral treatment for their disease.
People living in England and who have enrolled in the treatment of a general practitioner can also apply for a trial at their local sexual health clinic.
The program will cost 10 million pounds, and the English NHS signed a contract for pharmaceuticals this week following an international tender.
The purpose of the experiment is to assess the potential of PrEP by gathering extensive clinical evidence of its targeting, taking and treatment implementation.
“This significant new intervention should complement and intensify the already diverse and better-performing ways to prevent HIV,” says Stevens.
‘That yet another significant achievement in the more than 30-year struggle to solve one of humanity’s greatest health challenges.”
“This is a crucial moment in the fight against HIV,” said Deborah Gold, director of the national AIDS.
“If PrEP medication is correctly targeted at those who need it and those at risk, it could have a revolutionary impact on England’s HIV epidemic.
Starting in September, those at risk will receive a medicine through an NHS-funded experiment that they can use independently without the need to negotiate with a partner. It will have an empowering effect that will improve the lives of very many people.
This was welcome news.”
“The most important thing now is to ensure that the experiment is launched quickly throughout the country, and no one at risk is excluded from it,” said Ian Green, director of the Terrence Higgins Foundation.
‘The PrEP drug used in the experiment has been acquired, and we will soon be able to protect ten thousand people in high-risk groups from HIV infection.’
“The start of the PrEP experiment is a welcome event that was prolonged in vain for several months due to the government’s sluggishness,” said Sharon Hodgson, Health Minister of the Shadow Government.
“We have evidence that the drug can have a revolutionary effect on the work we do to prevent HIV and stop the spread of this imminent disease.
The experiment will help us to better understand the real benefits of PrEP.
It is now important that the experiment is launched as soon as possible throughout the country, so that we can protect those exposed to HIV and once again get one step closer to a situation where the spread of HIV in society has managed to halt.”
The quality of life: the mid-layer and smart interior design solutions have made the small Earl’s Court apartment a spacious home
Joanne Leigh, a former banker interested in renovating the properties, moved from the Knightsbridge residential area to a smaller apartment in Earl’s Court in 2014.
The apartment is on the first floor of Queen Anna's-era-style house, according to the fact that Joanne hasn't packed his gear and moved.
“Before this, I lived with my ex in Knightsbridge in a significantly larger apartment,” Joanne says.
‘I’m looking for something similar in my price range.
I went here to the apartment display and noticed that the rooms were as high as in my apartment at the time.
I immediately fell in love and made an offer on the same day.”
Since then, an apartment of about 90 square meters has undergone a complete makeover.
It was a two-room apartment where you could only get into the bedroom from the kitchen. Joanne wanted more storage space and an interlayer so that the room height could be utilized.
“I wanted space, big windows and a lot of light.
That’s my demand,” he said.
Erfan was commissioned to take advantage of the space in the nearly four-meter-high rooms and to create an apartment that would not only be a renovation and sale, but a home.
The height of the room was not quite enough for two overlapping spaces, so the challenge was to build an interlayer where you could stand.
Erfan's solution required creative thinking.
“Steel structures are quite complex.
The premises had to be arranged on top of each other so that there was no need to crouch.
The structures had to be modified so that the height was just right both on and under them.”
Erfan was able to place two full-height rooms with fascinating details.
On the inter-floor, Retrouvius' copper truss window opens into the elegant living area.
Mirror gold furniture, silver effects and Serge Mouille's vintage luminaires create a signature style.
The use of mirrors brings theatricality, a sense of space and light.
Mirrors at both ends of a short corridor make the corridor look much longer.
The mirror in the middle of the kitchen is like a window with a view of the other room.
The mirror-surfaced furniture at the entrance has storage space for Joanne's cutlery and glassware.
Elegance is enhanced by the fact that the kitchen can be hidden when it is not in use.
Kitchens equipped with a folding door are not a new solution,
But in Joanne's kitchen, what's unique is that the door is lifted up in the same way as the garage door.
‘It took a little planning,’ Erfan says of the steel-reinforced MDF door.
‘It is balanced, that is, it glides very easily when lifted into place.’
With the sensible use of space and the planning of the storage space, Joanne received 16 extra square meters, a second bedroom and an even better layout.
‘When selling an apartment, the area determines – or the valuation of the property,’ Erfan says.
‘Basically, the real estate agent walks into the apartment and calculates the price on the basis of the area.’
In Joanne's apartment, the surface area was increased using high-quality materials that bring luxury to the space.
‘The tenant is in contact with the materials in the apartment, and the emotional connection to the apartment arises partly based on them,’ Erfan says.
‘No one attaches to the electrical wires in the walls.
They are also important but not in the same way.”
Joanne's apartment is an example that good planning and additional square meters can make the basic apartment exceptionally fine.
It can also happen that you don’t want to move out of the apartment anymore.
Pregnant Princess Sofia beamed in her summer look
