He's raising us with sweet chili
In the summer of 1996, a stall appeared at the Lahti market where Thai food was sold.
They didn't dare to taste, they walked by.
At the stall, food was sold by Panya Suamsri-Penttilä, among acquaintances Panja.
He didn't know much Finnish, and the Finns didn't know Thai at that time.
Since people didn't have the courage to eat, Panja offered free tastings.
That's how he taught the people of Lahti to eat Thai food, one spring roll at a time.
Taste countries, he said.
I got hooked.
I went more and more, eventually every day.
In the summer of 1997, Panja opened its Thai canteen in Lahti Market Hall.
It had two small tables, I think there were less than 20 seats.
He taught us not only to eat Thai food, but also to sit next to a stranger.
When you're hungry and there's a limited number of seats, you can't leave an empty chair between yourself and the guest.
Little by little, we got to know each other better and became friends.
In the end, I sat by the plate with the dictionary to learn the Thai language.
Two years went to the market hall, but then the restaurant had to move, because I was told that homemade spice pastes smelled too much in the hall.
A new location was opened at the end of the market hall.
There's room.
Now we gossipers get to sit on our own tables again, only in the most crowded times we have to shyly ask the guests if there is room next door.
Panja knows her clients.
He'll be told what we've been doing and who we've seen.
He knows what food everyone eats.
He has seen how the life stages of the customers vary and the food company changes.
He remembers the name of a man I went to eat my first spring rolls.
He remembers me skinny and fat and remembers what I was like when I was young.
It's nice to go for lunch a little later than others, sit in my place next to the kitchen door and catch up with Panja.
Food isn't the most important thing we get from him.
He has taught tolerance and internationality with sweet chili sauce.
