The brothers are fighting for more than 500 million dollars in real estate
Two brothers are arguing about Victoria's largest real estate empire worth more than 500 million dollars. The controversy relates to a family business founded 40 years ago by their Italian family.
Colin De Lutis, a former chairman and real estate developer of Carlton's football club, lives in Tooraki's value area only three houses away from his younger brother Paul. The younger brother has brought a dispute over the distribution of real estate and business assets between the brothers to the Supreme Court.
Paul has been wanting to leave the family business since 2014, but there is a bitter controversy.
The brothers and their mother Anna jointly own 38 properties worth more than half a billion dollars. The family began accumulating property when they bought a deal on Collingwood's Smith Street in 1973.
The family set up the Westco Jeans store, which expanded into a chain of 160 stores.
The retail chain was sold for US$85 million in 1999.
The money was spent on real estate, and the family now owns offices, real estate development areas in Brunswick and Sorrento and apartments in the Gold Coast and Mt Buller, as well as suburban shopping malls.
According to an agreement with the family’s late father, Colin owns about two-thirds of the company’s assets. Paul describes his father as “the family’s driving force and central figure.”
According to court documents, the distribution ratio of the property means that Colin owns about 316 million dollars worth of real estate, and Paul about US$176 million.
Paul's not happy with the distribution of real estate and money.
‘In my opinion, Colin left the best properties to himself in his proposal,’ Paul wrote in his first sworn testimony to the court in 2015.
The case was heard last Friday for the first time by the Supreme Court after several mediation attempts.
The brothers are now also arguing over who controls the existing business. Paul argues that Colin has ruled him out of the business.
According to Colin, the claim isn't true. He accuses Paul of poor management of some properties.
Colin writes in one of his first sworn testimonies that he would like to come to a solution.
‘According to our Italian traditions, the family is most important to me,’ Colin said in a statement
There are hundreds of pages of sworn statements, emails and reports. The case is being investigated by consulting companies such as Deloitte, Pitcher Partners and Charter Keck Cramer, as well as the country’s best and most experienced lawyers, such as Allan Myers.
Anna De Lutis and her late husband Luigi were Italian colonists who founded milk bars and delicacies in the late 1950s. In the early 1970s, they bought a shop in Brunswick.
They were used as collateral when the family bought a property in Collingwood, where Colin started the family's first denim store.
Colin says he founded the store “in the last years of high school, when Paul was still a child (about 10 years old).
Anna De Lutis says that she and Luigi, who died in 2012, wanted to move the properties to their sons harmoniously.
‘Luigi often said he didn’t wait for the brothers to end up in court,’ Anna wrote in
The family's other two sons, Mark and Luke, are no longer involved in De Lutis Group's business. According to Colin, Mark received US$10 million and Luke 26 million when he left the company.
According to Paul, Luke and Mark are ‘bitter about the economic conditions of their departure’.
Colin has kept a low profile, despite being a former board member of the Australian football league clust and TV star Eddie McGuire's best friend according to his 2015 biography.
Fairfax Media contacted Colin and Paul De Lutis and their lawyers on Friday.
Only Colin replied, saying in a text message he couldn't really comment because it's being dealt with in court.
“It’s very sad that my brother Paul has decided to sue me and drag our family’s name into a legal battle,” he wrote.
In mid-2016, Colin wrote to the court that he had tried to comply with his brother's demands.
‘Our father would not have wanted us to argue in this way,’ Colin continued, and said that he still hoped that a solution would be reached.
On Friday the hope seemed to have passed out.
‘Our case will be decided in court in the near future,’ Colin wrote.
Trump's new chief of staff intends to limit the president's media use.
Others have tried and failed.
Perhaps John F. Kelly will finally succeed.
The first.
According to Poltico magazine, the White House's new chief of staff plans to limit President Trump's use of the media and the follow-up of news so the boss doesn't get into trouble so often.
Journalist Josh Dawsey reports:
“When John F. Kelly, New Chief of Staff of the White House, met with executive staff on his first working day, he brought up a key issue that he intends to solve: he intends to limit President Donald Trump’s access to information.
Kelly told White House employees that all informations—both those who have been pressed and heard at briefings—must pass through him because the president makes better decisions when he receives good information.”
Kelly's words make sense, but others have also tried to limitate Trump's media use and failed.
President Trump has a relationship with television for decades – and as president, he still watches a lot of television.
“If presidential candidate Trump got nervous about the unfair news, it was worth showing him that sympathetic media discussed the issues in a more equitable way,” Sam Nunberg, an adviser in Trump’s campaign, told Politico newspaper in February.
Politico reporter Tara Palmeri wrote at the time that “six former campaign workers say it’s essential to control Trump’s use of Twitter to have a steady flow of compliment.”
All right, okay. Okay. Okay.
But it's ridiculous to argue that Trump's use of Twitter would ever have been under control.
“Maybe these campaign workers know something that the rest of us don’t know – maybe Trump’s tweets would have been even wilder without their efforts.
We'll probably never find out what kind of messages Trump didn't send.
If his staff has been able to prevent him from causing the problems at least a few times, their work has not been in vain.
However, no one has been able to consistently prevent Trump from causing controversy.
Part of the problem is due to the fact that there are rival factions in the White House trying to pursue their cause by giving Trump information that supports their view. That information can be trustworthy – or not.
In May, Politico wrote that advisers deliberately give the president questionable information.
Journalist Shane Goldmacher told about one of the cases:
‘Trump’s current and former employees say that he can become immensely outraged by newspaper clippings criticizing him – especially if they tell them about harmful information leaks, so he has to know where the information came from.
That's what happened at the end of February when someone misciparriously gave the president a copy of a news article on GotNews.com, founded by online provocator Charles C. Johnson, accusing Deputy Chief of Staff Katie Walsh of several White House information leaks.
Johnson had been permanently removed from Twitter due to harassment, and he had previously filed false accusations and later canceled them. He also did not provide concrete evidence.
Even so, Trump had started reading the article asking the staff about Walsh.”
Goldmacher added that Reince Priebus and White House Staff Secretary Rob Porter “had tried to put in place a management system for documents going to Trump.”
And what was the result?
Kelly's trying the same three months later.
Maybe he turns out to be a more powerful leader than Priebus, but Trump is still his own impulsive self and his aids are still pushing for their own affairs.
These immutable facts make Kelly's mission extremely difficult.
