The majority of Syrian refugees do not believe that they will return to their homeland permanently unless the war ends and ISIS leaves the country
The A Voice for Young Syrian Refugees study examined the opinions and attitudes of young Syrian refugees living in Jordan and Lebanon.
Refugees from Syria baking bread in a refugee camp in Jordan.
The majority of Syrian youths do not believe that they will return to their home country permanently.
According to them, the end of the war in Syria and the departure of ISIS from the country are the most important things that should happen so that they can return to their homeland.
Trump's administration isn't seen as bringing a change in the Syrian conflict.
Burson-Marsteller investigated the opinions and attitudes of young Syrian refugees in an international study.
Burson-Marsteller has been researching the opinions and attitudes of Arab youth for nine years.
This year, the Refugees of A Voice for Young Syria study examined the opinions and attitudes of young Syrian refugees living in Jordan and Lebanon.
Between February and March 2017, a total of 400 Syrian adolescents aged 18 to 24 responded to the survey, half of whom are women and half men.
Half of the interviews were conducted in Jordan and half in Lebanon.
According to the study, more than half of young Syrian refugees do not believe that they will return to their home country permanently in the future.
42 per cent of the respondents may think that they would return to their home country and four per cent could not say.
According to the study, young people's decision to return home is most influenced by the ongoing war in Syria and the presence of ISIS in the country
Nearly half (47%) consider the end of the war to be the most influential factor in a permanent homecoming, while a quarter think that the most important thing would be the departure of ISIS from the country.
Fewer than one-tenth of the most important factors influencing the decision are the improvement of the economic situation or the departure of Bashar al-Assad from Syria.
Military action alone will not solve the conflict
Syrian young people were asked what is the most important factor in terminating the conflict.
The majority is of the opinion that the end of the war is more essential than President Bashar al-Assad's abolition of power.
More than a quarter considers Al-Assad's presidency an obstacle to the peace treaty.
Nearly half feel that both political and military solutions are needed in order to put an end to the ongoing conflict in Syria.
Only more than a quarter believes in resolving the conflict through military actions alone.
The majority of Syrian refugee youths see ISIS power deteriorating over the past year, while a fifth feel that its power has strengthened.
According to the defendants, the most important thing in the fight against ISIS, and terrorism in general, is that the media would convey to people information that ISIS has nothing to do with Islam.
Nearly as many raised military measures and educational reform as their primary means.
According to just over a tenth, the most important thing would be to create better-paided jobs for young people.
When asked about the Trump administration's impact on the Syrian conflict, 66 percent of respondents believe the conflict won't change under the Trump administration.
Nearly one in four believe that the conflict in Syria will worsen under Trump.
Opinions are divided in terms of whether Russia's and Iran's involvement has improved or exacerbated the conflict.
Monty Widenius spends half of his time coding to keep the internet organized - the database he has developed is used, for example, by Wikipedia and Google
The Espoo-based company MariaDB develops the "world's fastest growing" open-source database.
Founded by coder Michael "Monty" Widenius, MariaDB's main product costs users absolutely nothing, and it can be developed online by any skilled programmer.
For example, Google and Wikipedia are users of a Finnish database, but they may not be paying customers.
With the help of databases, huge amounts of data are kept organized and quickly accessible.
For example, if you're using Linkedin, Twitter or Booking.com, they'll take advantage of MariaDB's or MySQL's database.
"All information that's not in image format comes from the database, and even the images are linked there," Explaines Widenius.
Widenius, the Chief Technology Officer of MariaDB, spends about half of his time coding.
A man working remotely from Kauniainen contributes to ensuring that the Internet operates smoothly.
Widenius developed MySQL, the world's most popular open-source database, together with the Swedish David Axmark.
In 2008, the technology company Sun acquired MySQL for one billion dollars, and a year later software giant Oracle devoured the Sun by 7.4 billion.
When the Oracle acquisition took place, a large part of MySQL's developers announced they were leaving.
"They didn't want to work for Oracle, who didn't make a certain promise about the future of MySQL," Widenius recalls.
Already at this stage, he had founded a company that began to develop MariaDB.
He didn't want his life's work to die.
I hired about twenty of the best developers from Suni and made sure that the continuity of the code is ensured with the help of the same guys who created it.
Since MySQL is an open source database, MariaDB was able to develop its own branch.
The name just had to be changed.
A foundation was established to monitor the freedom of development of MariaDB's code.
"During MySQL, these confirmations were not made," Widenius regrets.
Although many people have heard of Widenius and MySQL's billion-dollar deal, few Finns know anything about MariaDB.
"Last few years, our growth rate has been about 50 per cent per year," says Kenneth Paqvalén, CFO of MariaDB.
MariaDB, which focuses on growth, does not yet make a profit, and Paqvalén is scarbacked by the company's key figures.
He reveals that in the current financial year MariaDDB expects to receive orders worth 20 million euros.
Because the database is free of charge, the company has developed services around it that corporate customers are willing to pay for.
MariaDB will also help you exchange databases, which can be more than a year's work if the customer has used a closed database.
You can switch from MySQL to MariaDB in an instant, because they're versions.
Among other things, the Spanish operator Telefónica and DBS, the largest bank in South-East Asia, are the paying customers of MariaDB.
The most popular cloud services, such as Amazon's AWS, Microsoft's Azure, Alibaban Cloud, offer MariaDB to their customers.
At the same time, MariaDB automatically spreads to new users in Linux operating system distribution packages, where MariaDB has largely replaced MySQL.
Even though we're a small company, we've got connections with the biggest gamblers.
"Among our customers is one of the world's largest telecommunications operators, gaming companies, banks and even a few central banks," Paqvalén lists.
The database market is still powered by giants such as Oracle, Microsoft, IBM and SAP in their closed databases.
MySQL is a modest side business compared to the company's traditional enclosed database.
According to Gartner, the largest database companies have lost market share in recent years, in terms of net sales, gamblers such as MariaDB are still negligible, albeit fast-growing.
In a database market of about US$50 billion, open-source databases don't even reach a billion.
However, the weight of open source cannot be measured only in money, because the majority of users do not pay anything for these software.
Gartner predicts that in 2018 companies will develop more than 70 percent of their internal applications on top of open databases.
In addition, more than half of the old databases would then have been switched to open databases, or at least the transition would have begun.
Big companies don't get new customers, because most of them choose open source.
This has led to the fact that license fees for commercial databases have jumped into the clouds.
The companies want to make as much money as before with a smaller number of customers," Widenius reflects.
Due to the hassle of changing a closed database, the majority of customer companies have obediently swallowed up price increases.
"There are a lot of customers for whom it's really difficult to navigate, because their old applications and others are stuck in a closed database," Widenius says.
However, he still believes that the size of the customers is about to be reached.
When license fees increase every year, sooner or later, the customer switches to an open-source database.
The price is 10-20 per cent earlier, and the service is better, because the customer can easily switch to another database if they wish.
Moreover, many companies are faced with major system changes when they move their operations to cloud services.
At the same time, many people change database software.
Last spring, MariaDB received a loan of 25 million euros from the European Investment Bank (EIB).
It was the largest growth funding ever granted by the EIB to the Nordic countries.
The company has approximately 115 employees worldwide and now intends to hire more.
One of the reasons for the EIB's funding was MariaDB's impact on public IT acquisitions.
The company has public sector customers, for example, in the UK and Germany.
The public sector in Europe pays billions of euros each year from databases.
"Many countries want to move to open-source solutions to use tax money more efficiently," Paqvalén says.
Widenius is annoyed at how money is wasted in Finland, for example, on closed healthcare systems, when open systems are cheaper, and the subscriber is not at the mercy of a single software company.
Some say that open source is an ideology, but I think it's a better way to make code.
The community helps and does something that you can't invent or do yourself.
The 55-year-old Widenius is one of the best-known open-source promoters in addition to Linus Torvalds, another Finnish Linux developer.
Widenius helps other companies develop functional business models on top of open source.
I still hold 4-5 speeches every year around the world on this topic.
Italy is stepping up its approach to non-governmental rescue vessels
Italy has warned non-governmental organisations that rescue refugees on their vessels in the Mediterranean that they may not continue to operate unless they undertake to comply with the new rules governing their activities.
‘If NGOs do not commit to the new Code of Conduct, it is difficult to see how they could continue to operate,” Interior Minister Marco Minniti said in an interview with La Stampa.
Minnit's warning came the day after the Italian authorities seized a vessel of the German Nouveau Rettet aid organisation, which is suspected to have cooperated with smugglers who promoted illegal immigration.
The aid agency, which operated for only a year, declined to comment on the detailed charges against prosecutor Ambrogio Cartosio.
“For us, saving lives is and will be the most important thing. We are very sorry that we are currently unable to operate in the search and rescue area,” the organization said in a statement on social media.
‘We can only assess the accusations once we have compiled all the necessary information and are able to assess the situation.’
The Italian authorities had been observating Jugend Rettet's Iuventa platform since October.
The crew is suspected to have boarded boatloads of refugees directly from people smugglers who regainned the boats.
One such case is alleged to have occurred just a few kilometres off the coast of Libya, according to the prosecutor's document, the contents of which have been published by the Italian media.
The crew is suspected of defying the Italian Coast Guard, which supervises rescue operations in the area, and the motivation is the enthusiasm for assistance.
According to the Code of Conduct, vessels such as Iuventa must be accompanied by an Italian police officer who supervises the activities.
Only three of the nine NGOs participating in search and rescue operations have so far agreed to follow the Code of Conduct: Save the Children and the Maltese MOAS, as well as the Spanish Pro-Activa Open Arms;
which announced on Thursday that it would consider the new rules unnecessary but acceptable because they do not create ‘significant changes or obstacles’ to the organisation’s rescue operations.
Among those refusing to follow the Code of Conduct is the Nobel laureate Doctors Without Borders.
Minniti, on the other hand, noted that the number of migrants arriving in Italy has fallen sharply in recent weeks, as he sees as an indication that efforts to strengthen Libya's coast guard and cooperation with local mayors will begin to produce results.
‘In the last few days, there has been light at the end of the tunnel,’ the minister said.
He revealed that there are plans to continue discussions this month with the mayors of Libya on economic development projects, as well as with Chad, Niger and Mali on measures to reduce the number of refugees leaving for Europe.
A seasonal worker is suspected to have killed his cohabiting partner in Enontekiö, the suspect was found in the terrain - the case to the prosecutor
The alleged crime took place in Kuttanen, Enontekiö, last December.
