The man was found coldered in the terrain and did no resistance when he was arrested.
The charge must be filed in less than a week.
The case may still be discussed in the District Court of Lapland during August.
Known for Harry Potter and My All-Haired Friends, Robert Hardy has died
Robert Hardy has died at the age of 91.
The actor's death was announced on Thursday by his family.
Born in 1925, Hardy began his acting career at the age of 24 in theatre.
During his long career, he also starred in movies and television.
Hardy is best known for the role of Minister of Magic Cornelius Toffee in Harry Potter films, as well as his veterinary role in James Herriot's all-haired friends.
The BBC's popular series was filmed for a total of seven seasons.
Michelle Carter sentenced to imprisonment for textal messages inciting suicide
A Massachusetts woman has been sentenced to two and a half years in prison for text messages and phone calls to incite her boyfriend Conrad Roy to commit suicide.
Michelle Carter, 20, is serving 15 months in jail and five years on parole.
Carter was sentenced to manslaughter in June. Her boyfriend Conrad Roy committed suicide on 13.7.2014.
‘My family is devastated,’ Conradi’s father said at Thursday’s session.
"My son was my best friend."
Carter appealed the verdict and waited for the application to be processed in freedom.
He was threatened with a 20-year sentence, but according to his lawyers, both Carter and Roy suffer from mental health problems.
Dressed in red trousers and a cream-colored blouse, Carter listened with his verdict his hands crossed with a weeping gaze on the ground.
‘His actions killed Conrad Roy,” the prosecutor told the court.
“He ended Conrad’s life to heal his own.
“He didn’t take responsibility,” the prosecutor said.
“He has shown no remorse.”
According to Carter's lawyer, the incident was caused by "shocking circumstances that Carter deeply regrets."
However, Judge Lawrence Moniz was of the opinion that Carter's “age or maturity level, or even mental health problems, had no significant impact on his actions.”
The prosecutor read Conrad's mother Lynn Roy's statement, in which the mother told me that “not a day passed that I would not mourn the death of my beloved son.
I want him to be proud of me and how I can cope with all of this. I will try my best to comfort his sisters in this pain that we will always carry.”
Conrad's sister, Camdyn Roy, told the court that she was “persecuted” by the idea that she will never see her brother’s wedding and not being an aunt to her children.
‘It’s painful to lose someone who’s been in my life every day since I was born. That pain will never relent,” Camdyn said.
There seems to be a precedent, as there's no law in the state of Massachusetts that inciting suicide would be a crime.
Carter was 17 years older when Roy was found in a car deceased of carbon monoxide poisoning in
The incident attracted nationwide attention when it emerged that Carter's text messages had incited his boyfriend to commit suicide.
‘Hang yourself, jump off the roof, stab yourself. I don’t know, there are many habits,’ Carter wrote in a number of messages he had sent to Roy in the two weeks prior to his death. Roy was on vacation with his family at the time.
Just shortly before Roy’s suicidal Carter wrote, ‘You’re going to have to do it, Conrad, and “You just have to launch the generator, and you’re free and happy.”
Judge Moniz said Carter sought attention with Roy's death. The judge ordered that Carter should not benefit from the case by giving interviews.
The case has divided the opinions of experts.
Only a few have defended Carter. One of the defenders is Amanda Knox, 30, who was sentenced to the murder of a British roommate in Italy in 2007. He was released two years later.
The Los Angeles Times published Knox’s opinion piece, stating: ‘Michelle Carter needs compassion and help, not jail.’
Secretary-General of NATO: The worst relations with Russia since the Cold War
Relations between NATO and Russia have clearly deteriorated, NATO Secretary-General Jens Stoltenberg said on Thursday.
Stoltenberg accused Russia and its position in the conflict in Ukraine.
“I think you can say that NATO’s relationship with Russia is now more difficult than it has ever been since the end of the Cold War,” Stoltenberg said in an interview with CNN.
After the end of the Cold War, we hoped that we could seek close cooperation with Russia.
But, especially after the illegal annexation of Crimea in 2014 and due to the continuing instability caused by Russia in the territory of eastern Ukraine, NATO's relationship with Russia has deteriorated considerably.
According to Stoltenberg, however, NATO is still committed to acting in such a way that tensions will not peak.
As long as we are strong and as long as we are predictable, we will be able to engage in a political dialogue with Russia to avoid the escalation of the situation and a new Cold War.
Prior to Stoltenberg's comments, U.S. President Donald Trump said that relations between the U.S.-Russia had even fallen dangerously poorly.
Trump urged to thank Congress for their bad relations, which last week approved new sanctions against Russia.
Audi is making changes to the 850 000 diesel engines due to a setback experienced by car manufacturers
The day before Audi's announcement, BMW offered to update the software of more than 350 000 cars.
The head of BMW said the company believed that “there are more sensible alternatives than driving restrictions.” He referred to European cities, such as BMW’s hometown Munich, which are considering restricting or prohibiting the use of diesel vehicles.
Daimler announced on Tuesday that it will make changes to more than three million Mercedes engines to reduce diesel emissions.
None of the companies talked about the recall.
European car manufacturers have strongly marketed the use of diesel vehicles in Europe and the United States in order to achieve CO2 emission targets.
Nitric oxide emissions from diesel engines are considered to cause cancer and asthma.
The installation costs of devices that neutralize emissions will increase, which makes it difficult to price cars competitively.
German car manufacturers are closely monitored, and Chancellor Angela Merkel has been accused of complimenting large car companies and ignoring problems.
The companies are trying to avoid Volkswagen's mistakes. Volkswagen installed scamming devices in the cars, which reduced emissions to comply with the regulations in tests. In normal driving, the emissions were higher.
There have been a number of charges against Volkswagen executives in the United States, but the actions of the leaders are being investigated on both sides of the Atlantic.
Last month, a former head of thermodynamics in Audi's engine development department was arrested in Germany.
Zaccheo Giovanni Pamio is Italian, so he can be extradited to the United States for a possible trial.
Earlier this year, Audi was involved in a criminal investigation into Volkswagen. Authorities suspected that Audi had installed a system in its cars to circumvent European emission requirements. The accusation was based on similar research in the United States.
Audi's investigation could significantly weaken Volkswagen's status: the luxury car manufacturer's share of Volkswagen's profits is disproportionate.
The Venezuelan vote began a fraud investigation, big demonstrations on the way
According to the company, the number of voters reported by the Venezuelan authorities is at least one million higher than the number of actual voters.
“This is an unheard-of opinion from a company that has no role in the election result,” Tibisay Lucenan, chairman of the National Electoral Commission, told reporters.
Maduro assures that the crisis will end
On Sunday's controversial vote in Venezuela, a spokesman was elected to amend the country's constitution.
The opposition has strongly criticised the vote and organized a change against the demonstrations.
Opposition to Maduro has postponed its own demonstrations so that they would be underway at the time of appointment.
According to El Pais, the country's national security forces began to take control of the country's parliament building on Wednesday evening.
The troops are supposed to ensure the safety of the appointment ceremony.
Climate change threatens Provence lavender
Laventel has become a symbol of the Provençal region of southern France, but it is now threatened by climate change.
Lavender had declined due to drought and insects carrying diseases. Scientists and farmers are feverishly trying to find a solution to the problem.
‘Laventeli is downright endangered in the Provençal region because it has been affected by the effects of climate change in recent years. As a result, rainfall in spring and autumn in particular has clearly decreased,” says Eric Chaisse, an expert at the CRIEPPAM research institute in Provence, which specialises in agriculture.
“This shortens lavender.
In addition, the drought has brought a lot of small cicadas to the area that spread microbacteria.”
The stakes are high.
Lavender oil attracts tourists, and it is also a highly sought-after ingredient of perfumes and cosmetics.
‘Instead of long cobs and large flowers, we now have smaller flowers that are poorly developed or weakened, or are completely absent,” Chaisse said.
‘In other words, there are fewer parts containing essential oil.”
The solution is sought by intensifying irrigation, slowing the evaporation of soil moisture and spraying plants with a protective mixture of clay and water. In addition, droughting is better resistant varieties.
Journalist's murder manifests a growing threat in Mexico
On Wednesdays, the editorial office of the once-a-week Riodoce newspaper usually meets to discuss how the magazine handles the most recent cases related to organized crime, corrupt authorities, and an endless drug war in Sinaloa state.
Today, however, we are discussing security because of a recent tragedy.
Journalists have been urged to change their daily routines.
Two experienced journalists are discussing which is safer: leaving the children at home or taking them with them to the delivery, which underwent a grenade attack in 2009.
Security experts have written three words on the chalkboard at the front of the room: opponents, neutrals, allies.
They'll ask reporters for names for each group. No evidence is needed, observations and sensations are enough.
Names of drug traffickers, politicians, businessmen and journalists who are suspected of being on the payroll of the government or drug cartels are written on the board, and they're criminals who make Mexico's chaos life-threatening.
Violence is incessant, and is increasingly targeted by journalists. According to the Reporters’ Protection Committee, at least 25 journalists have been murdered since President Enrique Peña Nieto took office in December 2012. There are 589 reporters who have been subjected to attacks and threats. At least seven journalists have been murdered in seven states this year.
The most recent victims are Javier Valdez Cardenas, founder of Riodoce, journalist and source of inspiration.
“The biggest mistake is living in Mexico and being a journalist,” Valdez wrote in one of his many books on drug violence.
He is deeply needed, even though he is everywhere. The façade of Riodoce’s building is covered by a huge image of Valdez showing the middle finger. The picture reads: “Justice”. Journalists Aaron Ibarra and Miriam Ramirez in their thirties are dressed in T-shirts adorned with Valdez’s smiling face. Valdez has glasses and a trademark.
The meeting will be held less than two months after Valdez's death. Reporters will discuss their joint trauma, nightmares, insomnia and fears of paranoia.
In 201910, a federal prosecutor's post was established specifically for the handling of cases of journalists, but according to then only two charges have been filed, according to the Committee for the Conservation of Journalists.
Journalist murderers rarely go to court, as well as of the thousands of murders involving drug trafficking and organised crime each year, only a few go to court.
Riodoce's journalists plan to continue writing about the violence in Sinaloa, even though they're heartbroken and the situation is even more dangerous.
According to Ibarra, there is little benefit from the security meeting because the killers are not known and there is no hope for justice.
‘To be honest, this meeting is a waste of time,’ he says.
“We can’t trust anyone as long as we don’t know why.”
Valdez had left Riodoce's delivery in Culiacan, the state's state, on the morning of 15.5.2017.
He had driven only a couple of blocks on his red Toyota when two men stopped him and forced him out of his car and fired twelve shots. The number is presumably related to the name of the newspaper: Riodoce means the twelve river.
The shooter drove off Valdez's car and crashed it some distance away.
Valdez was 50 years old.
He was mourned by his wife and two adult children.
Rumors move quickly and float freely in Culiacan.
Valdez's death, however, is silent.
Sinalo has been one of Mexico's bloodiest battlefields since Guzman was arrested last year and deported to the United States in January.
On the streets of Sinaloa, someone or several people are shot to death every day, and the cemetery has two-storey two-storey burial buildings for drug lords, which are larger than the homes of many.
It’s ‘calm’ when only one cartel controls ports and drug trafficking routes to the United States.
Although Valdez was well aware of the dangers of his work, Riodoce's founder and director, 60-year-old Ismail Bojorquez feels guilty for not being able to protect his friend.
He believes two mistakes led to Valdez's murder.
In February, Riodoce published an interview with Damaso Lopez. He leads a rivaling faction that wages a war against Guzman's sons.
Guzman's sons may have been angered by the story: customers suspected of being members of the gang bought every issue of the magazine as soon as the magazine entered the points of sale.
Another mistake was that Valdez was not forced to leave the country after another newspaper that published the same story was attacked.
Valdez was a legend in Mexico and abroad, and his murder is seen as a turning point in violence against journalists.
He had survived so many that his friends and colleagues could no longer be afraid on his behalf.
He had made a long career in Noroeste when, in 2003, he decided to establish Riodoce with five colleagues. The magazine's shares were then sold for 50 dollars.
“It was impossible to do journalism without addressing the drug problem,” Bojorquez said.
Over time, the magazine began to feel its bold and honest stories, and its sales and advertising revenue grew.
Journalists appreciated the opportunity to publish hardcore investigative journalism without fear of censorship, and readers were fascinated by articles in the magazine that no one else dared to publish.
Eight years after its founding, Riodocé won the prestigious Maria Moors Cabot award for her coverage in Latin America.
In the same year, Valdez received the International Press Freedom Award of the Editors' Protection Committee for his courage to follow the story of Mexico's drug trade relentlessly.
He openly admitted that he was afraid.
‘I want to be able to move on,’ he said when he was rewarded by the Editors’ Conservation
“The drug deal is a ‘lifestyle’ in Sinaloa,” Valdez said in an interview with Rompeviento TV last October.
‘The reporter must accept a task that falls upon him – or play a fool.
“I don’t want anyone to have to ask me where I was when I was swimmed into death – why didn’t I tell you what was going on?”
Riodoce’s staff needs Valdez – a joker who cursed like a harbor worker. They long for a friend who always gave a hug and advice. They need a teacher who knew how to cope.
They relied on his routine.
He's always wearing a panama hat.
He went to El Guayabo bar opposite the delivery and was always sitting at the same table.
Now they're asking if the routines were his fate.
The death of a colleague has also made them think about how they can best do their job and stay alive.
‘Criminals don’t like it if we’re discussing their women and their children and their clean businesses, their secret drug trafficking runways.
They were excluded,” Bojorquez said.
In the best of cases, it mean strong self-censorship and self-preservation,
Relying on one's own instincts.
If you feel like everything's not right, you have to stay away.
According to Riodoce’s reporter Andres Villarreal, the problem is that ‘the impression can be deceptive, and that’s what happened in Javier’s case.’
The old rules no longer apply in Sinaloa, nor do they apply in Tamaulipas, Veracruz, Guerrero and other states where there are dangerous networks: attractive smuggling routes, wealthy agencies and corrupt authorities.
The operating environment is constantly changing.
A few days earlier, it had emerged that only spyware sold to the governments had followed activists and journalists in Mexico.
Outside the building, two police officers had gone to the shade of the tree to shelter from a 40-degree heat.
The state government had ordered them to guard Riodoce's premises, located in a four-storey building in the middle-class Culiacan region.
Some reporters were half-jokingly wondering if these two police officers belonged to the group of 50 per cent that the state's governor didn't consider reliable.
Months had passed since journalists had been visiting the mountainous countryside dominated by drug gangs.
For this week's issue, Riodoce's delivery planned three main things.
Julio Cesar Chavez's brother had been killed in Sinaloa.
The newspaper also intended to reveal that the government's spending had focused on the governor's hometown.
In addition, one of Culiacan's most expensive restaurants, just a block away from the prosecutor's office, had been kidnapped by a group of people.
There was no official information on who had been abducted and how, so it had to proceed cautiously: everyone in the city knew that the restaurant was favored by both drug traffickers and the authorities.
The reporter found out in public documents that the restaurant was registered under the name of a politician belonging to the ruling Institutional Revolutionary Party (PRI). PRI has ruled Mexican politics at all levels for almost a century.
Governors have recently been accused of corruption in cases that have received a lot of publicity.
Villarreal asked the reporter to write about previous incidents at the same place. In one of them “El Chapo” Guzman’s son managed to escape and avoid an army raid.
A few months earlier, readers would have knowned to receive reliable information about the abduction in Valdez's column.
‘In the past, we would have already known what had happened,’ says 46-year-old Villarreal, nicknamed ‘El Flaco’ due to his slender physique.
‘Now all contacts with our informants are exhausted.’
Valdez's office has been transformed into a warehouse that preserves signs and stickers against the persecution of journalists. Employees of the magazine have been given a new, unexpected task: they have become activists pushing for freedom of the press.
Journalist Miriam Ramirez took a few signs with him the next morning in front of the local prosecutor's office for a protesting, which was caused by yet another murder of a reporter.
Salvador Adame disappeared in the western state of Michoacan three days after Valdez's murder.
After the disappearance, a burnt-out body was found, based on DNA tests carried out by the authorities, Adame.
Journalists have begun to protest loudly throughout the country, with “SOS Press” texts appearing on the sidewalks and buildings.
Today, leader Bojorquez is in Washington, where he is trying to gain international support for the investigation into the Valdez case.
The day after Valdez's assassination, Ramirez met with the governor of Sinaloa and accused authorities of spying and killing journalists because they tell the truth.
He has since requested that he be moved away from government cases because he is concerned that his anger will not be able to be objective.
Valdez had repeatedly stated that journalists in Mexico were surrounded by organised crime, crime-permissive authorities and an indifferent society.
In his last book, Narcojournalism Valdez writes that drug gangs kill journalists, but they are also killed at the behest of politicians and security forces in agreement with organised crime.
According to Articulo 19, more than half of the attacks on journalists last year were carried out by the police and authorities.
‘People are silenced in Mexico by killing,’ Ramirez says.
Murders have a paralyzing effect.
No one forgets the death of blogger Maria Elizabeth Macias in the northern border state of Tamaulipas six years ago.
There was a message signed by the Zetas Cartel with his body: “I’m here because of my writings.”
There was a computer keyboard and headphones next to his cut-out head.
Some newspapers have decided to shut down, such as El Norte in the northern border state of Chihuahua after the murder of correspondent Miroslava Breach in March.
Some will continue to operate, such as Nuevo Laredo's El Manana, although its leader was assassinated in 2004.
In 2010, Diario de Ciudad Juarez posted a message to drug cartels: ‘What do you want from us?’
Some of the reporters have fled their states or the whole country.
It's a difficult decision.
It's hard to find a job in exile, and you have to be on guard all the time.
Sometimes criminals searched for a reporter, as apparently happened to photographer Ruben Espinosa, who was murdered with four women in an apartment in Mexico City in 2015 three months since he escaped from Veracruz.
For those who continue their work, every day is full of dangerous decisions.
Ibarra – who was supposed to become a poet at the time – admits that writing about the drug trade is frightening.
‘I became a reporter because Mexico is on its way to hell,’ he said.
Some time ago, Riodoce's reporters sat in front of the editorial office on a beer on the pavement at midnight, when the magazine's latest issue had been completed.
19 people had been killed in a shooting between rivaling gangs and security forces at the Mazatlan beach resort.
The war accelerated, as promised in the cartel's messages found in the area.
Journalists put the news on Riodoce's website on the sidewalk with their smartphones.
I had to do the front page the next day.
Sirens howled nearby – yet another shooting.
Bojorquez glanced at the officers guarding the delivery:
If they were afraid, they covered it very well.
Riodoce's editorial board continued the tradition of the magazine's murdered co-founder under this massive portrait, in which the man shows the middle finger to the whole Sinalo.
Bojorquez says he can’t even consider shutting up. “How could I shut down the magazine when our trainee asked me to get on the street on the same day Javier was murdered?”
Hannu Manninen's Olympic project started with a huge setback - "Hard place"
Hannu Manninen's party, which turned 39 in April, was short-lived, as the professional pilot started preparing for the Olympic season.
However, the training period began disastrously when Manninen fell ill with the flu.
This was the beginning of a long period of illness, which stretched for almost five weeks.
May went practically completely when I got sick.
First there was a cold and then there was such a pediatric disease as an ear infection, for which I had to take a course of antibiotics.
I was able to do only ten per cent of the planned exercises.
I can't remember the last time I've been sick for so long.
It was a tough place when I couldn't take advantage of my training enthusiasm, Manninen tells Ilta-Sanomat.
Test results plummeted
The 2007 world champion did not get a training permit until the second week of June, which was reflected in the roller mat test carried out on 12 June by the Finnish Centre for Competitive and Top-Leaved Sport (KIHU).
In the test, the athlete skies on the mat, the speed of which is increased every three minutes.
After every three minutes, the mat stops for about ten seconds, and the tester takes a lactic acid sample from the athlete's fingertip with a needle.
Last autumn I managed on the mat for 32 minutes, but now I only stayed for 28 minutes.
There was a wall, Manninen says.
In a better direction
After the initial difficulties, Manninen says that he remained healthy.
I've done a variety of calm aerobic exercises that I've tried to normalize the body's metabolism.
Now it's a really good five-week period.
Midsummer was also a week and a half holiday from flying.
My family also had time to go to the cottage.
Last season Manninen took part in only four World Cup competitions ahead of the World Championships in Lahti.
The best result was seen immediately in the opening competition, when Manninen climbed to 18th in top skiing.
Guidelines drawn
Manninen and Petter Kukkonen, the head coach of the national team, have drawn up a training and competition plan extending until next February.
We are now looking for a different rhythm compared to last season.
After the First Snow Games, Ruka would have a weekend of three World Cups.
In December, I would like to participate in at least one World Cup weekend, maybe in Ramsau.
At the end of January, we are still planning to participate in Seefeld.
I hope after that the name can be found on the Olympic team's list, Manninen says.
The Pyeongchang Olympics would be the sixth Winter Olympic of his career.
Now the most important thing is to stay healthy, the Nummela resident, who celebrated gold in Salt Lake City in the 2002 team competition, underlines.
Dinosaur protected himself from his enemies by disguising himself
At first, it looks like a frightening statue.
Now scientists have uncovered the first research results of a rhino-sized creature discovered by a Canadian miner that was so well-preserved that experts call it the Mona Lisa of the dinosaurs.
It had been preserved so well that the researchers were even able to determine that its scaly skin was reddish-brown and had a “problematic past.”
According to Current Biology magazine, it is the best-preserved armored dinosaur of all time and one of the world's best representatives of its species.
The newly discovered fossil is a nodosaurus that lived 110–112 million years ago in the middle of the Cretaceous period.
Scientists consider this surprisingly well-preserved fossil to be a truly significant discovery.
According to the National Geographic, the armoured vegetarian is the best-preserved fossil of its kind ever.
It was found at the Suncor Millenium mine in northern Alberta, Canada, on March 21, 2011, and was discovered by a miner named Shawn Funk.
He hit something that didn't seem to be a natural part of the surrounding cliff and decided to examine his discovery more closely.
A fossil found in Kallio was sent to Tyrrell's Royal Paleontological Museum.
That's where the beast was excavated for six years from a boulder of 1 100 kilograms.
The results of the hard work can now be seen.
‘If you squint your eyes, you can almost believe that it was asleep,’ says science writer Caleb Brown, who works as a researcher at Tyrrell’s Royal Museum.
“It will go into the history of science as the Mona Lisa of dinosaurs, one of the most beautiful and best-preserved dinosaurs.
According to Naha's studies, meat-eating dinosaurs were likely to pose a significant threat to this vegetarian, even though it had armour and it resembled a walking tank.
Its red-and-white armor disguised it from predators and was also protected by a darker and lighter coloring on the top, which is still found in many animals.
According to experts, it was thus better able to mix its surroundings and avoid its enemies, larger dinosaurs.
The 110-million-year-old nodosaurus made headlines in May and is now on display at Tyrrell's Royal Paleontological Museum.
Investigators have named it Borealepe a marmitchell museum technician after Mark Mitchell. Mitchell belonged to a team that took more than 7 000 hours to dig up the dinosaur.
Nodosaurus has been preserved so amazingly well that Mitchell and an international team of scientists have been able to determine the patterns and shape of its body scales and armor.
They found that it was still protected in the animal world by the general coloring, in which the body is darker on the top and lighter underneath.
However, the sighting was still a surprise, as Borealope is significantly larger than existing animals with similar coloring.
It can be concluded that meat-eating dinosaurs predecied it to such an extent that evolution favored hiding as a means of survival rather than in battle.
Among today's animals, for example, deer, zebras and armadillas have the same coloring. They are significantly smaller and therefore easier prey, suggesting that nodosaurus was also seriously threatened.
‘Predation of a large, heavily armored dinosaur tells you how dangerous chalky dinosaurs were,” Brown said.
The chemical analysis of the organic compounds in Nodosaurus scales reveals that its skin was reddish-brown.
Scientists continue to investigate the nodosaurus in order to draw conclusions about its life. Among other things, they study the contents of its intestine to find out what it ate at its last meal.
Investigators believe it fell into the river when it died and drifted to the sea where it sank on its back to the seabed.
It was as warm in Alberta at the time as it is today in Florida, and the rivers and seas were likely to go much further than they do today.
“This nodosaurus is really significant, because its scaly leather has been preserved and has also been preserved three-dimensionally in its original form,” Brown said.
‘That’s why it looks almost the same as it did in the early Cretaceous
It doesn’t take very much imagination to perceive it: if you squint a little, you can almost believe that it was sleeping.”
The Cretaceous period was an era of theropods, huge two-legged, flesh-eating dinosaurs.
Even though their king, the Tyrannosaurus rex, lived millions of years before Borealopelta, its mighty predecessors may have preyed on this armored dinosaur.
Tyrannosaurus rex's predecessors included, for example, Acrocanthosaurus, about 11.5-meter-long six-ton monster.
The latest discoveries from Nodosaurus researchers have been published in the journal Current Biology. They believe that Borealope washed into the sea after his death and mumbled into the mud.
It was found at the Suncor Millenium mine in northern Alberta, Canada, on March 21, 2011, and was discovered by a miner named Shawn Funk.
According to the museum, it is the world's best-preserved armored danison with its skins and armor from snout to hip.
Nodosaurs, on average, were five meters long and weighed as much as 1,300 kilograms.
There were two half-metre spikes from their stages.
Scientists believe this armored vegetarian passed through modern-day Western Canada until a flooded river transported it to the open sea.
Its armor has been preserved up to small details in an underwater tomb.
Its fossilized remnants have been preserved so well that its leather is still left in the bumpy plates of the armor along its skull.
Michael Greshko wrote in the National Geographic magazine that such survival is “as rare as winning the lottery.
The closer you look at it, the more astonishing it feels.
There is still fossilized leather in the bumpy plates of its skull trap.
It's the right front leg on its side, five toes up.
I’ll also be able to lower the scales of the sole.”
How did an activist learn how to answer the question: who does the streets belong to?
Brittany Ferrell was deeply shocked 9.8.2014.
A day earlier, he had arrived in New York from his home in St. Louis and sat on the bed of his airbnb apartment, browsing Facebook on his phone.
That's when she noticed a message from a high school friend:
“Police just killed an 18-year-old kid, and his body is still on the street.”
Confused, he noted that no one else had posted the same information on his feed. That's why he shut down Facebook and opened Twitter.
There were numerous retweets on his timeline from @TheePharoah.
The username belongs to a rapper living in St. Louis who tweeted in real time about Michael Brown's death. White police had shot Brown, an unarmed 18-year-old gypsy, in Ferguson, Missouri.
The user in question had also posted a picture of Brown's lifeless corpse on the street, where it was left for four hours.
‘It’s unbelievable,’ Ferrell thought.
But the death was nothing new for him and his community.
When he came back to Twitter later that night, it was reported that people were gathering on Canfield Drive.
Users posted pictures of police shut-downs and screaming people, as well as a man who claimed to be Brown’s father, whose sign said: ‘Ferguson police just killed my unarmed son!’
Ferrell watched a real-time videotape of the police standing in front of the demonstrators with the barking dog.
He also said he saw a video in which another police officer tuned his rifle and shouted, “I’m going to kill all of you... animals.”
‘This isn’t normal,’ Ferrell said.
“It wasn’t the murder we’re used to and could be called typical.
That's when I felt chills.
I was angry and I cried.”
He booked a return flight home the next day.
The plane landed at eleven o'clock in the evening. Ferrell went to pick up his six-year-old daughter at her father's and decided that they would go to Ferguson the next morning.
About what happened to Ferrell and Ferguson in the next nearly three years, a documentary film “Whose Streets” has been made. It premiered in cinemas on 11.8.20017.
The film is a story of survival and demonstration, love and loss, as well as strength and perseverance from the point of view of the local people who went to the streets to demand answers.
When Ferrell drove towards Ferguson with his daughter dressed in a floral dress and a crown compatible with it, they reconvented a discussion about “what it’s like to live in gypsy and under what conditions.” They had talked about it countless times before that morning.
“Remember when I told you how black people have had to fight for the things they believe in?” Ferrell recalls saying to his daughter.
“We’re going to Ferguson because police killed an 18-year-old boy and that’s not right.”
‘I couldn’t help but take my daughter with me,’ Ferrell said later.
“Today’s events are the culmination of the events of the past.
My daughter has the right to know it and grow into it.
He must be prepared for activism and organization, but he must also know the different layers and circumstances of the experience of living in black. And he needs to know what we need to do to liberate in order to liberate ourselves.
Even though I've fortunately been able to protect her from blatant racism, she's still just like other black girls.
“I think I’d harm him if I protected him from reality.”
Their first stop in Ferguson was South Florissant Road, which is located opposite the police station. It became a camp site for activists when they waited for information on the handling of the incident and eventually learned that no charges would be filed against the police.
That's where Ferrell and his daughter went to help a local entrepreneur who made lunch packages.
They packed lunches in bags and distributed them to the demonstrators.
Ferrell gradually began to approach activism in a new way.
‘My activism completely changed since August 9,’ he said. While studying at the University of Missouri in St. Louis, Ferrell was the president of an organization of minority nursing students. At the time, he focused on food rights and health inequalities in black communities.
‘I had no experience of organizing.
Somehow it just happened.”
Weeks later, Ferrell met Sabaah Folayan, the assistant director of the documentary.
Folayan said he and cinematographer Lucas Alvarado-Farrar “just wanted to tell” what was going on.
“Do you really want to tell me what’s going to happen, or do you want a story that you can take advantage of?”
The reason for the suspicion was the countless times that people had appeared in the local community, usually white, who just wanted to benefit from its difficulties and persistence.
“We’ve heard that those born and raised in Ferguson didn’t have a common voice,” Folayan said in a recent interview. “We wanted to do things differently.”
Folayan had already contacted co-director Damon Davis, known for his activism among prisoners sentenced to death.
Davis' involvement prompted Ferrell to join the project along with six other locals, and the film crew followed the events in the years following Brown's shooting.
“This isn’t a documentary where someone speaks to us or speaks for us, but we talk,” Davis said.
“The most important thing for me was how the local people are portrayed in the movie, because I’m portrayed in the same way.”
According to Folayan, the intention was not to focus on Ferrell's experiences, because they wanted to show that the popular movement “is not about a charismatic leader, but about the community". However, Ferrell turned out to be the most open of those described.
That's why the documentary sees surprisingly private aspects of Ferrell's life, such as falling in love and getting married.
‘I felt pretty nude,’ Ferrell laughs as he told me what it felt like to see the movie for the first time.
“But I understood that this work and the humane depiction of blacks requires a certain vulnerability.”
In addition, Ferrell's story recounts and re-emphasizes the role of black queer women in the larger Black Live Matter movement.
The movement was created on social media (#BlackLivesMatter) after a policeman had fired black teenage boy Trayvon Martin in 2012. Two of the store's three founders identify with queer identity.
‘The leadership of black queer women has sustained the whole movement,’ Ferrell says. ‘It’s because we’re constantly having to fight on multiple fronts.
“Black queer women have had to endure everything.”
Folayan agrees: “The movement is indeed sustained by black women, among whom there are a lot of queer women.”
However, he emphasizes that Ferrell was not included in the documentary ‘self-intentionally’ because of his queer identity.
‘He got the energy moving,’ Folayan said.
“However, it’s no coincidence that he was on the front line. Black queer women live at the intersection of many things, and they have a lot at stake.
His energy is transmitted on the big screen.”
Ferguson has been left by news film crews a long time ago, but relations between the community and the police are still tense.
Ferrell still has two and a half years of parole left, and he was sentenced for blocking the motorway at a demonstration.
His description of the social political climate in his neighbourhood is brief and concise: ‘Confusion.’
Yet he still relies on the slogan he learned by heart on the streets of Ferguson three years ago:
“It’s our duty to fight for our freedom.
It's our duty to win.
We need to love and support each other.
“All we can do is lose our chains.”
Appropriate resemblance: the artist creates portraits of Chelsea Manning's DNA
The Fridman Gallery in New York has suspended a face in the middle of the exhibition space – white, black and brown faces, between which you can see a resemblance.
These 30 works are portraits made by artist Heather Dewey-Hagborg, based on DNA samples taken from Chelsea Manning's cheek and hair.
Manning sent samples from Fort Leaveworth Prison, where he served his 35-year sentence. Manning was sentenced for leaking classified diplomatic telegrams to WikiLeaks.
Dewey-Hagborg's A Becoming Resemblance exhibition was opened 2.8.2017.
Dewey-Hagborg combines technology, information technology, and art. Manning is a transwoman and groundbreaking dissident whose jail sentence Barack Obama relieved on the last day of his term in office.
Dewey-Hagborg analyzed Manning's DNA algorithmically and created 30 portraits based on his genetic data. They are a sharp, albeit slightly theoretical, statement on the adaptability of DNA data, ambiguity and the cause-and-consequences behind the interpretations, as well as identity.
Chelsea Manning, born Bradley Edward, has openly stated that she has identified with women since puberty. In 2013, she was found guilty of 20 offences, such as violations of espionage law, computer fraud and theft.
There was only one picture of Manning sentenced to prison: a grainy, black-and-white selfie, in which Manning sits in the driver's seat in the car. He wears a platinum-blossomed wig that became his hallmark.
Dewey-Hagborg had the hugely popular Stranger Visions project in 2015, which he carried out in 2012.
In it, he created portraits based on forensic information. He received DNA information, for example, about cigarette butts and chewing gubs, and created pictures of what the people in question could have looked like.
Then the artist received an e-mail from Paper Magazine.
“They were interviewing Chelsea Manning and needed a picture,” Dewey-Hagborg told a news conference.
You couldn't visit Manning and you couldn't photograph him in prison, so the newspaper contacted Chelsea and asked him if he would be interested in turning Manning into a DNA portrait.
Surprisingly, correspondences came from the artist and his imprisoned muse, writing to each other for a couple of years.
They even co-authored the Suppressed Images comic book, illustrated by Shoili Kanungo. It depicted a future in which the president would have mitigated Chelsea's verdict and he would be able to watch the exhibition.
In reality, Obama shortened Manning's verdict just a few days after the comic book's publication. It was a lucky coincidence after the seven tough years spent in the National Military Prison.
It's also impressive that the exhibition is about to be opened right now as Donald Trump is waging his own war against information leakers and transgender soldiers and on Twitter is coming up with more and more ways to reinforce the powerful divisions he actually had to mitigate.
Dewey-Hagborg believes that “everything has a purpose.”
On the wall of the gallery there is an artist’s statement, and under it Manning’s great pseudonym, where the surname ‘dot’ is the heart. In his statement, the artist calls for an end to the “automatic division on which gender, race, sexuality and culture have been based.”
On the opposite wall is Manning's DNA sequence: a long sequence of letters C, G and A written with a pencil.
With this combination of 200 letters – or a nucleotide, like the scientific definition used by the artist, Dewey-Hagborg wants to illustrate how similar we all are, at least from our biology.
‘I hope my exhibition will help people understand that we are equal in our genetic heritage and that genetic information is very personal and open to interpretations,” says Dewey-Hagborg. The exhibition also features his Spurious Memories video work from 2007.
‘DNA tells countless stories. There are 30 of them on display here.’
The last work of the exhibition is in its own room: a page from Manning and Dewey-Hagborg's comic book.
Manning's figure rises from prison like King Kong, holding a megaphone.
‘If they can shut you down, they’ve won,’ the picture reads.
‘Never be silent.’
“The text is from a letter I received from Manning,” says an artist from Philadelphia.
“It’s so impressive that I’m still getting chills.”
Contrary to what was described in the comic book, Manning has not yet seen the exhibition. When Manning arrives, he is greeted by a face based on his own genetic information like a group of demonstrators in the middle of the exhibition space.
It's intentional, like everything else in this exhibition: for example, Manning's DNA sequence is written with a pencil at a height that is the same as his length (157.5 cm).
“My face used to be on the wall, but I wanted to bring a sense of people into them,” Dewey-Hagborg says.
“I wanted to portray the mass movement created by Chelsea, which was also behind his liberation.”
Northwestern University professor and Oxford University employee on suspicion of murder
Police say they have launched a nationwide search to find Northwestern University's professor and an Oxford University employee suspected of stabbing a man to death in a luxury Chicago apartment.
It is clear from Cook County court documents that arrest warrants were issued on Monday for Wyndham Lathem, 42, and Andrew Warren, 56, who are suspected of involvement in Trenton H. James Cornell-Duranleau's death. Arresting orders have been handed down for first-degree murder without the possibility of being released on bail.
According to documents, Cornell-Duranleau, 26, died after being stabbed several times.
According to a local bulletin published by the Chicago Police Department, the body was found on 27.7.2017.
Officers who were alerted to the Grand Plaza apartment building found a victim with several cut wounds on their bodies.
Cornell-Duranleau was pronounced deceased at the scene and the stabbing is being investigated as a murder. Police are actively searching for two suspects.
Lathem has worked as an assistant professor of microbiology and immunology at Nortwestern University since 2007.
Alan K. Cubbage, the university's vice-rector of public relations, said in an e-mail statement that Lathem is now on administrative leave and has been denied access to Northwestern University campuses.
“This is a criminal case that the authorities are investigating, and Northwestern University is assisting with the investigation,” Cubbage wrote.
Warren works as a senior financial assistant at the University of Oxford.
According to an e-mail statement by a spokesperson to the University and Somerville College, “we have been in contact with police in the UK and we are ready to help authorities investigating the case in the United States in every possible way.”
In addition, the statement stated that Warren's colleagues are shocked and hoped he would surrender.
Anthony Guglielmi, the Chicago Police Department's Chief of Information, said he urged suspects to seriously surrender to the authorities.
“The police have an idea of their whereabouts, and we’re constantly clarifying our searches,” Guglielmi said.
According to Guglielm, local, regional and national authorities will assist in the search for suspects.
He said the police received a call from the manager of the Grand Plaza building, who had received a vague hint that something had happened in apartment 1008.
Police described the sight at the scene as horrific.
According to Guglielm, it is unclear whether the victim had been murdered on the same day or earlier.
He said security cameras revealed that Lathem and Warren had been in the building.
“We believe Professor Latham and the victim were having an affair,” Guglielmi says.
Grand Plaza’s management team released the following bulletin: “Police are currently investigating the course of events and the background of the victim, as well as possible motives, including the possibility of domestic violence.”
The man threatened to blow up an Islamic bookstore and "kill Muslims" in London
"I'm not an anti-Muslim, I'm Catholic," the condemned told police.
Moffatti must perform one hundred hours of unpaid social services.
The 39-year-old man was sentenced for his threats to blow up an Islamic bookstore in London and "kill all Muslims."
According to the Independent, David Moffatt was convicted of aggravated harassment, among other things.
According to the police, they were alerted by the bookstore's staff on May 23.
According to staff, the man had "threatened to blow up a bookstore and kill Muslims."
The suspect left the store after intimidations.
Moffatt was caught when he called the police for threats.
Threatening wasn't related to what happened in the bookstore
Authorities noticed that Moffatt was dressed in the same outfit as the suspect in the bookstore, a police spokesman said.
“I’m not an anti-Muslim, I’m Catholic,” Moffatt told the police during interrogations.
As a punishment, the man must perform 100 hours of unpaid social services and pay 620 pounds, i.e
According to Scotland Yard's spokesman, a number of hate crimes will not be reported.
‘We are taking action in accordance with WADA’s roadmap in the near future’ – Russian Deputy Prime Minister Vitaly Mutko
Russia's Deputy Prime Minister Vitaly Mutko has said that Russia will soon finalise the World Anti-Doping Agency (WADA's road map) measures to restore the powers of the Russian Anti-Doping Agency RUSADA.
‘Some of the roadmap measures have already been taken, and RUSADA was granted the right to design and test in cooperation with the UK Anti-Doping Commission (UKAD).
WADA’s road map has been updated in these respects,” Mutko said on Thursday.
The Deputy Prime Minister commented on the second part of WADA's road map, which defines the conditions for restoring RUSADA's powers. The second part of the roadmap was recently published on the organisation's website.
According to Mutko, it does not contain anything worrying from Russia's point of view.
“Everything has been announced, and everything will be implemented.
The results will be presented to the International Olympic Committee and WADA in the near future,” Mutko added.
However, one of the points on the roadmap requires the Russian anti-doping authorities to publicly approve the results of a study by WADA's Canadian lawyer Richard McLaren. McLaren is investigating the state's alleged contribution to Russian sports doping.
Mutko said: “The study is ongoing because our anti-doping system has admitted failure.
All measures have been taken.
There were no government programs, and we cannot admit what didn’t exist.”
Mutko's words contained echoes of the statement of Vitaly Smirnov, chairman of Russia's independent public anti-doping committee.
Speaking to the Russian RSport sports media earlier that day, Smirnov admitted that there have been problems with Russian anti-dopings. “We’ve said on several occasions that the report contains controversial statements and regulations.
“We’re not going to accept the report unconditionally,” he added.
WADA ordered RUSADA to stop doping control in Russia in November 2015, after the scope of the doping scandal had been revealed.
However, it received permission last June to plan and coordinate testing under the supervision of international experts and UKAD.
Olivier Niggli, the head of WADA, visited RUSADA’s headquarters in Moscow in July. After the visit, he stated that the organization was “on the right track” towards the restoration of its powers.
British organisation skeptical about the innovation beloved by Finns: "There is no evidence of the health benefits of maternity packages"
The British organisation doubts the benefits of Finnish-style maternity packages.
According to the BBC and the Guardian, Lullaby Trust, which fights cot deaths, prohibits the use of its own brochures in maternity packages distributed in the UK.
The distribution of maternity kits for all pregnant women will begin in Scotland this month.
Also in England, packaging is distributed in some areas.
According to Lullaby Trust, there is no evidence that the maternity kit would reduce the risk of cot death.
Cot death is the inexplicable sudden death of the baby.
Lullaby Trust acts as an expert on British health authorities in the field of the child's safe sleep.
We will no longer be allowed to package our official brochures on them because it would imply that we would recommend this product.
Finland has one of the world's lowest numbers of infant mortality rates, only two deceased per thousand births.
According to Lullaby Trust, Finland's good reading is the result of many different reasons, such as the low number of teenage pregnancies.
Giving a box for each family there might be one factor.
However, we can't say it for sure," said Francine Bates, CEO of Lullaby Trust.
We are concerned that maternal packaging is marketed as products that reduce infant mortality and cot deaths.
The Maternity Package is a Finnish invention, and it has been given free of charge to all mothers in Finland for 80 years.
The package includes a variety of accessories, such as children's clothing.
This year there are 53 products on the package.
The packaging itself is a cardboard box where the child can be put to sleep.
According to the Scottish government, the boxes it distributes meet all safety standards.
Lullaby Trust, on the other hand, says there is no safety standard for cardboard boxes to sleep.
According to Lullaby Trust, packaging may be the best option as a place to sleep if there is no cradle or cot available.
The organization recommends using the package only for a nap and to stop using it when the child is able to turn around.
The organization also recommends keeping pets away from the packaging and making sure it doesn't get wet or dirty.
Vikkelä's villain escaped from a Japanese zoo for the second time
The staff at the Japanese zoo are looking for a runaway turtle.
The giant turtle has surprised the staff with its speed and escaped for the second time in two weeks.
A turtle escaped from Shibukawa Zoo in Okayama, western Japan, on Tuesday morning.
The security cameras revealed that the villain left the zoo via the main entrance.
“It doesn’t die right away because it eats the grass around the zoo, but we’re very worried,” Yoshimi Yamane, who works at the zoo, said.
The 35-year-old villain who has escaped is female, 55 kilograms and is about one metre long.
Yamane describes the fugitive as quiet and gentle.
During the opening hours, the villain is allowed to walk freely in the kindergarten area.
The zoo has not received eyewitness sightings from the turtle.
Konna's last escape took place the other week.
Yamane found a fugitive walking along the road 150 meters from the zoo.
I noticed it on my way to the zoo.
“I stopped my car and asked my colleagues for help,” Yamane said.
It can walk faster than we could ever imagine.
These start-ups will help your business avoid Uber mistakes
Amy Errett wanted to measure employee satisfaction in an e-commerce start-up, but the surveys didn't work.
The responses were often vague, useless or even misleading.
Although he assured that the questionnaires were anonymous, some of the employees were suspicious of them.
‘The surveys were simply neither consistent nor objective,’ Errett says. As his hair care company, Madison Reed focuses on e-commerce. The company has 75 employees.
Errett decided to seek outside help.
There have been an increasing number of new types of HR startups entering the market to help other companies figure out how their employees are doing.
They develop and license software to measure employee engagement. Companies can conduct surveys and target specific teams and demographics, as well as provide their employees with anonymous advertising services and, in some cases, a direct connection beyond senior management to the board.
‘You can monitor your own health with apps like Strava and Fitbit, but where’s the company’s Fitbit?’ asks Jim Barnett, the founder and CEO of Glint start-up in Redwood City. Glint provides companies with analytics software to measure employee engagement.
Errett has been using Glint's software for three years. He says that he has gained a deeper insight into the thoughts and feelings of his employees.
The program can be used to make quick inquiries and votes to certain teams. In addition, you can create a map where you can see at a glance which departments employees are most dissatisfied and which managers have received the weakest estimates. Based on these, Errett may have drilled into the heart of the problems.
Companies have understood that they need to follow the workplace culture so that they don’t become the next Uber. There has been turmoil around a transportation company ever since a former employee wrote on their blog about a culture where employees are harassed and those who talk about problems are punished.
For startups like Glint, such omissions are an attractive business opportunity.
Corporate governance, regulatory and compliance are a global industry whose value could exceed US$118.7 billion by 2020, according to the Let’s Talk Payments website.
Convercent, based in the state of Denver Colorado, is a startup that helps companies spot and prevent inappropriate behavior. Interest in the company grew earlier this year when Uber's fame crashed.
Convercent has almost 600 customers, including Airbnb, Microsoft and Tesla.
It also recently entered into an agreement with Uber.
Like Glint, Convercent allows companies to conduct customized surveys, collect responses confidentially in real time, and identify problem areas.
In addition, employees can anonymously report inappropriate behavior by text message.
And if there is reason to suspect the manager of inappropriateness, complaints go directly to the board of the company.
“General opinion has overthrew the authorities,” said Patrick Quinlan, co-founder and CEO of Convercent.
He points out that if a company treats its employees badly or acts unethically, the consequences can be costly, even if the authorities do not intervene. Consumers can begin to boycott the company, and it can also lose employees and be sued.
With more than 25,000 employees at 500 outlets, the Ruby Tuesday restaurant chain has used Convercent for more than a year because it wants to ensure that employees are aware of the operating principles and procedures and can easily get in touch with their headquarters.
In the past, the practice was that if an employee wanted to report a problem, they first had to find out a phone number or email address that allowed him to make a notification to the head office. Then he had to make an official complaint and hope that it would be taken seriously.
“It was often a frightening and unpleasant experience,” said James Vitrano, General Counsel at Ruby Tuesday.
In the past, problems remained hidden from executives working in another city or state. Now, problems such as discrimination, harassment and unfair wages can be detected and dealt with more quickly.
‘I’m gaining a more holistic and comprehensive understanding of the employee’s reality,’ says Vitrano, who leads Ruby Tuesday’s risk management team.
‘At the same time, we protect our shareholders from employment-related litigation.’
According to Quinlan, companies began to take ethics, values, and employee engagement more seriously since audit firm Arthur Andersen collapsed as a result of unethical activity in connection with the Enron scandal.
It was only with social media that companies realized that they could no longer prevent the public investigation of their dirty laundry.
‘Hope was used as a strategy before the technology came under the control of ethics,’ Quinlan says.
Glint and Convercent offer their software as a service that is regularly charged.
It is a business model and opportunity approved by private equity investors. Both Glint and Convercent are backed up by venture capitalists.
Convercent raised US$10 million in funding in February from companies such as Sapphire Ventures and Tola Capital, which has raised a total of US$47 million.
Glint received 10 million euros from Bessemer Venture Partners in November and has raised a total of US$60 million in funding.
Investments are not a surprise, given the links between companies, corporate culture and venture capital.
According to a number of studies, employees expect more from their workplaces than before.
In a competitive market such as Silicon Valley, good salaries, and interesting projects are just the beginning.
Employees want to feel that they are accepted and valued, and that they give their time to a truly useful company.
“Employants are more committed to the company’s success if they feel like they’re listened to and that their work is rewarding,” said Nina McQueen, Executive Vice President for Remuneration and Welfare.
Investors receive returns when companies succeed. Companies succeed when employees commit.
Third-party analytics tools promise to strengthen employee engagement, so it’s no wonder they interest investors.
According to workplace culture experts, information about employee engagement is important,
but it is useless if no action is taken.
If the company requests feedback from its employees, it can be considered a sign of a change.
And if change doesn't come fast enough or nothing changes, disappointment can reduce employee engagement.
“Asking for four thousand proposals, you have to be prepared to have four thousand conversations,” said Russell Raath, CEO of Kotter International, a management consulting firm.
‘If the company does not take action, employees will begin to wonder if they were heard
And does their opinions matter.
If the company doesn’t care about them, why should they care about the company?”
According to Errett, Madison Reed is currently conducting staff surveys on a monthly basis and is able to tackle the problem on the same day as announced in Glint.
When Errett had gathered feedback on the slowness of decision-making and the quality of communication from frustrated employees, he was able to make changes to the teams and organize communication training to solve the problems.
Ruby Tuesday better understands its employees and hopes that a deeper understanding will help it improve the long-term persistence of employees in an industry known for high employee turnover.
‘If a company does not commit to creating a culture of transparency, it loses employees,’ Vitrano says.
“If it loses employees, it also loses customers.
“Without customers, there are no businesses.”
A 20-year-old man in Sweden is suspected of secretly photographing his naked female neighbor, according to news agency TT.
A woman living in Gothenburg had come away from the shower at her home when she noticed a cell phone hanging at the end of a rope outside her window.
