The woman who was frightened by the incident had run to the other room to get dressed.
When the woman had arrived to check the situation, the mobile phone had first been pulled up, but soon after that had been lowered to the window again.
A 20-year-old man suspected of a cover-up has pleaded that he photographed
However, he claimed that he only photographed the woman's face.
The video filmed on the phone had been removed, and the police had not managed to return it as evidence.
During interrogation, the man has pleaded guilty to the crime.
The man had justified his portrayal by saying that he loves his neighbor "absolutely crazy."
According to the news agency TT, the man will be prosecuted.
Teachers call for more money in the autumn negotiations
Wage development cannot be lower than, for example, the export industry, but the difference should be narrowed, said OAJ's Chairman Olli Luukkainen on Thursday.
Temporary cuts in public sector holiday bonuses highlight the difference, he points out.
We do not have the opportunity to make agreements in such a way that would not involve the holiday pay cuts carried out in some way.
In the municipal sector, agreements arise only when private sector papers have been signed and the increase percentages have been announced.
Luukkainen predicts that OAJ's terms of employment, for example, have been agreed in January.
Percentages were not yet revealed.
Luukkainen is also the chairman of Jukko's negotiating organisation for public sector educators.
Luukkainen: Holiday bonus cuts increase earnings-development differences
According to OAJ, statistics and forecasts show that the purchasing power of public sector wage and salary earners is declining, while the purchasing power of the private sector is increasing.
According to Luukkainen, a fixed-term holiday bonus cut will further sharpen earnings development differences.
In Finland, it is necessary to define how many pupils can be per teacher
OAJ also points out that the equality of basic education must be ensured, as the differences between those who succeed the best and the weakest are growing all the time.
According to the organisation, learning outcomes are declining, and differences between both pupils and regions are constantly growing.
Bruins players' day was extended as the NCAA reduced daily practice from two to one
It's almost eleven o'clock in the evening. A bunch of UCLA's American football players have been left to talk on the other side of the campus's new training grounds.
Some of them have taken off their jerseys, and their backs are shining on the sweat after the first day of the training camp.
Quite a few of them have this few-minute break.
After a three-hour rehearsal, the students of the university's summer courses return to the dormitory to study for the final exams. The next morning, they gather at half past seven at Wasserman's football center.
“Next three days will be tough for these guys,” Bruins coach Jim Mora said shortly before Monday.
The purpose of the change was to lighten the workload of the players, but it has lengthened their days – at least until Friday, when the last final exams will be held.
That's why UCLA's first three rehearsals started a quarter past seven in the evening. It was already difficult to find space in the players' full schedules.
Restricting the number of training sessions together per day means that there are more training days than before. The camp started almost a week earlier this year than last year, and this is the first time in the Westwood coaching season, which lasted for six seasons in Mora, when the training sessions are held at the same time as the summer courses.
Exercises are scheduled to take place 18 times before August 20, when the actual preparation for the opening game of the season will begin: UCLA will face Texas A&M University in September.
Mora says she supported the NCAA's rule change, even though it made the player's schedule even stricter.
“We’re burdening them very much for three or four days, and that’s very challenging,” Mora says.
“But the players are ready to work. They’re great friends.
I think it's going to get easier when we get to Saturday, and that's when we'll have more time.
UCLA closes the drills from outsiders after the camp. It’s not easy, given the direct view from the training fields to the adjacent parking area and the Luskin Center, a high-quality hotel that’s already been bombarded with footballs that have flown over the protective net behind the goal post.
Bruins players are supposed to move to a hotel this coming weekend, but Mora acknowledges that problems can arise during the season when the rooms are available to outsiders.
‘We’ve got security,’ Mora said.
“Worried is that people can book a room and shoot exercises for the video. It’s not an exaggerated concern.
A lot is paid for the video filmed from the exercises.
But we've got guards there and in the parking lot.
We'll do our best.
I won't make too much of my head.
“We have a plan.”
Freedom in Rwanda
There will be a presidential election in Central Afraic Rwanda on Friday, the victory of which is already considered clear
Paul Kagame is officially running for his third season, but in practice he's been influencing the country's leadership since 1994.
That's when the rebel group RPF stopped the genocide organized by extremist hutus.
Kagame was the commander of the RPF.
Kagame has been criticised, among other things, for crushing the opposition and restricting freedom of expression.
He has also been blamed, for example, for distorting poverty statistics.
Kagame will run for a third term because the constitution that limited the presidency to two terms was amended.
In Rwanda, however, Kagame is also a genuinely well-liked leader.
During his time in Rwanda, social reforms have been carried out and international investments have flowed into the country.
In addition, Kagame has played a major role in uniting a country divided by genocide.
Rwanda's success has prompted many Africans to ask whether liberal multi-party democracy is the right model for African states.
Kagame, however, has pledged more freedom for its people since the country was lifted to its feet.
It might be a good time to start.
Faces are battling fake news by intensifying fact-checking
Facebook plans to post potential fake news more and more often to independent Fact Reviewers, as well as presenting the results below the original article. The world’s largest social network announced on Thursday.
The company published a statement on its website stating that it will start using updated machine learning to identify fake news. Possible fake news will be sent to fact reviewers and the results may be published under the original article.
Facebook has been criticized for being one of the biggest distributors of fake news. Many people think that fake news influenced the outcome of the U.S. presidential election in 2016.
The topic has arisen in a political debate in Europe, where French voters were bombarded with fake news before the presidential election in May, and Germany, which is preparing for elections in September, supports the plan to fine social media networks if they do not quickly remove hostile content.
On Thursday, Facebook announced in a separate statement in Germany that a fact-checking would be tested in the United States, France, the Netherlands and Germany.
“People want to see which stories don’t pass through by independent inspectors. They also want more background information so that they can decide more enlightenedly what they’re reading and sharing,” Sara Su, the company’s product manager for the newsfeed, wrote on the company’s blog.
He added that Facebook will continue to test related articles and make other changes to its newsfeed to reduce fake news.
The Birth of a Comic Book: The Story of a Castle
The Little Scotney Castle in County Kent is now known from Squeeze's music video and Margaret Thatcher's hiding place from the 1970's and 1980's.
However, it has 700 years of turbulent history behind it. Now, in a picturesque location in Bewl's river valley, this castle is the subject of a comic book written by Jamie Rhodes, a moped-haired writter who likes old documents.
‘I think it’s great to be able to explore a text written by someone who died more than 300 years ago,’ Rhodes says with longing.
‘I feel a personal connection when I hold the letter in my hand.’
Rhodes's book, A Castle in England, tells the stories of Families who lived in Scotney from the Middle Ages to the Edvardian period, illustrated by a different artist, a promising British cartoonist: Isaac Lenkiewicz, Briony May Smith, William Exley, Becky Palmer or Isabel Greenberg.
This isn’t Rhodes’ first book based on archives. In 2014, Dead Men’s Teeth and Other Stories was published, whose sources of inspiration were documents from the British National Library. The project was emotional.
‘I came to tears when I noticed the thumbprint in the letter. The letter had been written by a double agent who had worked for jacobites in the 1700’s,” Rhodes says.
“It was his fingerprint.
And the letters smell of smoke, because at that time fire was used as lighting.
Three centuries later, the smell makes the reader feel like he's on the spot himself.
I love the archives!”
Attracted to old documents, the author was allowed to live in jail for almost four months with the support of the National Trust and the English National Arts Council, which seemed too good to be true. He admits that he couldn’t stay in the castle for a little longer than he would have hoped.
He spent the days exploring the Victorian ‘new’ castle, archives, treasures, and rarities, such as hyena heads hung on the walls and bottles that had been on the moat for centuries.
He toured the ruins and estates of an old castle and talked with forest rangers, gardeners and sometimes illustrative artists.
In the evenings, she ‘taked whiskey and began
Scotney's closer history is quite unknown, as the castle was transferred entirely to the National Trust Foundation only after the death of the last heir, Elizabeth Hussey, in 2006.
While Rhodes arrived about ten years later, the foundation's staff were only just about to start reviewing the archive. Rhodes describes that the archives had been held for hundreds of years using the same method: taking everything extra to the attic.
There were diaries in the archives, letters from the times of the English Empire, and maps of who owned what. ‘You’d imagine that land disputes are boring,’ Rhodes says. ‘Here, however, families even fought for one birch tree.
It reminded me that I should let it go.”
Some of the stories in the book proceed from the castle to the history of England, such as The Labourer. It tells the story of a man associated shortly after the completion of the castle with the English Peasant Rebellion in
‘Knightly Families of Kent and Sussex mentions that there was a riot at the castle in 1380.
“The peasant rebellion of England began in the same area, and it came to my mind that the rebellion can begin with a riot.
“Maybe these events are interconnected?”
The other stories in the book are closely related to the history of the castle – such as The Priest, the story of a priest whom the Darrell family hid for seven years during the English Reformation.
The Smuggler, on the other hand, tells the smuggler Arthur Darrell's story from the 17th century. He is said to have staged his own funeral by filling the coffin with stones (it was noticed years later when his tomb was opened).
“The men of the family seem to have been quite robbers. They constantly fell into debt and bought things they couldn’t afford.
Darrell’s men solved their debt problems by marrying wealthy old women. I see them as handsome guys who knew how to court the heiress.”
Now Rhodes is looking for new writing experiences.
He spent two weeks in Finland writing a comic book about Elämänmäki, a sanatorium that operated in the early 20th century, of which only an eutrophicated plot remains.
Scotney Castle has a special place in his heart.
“It was sad to leave there,” he said.
‘I didn’t want to go back to this damn life yet.’
Relations with Russia the Worst Since the Cold War
“The relationship between NATO and Russia has clearly deteriorated,” NATO Secretary-General Jens Stoltenberg said on Thursday.
Stoltenberg accused Russia and its position in the conflict in Ukraine.
“I think you can say that NATO’s relationship with Russia is now more difficult than it has ever been since the end of the Cold War,” Stoltenberg said in an interview with CNN.
After the end of the Cold War, we hoped that we could seek close cooperation with Russia.
But, especially after the illegal annexation of Crimea in 2014 and due to the continuing instability caused by Russia in the territory of eastern Ukraine, NATO's relationship with Russia has deteriorated considerably.
According to Stoltenberg, however, NATO is still committed to acting in such a way that tensions will not peak.
As long as we are strong and as long as we are predictable, we will be able to engage in a political dialogue with Russia to avoid the escalation of the situation and a new Cold War.
Prior to Stoltenberg's comments, U.S. President Donald Trump said that relations between the U.S.-Russia had even fallen dangerously poorly.
Trump urged to thank Congress for their bad relations, which last week approved new sanctions against Russia.
Vladimir Klitshko quits rematch against Anthony Joshua
Vladimir Klitshko, a long-time world heavyweight champion of professional boxing, ends his career.
Klitshko, 41, from Ukraine, announced his decision on Thursday morning.
Klitshko, who had won gold at the 1996 Atlanta Olympics, competed professionally 69 times, winninging 64.
He hosted various World Cup titles in 2000-02 and 2006-15.
Klitshko lost the last two games of his career.
Tyson Fury beat him with points in November 2015.
In April this year, Klitshko faced Anthony Joshua at Wembley Stadium and lost after a wild match by a knockout in the 11th round.
Joshua and Klitshko were expected to meet in a rematch in Las Vegas in November.
However, Klitshko did not find the motivation for the match.
I deliberately spent a few weeks making a decision to get enough distance from the Wembley match," Klitshko said in a statement.
As an amateur and professional boxer, I achieved everything I dreamed of, and now I want to start my second career after sports.
I could never have imagined that I had such a long and incredibly successful boxing career.
I am very grateful for that.
Check Klitschko's reasons for his decision in the video.
Klitshko's manager Bernd Bönte said in a press release that the decision to quit was difficult for Klitshko.
However, he has always emphasized that he wants to stop if he doesn't have enough motivation anymore.
That's why it's definitely the right decision," Bönte said.
Klitshko has been dating with American actress Hayden Panettiere since 2009.
The couple has one child, Kaya, who was born in 2014.
Klitshko's older brother Vitali is also a former world heavyweight champion.
In 2008-12, the brothers dominated World Championship titles of different alliances at the same time.
