Experienced ex-official blunted politicians on Yle: "We don't have room for distribution now or for many years"
Despite the positive atmosphere of the economy, Juha Sipilä's government must keep the ice in its hat and beware of inflating the state budget with debt money, warns Erkki Virtanen, who has been preparing budgets at the Ministry of Finance for decades.
According to him, there's no division.
In 2015, Yle interviewed Virtanen, who had retired in Wednesday's Ykkösaamu.
The construction of next year's state budget is about to begin.
Virtanen considers the recent politicians' proposals to increase the guarantee pension, lower the car tax and support e-bikes, among other things that have emerged.
They mainly indicate that the elections are starting to come close, because when ten per cent of the state budget is financed with additional debt, we have no room for distribution now or for many years.
I even think it's deceptive to talk abountanced when taking on debt," Virtanen told Yle.
Virtanen emphasized that all debt owes to children.
Virtanen has extensive experience in the preparation of state budgets: in 1978, he joined the Ministry of Treasury as a budget secretary and then worked as the deputy manager and budget manager of the Budget Department.
Most recently, before retiring, he served as Permanent Secretary at the Ministry of Economic Affairs and Employment.
Virtanen pointed out in an interview with Yle that economic growth of three per cent is likely to be temporary.
All forecasters estimate that this is a spike, from which we will go downwards already next year, and one per cent growth awaits Finland for tens of years.
Washington changes the rules of permanent residence
According to the media, President Donald Trump announced that the rules for a permanent residence permit, the Green Card, will be changed with a new immigration plan.
The aim is to reduce the number of legal immigrants to 50 000 per year, tighten the requirements for temporary work permits and restrict family visas.
New rules have been proposed by Republicans and are backed by the president.
The bill is to be debated in the two chambers of Congress.
“This law reduces poverty, raises wages and saves billions of dollars in taxpayer money by changing the way permanent residence permits are issued to citizens of other countries.
It will also reduce chain immigration and replace the current programme.
Applicants who are able to support themselves and have skills that will benefit our finances will benefit from the new application process.”
The Danish queen's spouse does not want to be buried in the same place as the Queen
Prince Henrik doesn't want to be buried with Queen Margaret.
In the picture, Prince Henry and Queen Margareta photographed in April.
Queen Margaret of Denmark, 77, and her husband Prince Henry, 83, will not be buried next to each other, according to the Danish court.
Traditionally, sovereign couples in Denmark are buried in the same place.
“The Royal House has announced today that Prince Henry does not want to be buried in Roskilde’s cathedral,” the court said in a statement on Thursday.
According to BT magazine, the reason for this is the prince's dissatisfaction with his role in the royal family.
“It’s no secret that the prince has been dissatisfied with his role and title in the Danish monarchy,” Lene Belleby, the court’s head of communications.
For the Prince, the decision not to be buried alongside the Queen is a natural consequence of not being treated on an equal footing with his spouse.
According to the court, the Queen has long been aware of the wish for her spouse's burial place and understands the decision.
Horror moment on the road: The car was to come on the highway
Tommi had a common-law partner and two small children on board.
They were not injured in the accident, but will not receive money from the insurance if the other party cannot be reached.
The accident took place at Keimolanportti in Vantaa.
Archives photo from the construction site of Kehä III.
Tommi from Vanta says his family miraculously survived a car accident on Tuesday.
Tommi, his cohabiting partner Riina and the couple's two young children drove on the highway at Keimolanportti in the direction of Hämeenlinna.
From Ring Road III junction to Hämeenlinnanväylä, a white car drove along the acceleration lane, which was to drive to the side of the family's car, Tommi says.
Without the evasive movement, the car would have come on with Tommi.
I braked and turned the car at the same time, and it got out of hand," Tommi says.
The family's car collided with the railing at high speed.
The parents and the two young children in the back seat were not injured.
One of the children is just under 3 years of age, the other is less than
Apparently it was pretty good luck when nothing happened to us.
None of the passers-by even drove towards us, even though there was busy traffic at the place.
The accident happened around 17:20 a.m.
According tommi, no one stopped at them, and the family called the emergency services.
They got out of the car on their own.
Ambulance and rescue personnel came to the scene, and the condition of the family was checked.
The car's ready for redemption.
Tommi says that he and the common-law partner have a small income, and they received a grant from the family to buy a car a couple of years ago.
According to Tommi, without the information of another car, the family will not receive any money from the insurance.
That's why the family wants eyewitnesses to make observations about the car, which is why the family drove into the railing.
We need a car every day.
It's difficult to take children to kindergartery and go to work without it," Tommi says.
Tommi saw that the car was a white, older car.
It was probably from the end of the 1990's or the early 2000's.
In his opinion, the beginning of the car's registration number was SIG.
If we can't catch the culprit, I don't know how we're doing financially, we'll have to count every centimeter.
Tommi hopes that those who saw or knew what happened are in contact with the police.
The Eastern Uusimaa Police Department and the Central Uusimaa Rescue Department confirm that they have had a mission at the scene of the accident on Tuesday evening.
The title and story were repaired at 21.15: Contrary to what was originally stated in the article, the second car didn't come from the oncoming lane according to Tommi, but drove from the acceleration lane to the side of the family's car.
Directed by Lotta Jäppinen, the performance deals with relationships through humour - Self-exhibitor will be seen at the ‘Which?’ theatre on Friday and Saturday
Lotta Jäppinen, who is from Kouvola, will bring her debut directing to the ‘‘‘What?’ theatre.
A self-exhibitor is a musical theatre performance about relationships and inadequacy that have gone wrong.
According to director Lotta Jäppinen, it still makes me feel good.
The topic is dealt with by means of humour.
You don't have bad feelings, he says.
According to Jäppinen, the touring summer theatre performance has been carried out with a tiny budget and a large, young heart.
The name of the performance challenges you to think about what a person reveals about themselves when getting to know a new person.
Is he perhaps pullinging a role, or is he approaching the other jacket?
An unployed presentation confuses fact and fiction
Jäppinen has both directed and dramatized the performance.
He's also responsible for the lights.
The presentation consists of the texts of the working group and there is no plot.
The director believes that even though the performance draws from the thoughts of young people, it speaks to people of all ages.
Jäppinen emphasises that Self-Exhibitor does not directly tell about his own relationships or the working group's relationships.
Fact and fiction are mixed.
I'm sure I'll convey my values in the presentation.
I don't think that relationships are bad, but I also don't think that a relationship, especially a relationship, is a prerequisite for happiness.
A lot of people learn from their divorced parents
Jäppinen believes that Self-Exhibitor draws a topical picture of young adults living in a hundred-year-old Finland.
According to him, many have learned a lot about relationships from their parents, many of whom are divorced.
At least you have to listen to yourself.
You don't have to stay in a bad relationship.
Minea Lång, Niklas Rautén and Otto Rokka will star in the performance.
The working group also consists of Costumer Ella Snellman.
The arrangement of the music is Jäppinen's and Rokka's.
Love for the theatre ignited in Kouvola
Lotta Jäppinen, 26, from Kouvola, is studying to direct theatre expression at the Arts Academy of Turku University of Applied Sciences.
It's been two years and there's another thing ahead.
Jäppinen's enthusiasm for theatre has begunned during the years in Kouvola.
The pupil of the music classes has been an assistant at the Kouvola Theatre and has acted, for example, at the Student Theatre in Helsinki and most recently at the Linna Theatre in Turku.
I've become more and more interested in directing.
The self-exhibitor premiered last week in Helsinki on the Narri stage.
The 45-minute performance will probably also be seen in the future, at least in Jäppinen's student city in Turku.
Self-exhibitor at Kouvola's What?-theatre 4.8. at 19 and 5.8. at 14 and 19.
The Finnish Olympic champion recovered for a year from a horrific motocross accident - the cervical spine was fractured
Few athletes in Finland have the same unstoppable force of nature as Janne Lahtela.
The ace traveled the world hundreds of days a year for twenty years and chased his dream of Olympic gold.
The dream came true in 2002, but Lahtela's movement continued.
He was not only a tireless trainee, but also an experimenter of new sports.
There aren't many forms of physical activity on the ground that Kemijärvi's people haven't tried at least once.
It's been inspired to practice.
There's always something new, Lahtela says.
In the end, one of the hobbies was to stop Lahtela completely
In 2014, Lahtela was involved in a motocross accident.
As a result, his cervicals and both hands were broken.
I didn't work anywhere.
I licked wounds at home for a year, Lahtela says.
Lahtela had bad luck.
He also admitted that age may have played a role in the accident.
Riding the bike may sound wild.
But I didn't use it to seek boundaries.
It's just such a hard sport that it's not worth doing too much in your 40s!
After the accident, Lahtela found himself in a special situation.
His busy life came to a halt.
It was a long time as the head coach of Japan's freestyle national team.
Lahtela started in the position immediately after the end of his own career in 2006.
For the first time since I was 15 years old, I had to be still.
I didn't really do much," Lahtela says.
Even during his coaching, Lahtela often challenged his protégé on the slopes and physics tests.
Surprisingly, stopping was a good experience.
I was physically and mentally so tired that my own thoughts didn't make sense either.
Not even among the species.
It'd be good to stand still.
I really enjoyed it, because I actually just got to be for once.
Lahtela was inspired to become Finland's head coach.
Without stopping, he's afraid he'll be burned out.
It's easier to find places in the body that's broken than intact!
Without the accident, I wouldn't have realized that I needed so much rest.
Lahtela started as the coach of Finland's freestyle national team about a year ago.
At the same time, he found a safer hobby to replace the pace sports.
I switched motocrosses to disc golf!
It got out of hand right away.
At worst, I spent seven hours a day throwing.
Even though Lahtela has always been a super enthusiastic trainer himself, he doesn't insist that his trainees are similar to him.
I don't force everyone into the same mold.
In our culture, there is room to be your own and ways to become the best in the world.
Freestyle is a free style, and the mentality has always been in line with it, Lahtela says.
Sex robots are coming and we have to worry
It's here: a report on the development of technology in the sex robot industry – just as it's starting to feel like 2017 couldn't get any weirder.
Oh, yeah, yeah, yeah.
Sex robots are a big deal now.
The Foundation for Responsible Robotics (FRR) recently published a report on the development of sex robots within the next 5 to 10 years.
‘We need to take the prevalence of sex robots seriously,’ said Noel Sharkey, a founding member of the foundation and professor emeritus of robotics and artificial intelligence at the University of Sheffield.
Clients can choose the color of the robot's eyes, the shape of their nipples and the type of fingernails.
‘Sex robots are giggling, but they’re already selling quite a lot, and we’re going to see more of them,’ Sharkey said at the report’s announcement.
Sex dolls have evolved from inflatable dolls to robots that have warm silicone skin, as well as features created with artificial intelligence that resemble human use.
The user can also adapt the robot's own preferences from the color of the eyes to the shape of the nipples and the type and color of the fingernails.
What's even more worrying is that robots have a ‘personality’ that makes Stepford’s women look like progressive freedom fighters.
Sex dolls have evolved into robots with silicone skin.
For example, RealBotix robot's character traits can be adapted to the wearer's preferences, such as shy.
True Companion's Roxxxxy Gold robots have a pre-programmed personality: Frigid Farrah seems shy, Wild Wendy is adventurous and so on.
‘Roxxxy Gold’s personality adapts as perfectly as possible to the personality of the user,’ the company’s website advertises.
In other words, the robot likes the same things as the user.
It also has different moods:
It can be, for example, tired, in the mood of the conversation, or “in that mood.”
There are many worrying features in the generalization of sex robots. They replace genuine relationships, but it is even more dangerous to subjugate women and outright rape fantasies, as the user approaches, for example, a robot programmed to be shy and resilient.
True Companion compares the Roxxxy robot to the vibrator on its website:
‘Women have vibrators. Why shouldn’t men have a sex robot?’
However, sex robots aren't just a fetish or a new kind of sex toy.
Their generalization and development speaks of something darker and deeper in our culture: a recession from gender equality towards sexual interaction, where subjugation is one option among others.
Of course, the majority of men do not acquire a sex robot, and their popularity is still marginal, although the FRR report indicated their growing popularity.
Marginal phenomena, however, tell a lot about the spirit of the time.
And a lot of people don't start behaving strangely or offensively until the market allows it.
It's no coincidence that sex robots have begun to become more widespread while women's rights are threatened around the world and a president is sitting in the White House who has bragged about harassing women.
In the television series The Handmaid’s Tale (Orjattarsi) isn’t the most worrying of its powerful imagery, such as images of hanged bodies and excavated eyes. The most disturbing thing is how realistic the world of the series feels.
The republic of Gilead is far from our reality, but right now it seems to be a surprisingly short trip.
They try to turn sex robots as much like real women (in the proportions of porn stars) as is technically possible.
The developers of the robots want their skin to feel genuine and the movements mimic human movements as well as possible.
However, their ‘personalities’ are far from reality.
A robot can't leave a relationship or a situation.
They have neither their own will nor history – nothing that refers to independence or violate the fantasy of complete submission.
They're pleasant servants.
Slaves.
Police Suspect: Two Terrorist Acts Were Planned in Australia, Led by ISIS chief
The chief of ISIS led a group of Australian men suspected of planning to take the bomb to an Etihad Airways aircraft.
According to the Sydney Morning Herald, one of ISIS' leading figures had also sent parts of a bomb to Sydney.
According to suspicions, the bomb was supposed to be taken on a plane on July 15, but the attempt was interrupted before the security check.
The police did not specify why the company was interrupted.
Another plan of suspected terrorists was to build a device that secretes toxic colourless gas and take the device to a crowded public place, such as public transport.
Michael Phelan, Australia's Federal Deputy Commissioner, said the terrorists' plan was one of the most professional plans ever made in Australia.
Due to suspected terrorist plans, four men were arrested in police raids on Saturday.
One of the men has been released, but his interrogations will also continue.
The good side of Trump?
Make Mitt Romney look like a paragon of common sense
During Trump's reign, it's already been repeated to the point of getting bored that the strong dichotomy of voters has also broken up families on a daily level.
Around Christmas and Thanksgiving, American newspaper counselling forums showed how to get along with relatives who voted for Trump.
We all want to burst our information bubble.
Less attention has been paid to Trump's ability to unite families.
I'll tell you about this because my cousin visited me this week in New York. He's a left-wing poet living in Chicago who's been the buttons with our family's Republicans for a long time.
He recalled that a common enemy can have a powerful unifying effect.
Now that Trump is in the White House, all of my cousins – including the Republican sibling, with whom he’s been arguing about politics for decades – are suddenly and surprisingly on the same side.
It's a good thing at the dinner table, but more broadly, it involves significant risks.
Trump has given the left a new boost, but the distorting effect of his presidency could actually take us all to the right.
When we talk about Trump and ‘normalization’, we’re talking about the frightening possibility that one day we won’t be able to be horrified by his tricks.
In the long term, there's an even more terrible chance that Trump will normalize real-leaded Republicans, which, compared to Trump, suddenly seem quite reasonable.
I'm wistfully reminiscing Mitt Romney's subdued version of madness: a dog on the roof of a car, an income tax of 14% (and we've seen his tax returns). Compared to Trump, they seem like very mild violations.
When Trump's term is over, Republicans' next presidential candidate will only have to be lucid, and it immediately feels like a considerable improvement.
While my cousin was visiting me, we took our children to Central Park's carousel. It's the biggest carousel in New York's park department and the best you can get in New York for three dollars without caffeine.
Carousel's horses are huge, the characters in the middle – grotesque clowns and animals looking back in horror – are ominous, and the screaming music in the background is strangely captivating.
Many of New York's hallmarks stagnant over time into disappointments: the views from the tall buildings are starting to bore you, the city's silhouette becomes too familiar, and the city's reality doesn't match your dreams.
And I still haven't, after 35 years, been completely able to get over the fact that Swiss Cottage isn't a Swiss cottage, it's a big roundabout in North London. Ye Olde Swiss Cottage is a Swiss-inspired pub, but it doesn't count.
On the other hand, the Central Park carousel is still a strangely magical experience – even to the extent that two two-year-olds, one eleven-year-old and two forty-year-old women enjoy it immensely.
I would've given the carousel a full score if someone had asked for my assessment, but luckily no one came to ask questions.
On the other hand, I was askeded at the department store this week to rate my “payment experience” on a scale of one to five stars before I put my credit card on the editor.
It's the same thing in the café in my neighbourhood, but they also give me the opportunity to combine the 15 per cent service allowance when I'm picking up the coffee at the counter.
This slows down everyday interactions and pressures the customer service representative to put their personality on the game in a situation where it is not needed.
The department store's saleswoman smiled with an apologies when I tirelessly gave my credit card experience five stars.
Shefki Kuqi was fired from Inter - ‘There was no management confidence’
FC Inter have today relieved Shefki Kuq of his duties as the club's representative team's coach.
Fabrizio Piccareta, who previously served as assistant coach, will take the coaching responsibility.
The rest of the coaching group will continue in its current duties.
Piccareta has previously worked as an auxiliary coach in Sunderland.
