Red meat, dairy products and chocolate may relieve irritated bowel syndrome
Red meat and chocolate may sound like nightmares of the digestive system.
However, a recent study suggests the opposite: a diet rich in meat protein and dairy may ease irritated bowel syndrome.
Irritated bowel syndrome is an incurable disease that affects approximately 1.3 million people in the United States and 300 000 people in the United Kingdom. It causes stomach cramps, bloating, recurrent or bloody diarrhea, weight loss and extreme fatigue.
Irritated bowel syndrome sufferers are usually advised to avoid meat and dairy products. However, researchers at Washington University found that the essential amino acid tryptophan contained in the protein helps the body develop immune cells that strengthen intestinal tolerance.
According to Marco Colonna, director of the study, immune cells guard the intestine and ensure that harmful microbes that are hiding in food cannot enter the body.
Inflammatory cells are balanced by cells that strengthen tolerance. They protect the body without harming sensitive tissues.
If the balance drops too close to inflammation, irritated bowel syndrome may occur.
The experiments found that there are immune cells that strengthen tolerance in mice whose intestines contain certain bacteria.
Tryptophan is one of the building blocks of protein.
‘We found a link between the development of Lactobacillus reuteri bacteria and cells that promote tolerancy. Lactobacillus reuteri is part of the normal microbiome of the intestine,” says Professor Colonna.
“The more tryptophan there was in mice’s diet, the more they had such immune cells.”
If the same works in humans, the combination of Lactobacillus reuteri bacteria and a diet rich in tryptophan can strengthen intestinal tolerance and reduce inflammatory susceptibility.
While researching immunity-boosting immune cells, Postdoctoral Researcher Luisa Cervantes-Barragan found that although one group of mice had such cells, they did not exist in mice of the same monic species that were kept separate from the second group.
He suspected that the difference was related to microbiomes in the intestines of mice, i.e
When the intestinal DNA of mice was examined, six species of bacteria were detected in the mice that had immune cells, but not other mice.
When Lactobacillus reuteri bacteria were given to mice grown in a sterile environment who did not have a gut microbiome and did not develop immune cells, immune cells began to appear.
Researchers wanted to find out how bacteria affect the immune system. They bred Lactobacillus reuteri bacteria in the fluid and transferred small amounts of liquid – without bacteria – from mice to immature immune cells.
Immune cells evolved into tolerance-boosting cells.
When the active ingredient was purified from the liquid, it turned out to be a by-product of tryptophan metabolism, known as indol-3-lactic acid.
When researchers doubled the amount of tryptophan in mice's diet, the number of tolerance-boosting cells increased by about 50 percent. When tryptophan was halved, the number of cells decreased by half.
According to Cervantes-Barragan, humans have the same tolerability-boosting cells as the mouse, and most of us have Lactobacillus reuteri bacteria in our digestive tract.
It is not yet known whether tryptophan by-products of Lactobacillus reuteri allow cells to develop in humans in the same way as in mice. However, tryptophan-related genetic defects have been observed in patients with irritable bowel syndrome.
“We probably want to promote the development of such cells because they regulate inflammation on the inner surfaces of the intestines,” says Cervantes-Barragan.
‘Highy tryptophan levels combined with Lactobacillus reuteri may contribute to their proliferation.’
Jose Mourinho said Manchester United had a ‘nero’ after signing with Nemanja Matic
Jose Mourinho believes Manchester United got a ‘nero’ after signing Nemanya Matic, a midfielder who made his Manchester United debut in a match against Sampdoria on Wednesday night.
Matic, who moved from Chelsea for 40 million pounds, started with a 2–1 win in Dublin, United's last-season pre-season match before the UEFA Super Cup match against Real Madrid on Tuesday. He impressed as a defensive midfielder against stars like Paul Pogba and Andreas Pereira.
However, Matic wasn't in the best shape yet because he wasn't on the blue-shirted Asian tour before the start of the season. He only fared on the field for 45 minutes – according to Mourinho, there has to be a change before the Premier League's season starts.
“He needs time, but his experience, intelligence and way of thinking make him a genius – he has a naturative ability to think about football,” Mourinho said.
“We’re going to help him to be ready sooner rather than later.”
In addition to Matic, newcomers Victor Lindelof and Romelu Lukaku were also starting a game at the Aviva Stadium when Mourinho began to refine his starting line-up for the start of the season.
The Independent revealed this week that Mourinho wants three more players, even though the club has already done a “great job” by acquiring three of his four players.
‘Three out of four – you have to thank the club for that,’ he added.
‘I’ll be happier if I get all four, but they did an excellent job.’
Volleyball women bullied Germany for two rounds
Finland's women's volleyball national team lost their fourth match of the World Cup qualifying tournament to Germany 0-3 (23-25, 23-25, 14-25).
Germany's routine level in gaming is high.
Their game was steady in every sector, Finland's head coach Tapio Kangasniemi said in a press release.
In the first two rounds, Finland kept their opponents tight, but in the third period Germany was clearly stronger.
Germany leads the group.
When we played patiently and in certain places aggressively enough, we were able to rock along, Kangasniemi said.
Finland's most efficient was Piia Korhonen, who collected 14 points.
Finland, who have lost three matches out of four, will still face Slovenia on Sunday.
Reuters: Major jury has already sent challenges in connection with Trump Jr.'s meeting on Russia
In the United States, a major comprehension has sent challenges related to the meeting between Donald Trump Jr. and Russian lawyer Natalia Veselnitskaya.
A major jury convened by Special Prosecutor Robert Mueller has already sent challenges related to the meeting between Donald Trump Jr. and Natalia Veselnitskaya, a Russian lawyer known for the Kremlin.
According to Reuters news agency, which also confirms information revealed by the Wall Street Jaury earlier on Thursday about the convenation of a grand jury.
According to Reuters, a major jury has sent challenges related to a meeting between Trump Jr. and Veselnitskaya, known for Kremlin, which took place in the middle of the U.S. presidential election in June 2016.
Paul Manafort, Donald Sr's then campaign chief and his son-in-law Jared Kushner were also present.
According to Reuters sources, the fact that Special Attorney Robert Mueller has convened a major jury in Washington means an intensification of the pace of the Russia investigation.
Mueller wanted a major jury to try to investigate Russia's interference in the U.S. presidential election and whether Trump's campaign or his associates cooperated with Russia.
The grand jury extends Mueller's rights to collect evidence, send challenges and force people to testify on a sword.
The great of the jurors is a group of ordinary citizens who work behind closed doors.
A major jury weighs the evidence investigated by the prosecutor in the investigation and decides whether charges should be prosecuted.
The British inventor microchipped himself in order to get to his home and his car with a swirl
The British investigator is the first in the country to microchip himself so that he can open the front door of his home, get to his office and start his car with a waving of his hand.
The microchip is between 33-year-old Steven Northam's thumb and index finger, and now he's offering a service to companies and individuals who want to make their lives easier with Northam's BioTeq technology.
Northam collaborates with Geoff Watson, a consulting anaesthetist at Royal Hampshire County Hospital in Winchester, Hantster, to ensure the installation of a microchip meets medical requirements.
The microchip uses the same technique as microchips for cats and dogs, and it only takes 30 seconds to install.
Northam's company is also converting the locks of houses and cars to be compatible with the microchip. Handling one lock costs 300 pounds.
Northam is married and has one child. He has installed microchip technology both in his home and in his BMW Z4, which kicks in when he puts his hand on the steering wheel.
The White House exhibit paid thousands of dollars to taxpayers
Last month's decision by the White House to flight a naval helicopter to the White House's southern lawn for an event presenting American production was to cost taxpayers US$24 000, according to military documents obtained by TIME.
The green-and-white Sikorsky VH-3D helicopter is referred to as Marine One when the president is on board. It was an attraction at an event held at the White House on 17.7.2017, where American construction programs were presented.
President Donald Trump, Vice President Mike Pence and a group of White House officials familiarized themselves with products from all 50 states.
In addition to a helicopter manufactured in Connecticut, a sailboat from Maine, a fire truck from Wisconsin and a forklift from Mississippi were on display.
The White House military office had requested the location of the helicopter only about 36 hours before it landed on the southern lawn for an “exceptional” event, according to documents from the Navy’s HMX-1 helicopter squadron.
‘I just wanted to make sure that you were aware of it, because it’s an extraordinary event that gets a lot of visibility,’ the commander of the HMX-1 wrote in an e-mail the day before the event to his superior, the deputy commander of the navy aircraft.
According to the message, the helicopter would arrive at the southern lawn on 17.7. at seven o'clock in the morning and the event would start at three in the afternoon. The helicopter would leave the scene only after the lawn was emptied between six and nine in the evening.
In addition, it was mentioned in the message that if the helicopter's departure from the White House were to be delayed, another crew would be needed due to restrictions on service hours.
The original crew was in the helicopter during the incident, and there were unit security personnel at the helicopter's house when it was on the ground.
It is not clear from the documents obtained by TIME whether another crew was eventually needed.
U.S. Hatch Act prohibits federal employees from using state property for political purposes.
According to the documentation, HMX-1 inquired whether the event violated the law.
“One of our questions was whether this violated the Hatch Act. We were told not to break,” the commander wrote.
‘Sounds like you can take part,’ replied the assistant.
Made in America was an official event of the White House, so it wasn't covered by the Hatch Act.
But even official events have political tones.
At the event, the president spoke in favour of the health care reform that was discussed by the Senate at the time, as well as looser state regulation.
Using the Marine One helicopter and Air Force One aircraft for political purposes is not a new phenomenon.
Presidents must use them on their voyages,
but their use only for photographs is unusual.
Trump's actions attracted attention in February when Air Force One rolled into the background of the president's campaign event in the state of Florida. Trump has strongly criticized the use of his predecessor's presidential aircraft on Twitter.
“The White House is very pleased with the Made in America event, which showcased products made by American workers from all 50 states,” White House Press Secretary Lindsay Walters said in a statement.
HMX-1 unit crews regularly make training flights in the Washington area and train on the southern lawn to bring the president to the White House with centimeters of accuracy.
At the G-20 summit in Germany in early July, HMX-1 conducted 58 training sessions for the White House.
According to one clerk, the flights to Made in America were part of the crew's monthly training quota.
On that day, the unit’s helicopters were also flown for 11 hours of “non-presidential support missions.”
According to the White House, there were no other costs for the taxpayer.
Sikorsky's spokesman said the company did not participate in organising the helicopter's presentation on the White House's southern lawn.
HMX-1 does not have any records of flights related to presidential duties, and it is difficult to accurately estimate the costs of the transaction.
According to the Ministry of Defence's hourly allowances for 2017, the VH-3D helicopter's replacement level is US$24,380 per flight hour.
According to HMX-1 documents, flights from Quantico's basis from the state of Virginia to the White House took a total of an hour.
According to the White House, the operating costs of the helicopter are clearly lower.
‘Calculating the costs to taxpayers according to the level of compensation is misleading,’ Walters says.
‘The estimated level of compensation shall include staff, maintenance and many other so-called submerged costs taken into account in the annual appropriations.’
According to a study carried out by the RAND research company in 2015, the compensation levels do not include personnel costs, but they take into account fuel, maintenance and contract costs.
At the time of the incident, Sean Spicer, a former White House press secretary, defensed the use of a helicopter at the event.
‘The idea is to introduce American production,’ Spicer said.
“I know Sikorsky and the state of Connecticut are very proud that they are involved in ensuring national security and are probably employing hundreds or thousands of people.
And I think that, like most Americans, we pride ourselves on the helicopter and other military equipment that many Americans have worked hard on.
It’s perfectly appropriate to point it out.”
It's not certain whether Trump visited the helicopter during the incident, but at least he sat down at the fire truck driver's seat.
In the White House, Trump joked to the press about his Sikorsky squadron.
‘I know Sikorsky very well,’ the president said. ‘I’ve got three.’
Lightning hit the Vaasa track - the trains have to be replaced by buses
Passenger train traffic between Seinäjoki and Vaasa has been interrupted due to a technical fault, says the Rail Traffic Centre.
Trains will be replaced by buses for now.
Traffic control system failure was caused by thunder.
In the morning, the Railway Centre did not yet have an estimate of the duration of the repair.
Venezuela's State Prosecutor Demands Right to Prevent the Appointment of the Country's New Representative Council
Venezuela's State Prosecutor has filed a tribunal's decision that would prevent the appointment of the country's new Representative Council.
Venezuela's President Nicolas Maduro has announced that last Sunday's vote is expected to be elected on Friday.
In a controversial vote, a council of representatives was elected, which may, among other things, change the country's constitution.
According to Chief Prosecutor Luisa Ortega, the blocking decision has been requested due to suspicions of election fraud.
Ortega has also opened an election fraud investigation into the vote.
He nominated two judges for the position after the British company, which held the Representative Council vote, said the results had been manipulated.
According to the company, the number of voters reported by the Venezuelan authorities was about one million higher than the number of actual voters.
Only Maduro's allies were nominated, and Maduro's son and wife, among others, have been nominated in the 545-member council.
In addition to changing the constitution, the Council of Representatives can dismantle opposition-led congress and change laws.
Maduro described as a dictator
Maduro's actions are widely condemned.
The opposition sees the constitutionalization of Maduro as a way to strengthen its position.
The United States has imposed sanctions on Maduro and called him a dictator.
The European Union, the United Kingdom, Mexico, Colombia and Argentina have announced that they will not recognise the new Representative Council.
Maduro argues that the new council is a solution to the country's political and economic crisis.
Unrest has been going on in Venezuela for months.
Numerous people have been wounded and more than a hundred have been killed.
Venezuela fell into an economic crisis as oil prices dropped.
Now a lot of people can't afford food or medicine.
The opposition is also expected to protest on Friday, when the new Representative Council is scheduled to begin.
A Russian man extradited from Finland was sentenced to 4 years in prison in the United States
On Thursday, a Minneapolis tribunary in the United States sentenced 41-year-old Maksim Senah to nearly four years in jail for involvement in a global computer scam.
Senah was detained in Finland in August 2015 as a result of a
According to the Russian newspaper Fontanka, Senah had been on holiday in Finland.
He was arrested by Finnish border authorities when he was about to return home.
Finland extradited Senah to U.S. judicial authorities in February 2016 despite Russian protests, AFP news agency reported.
In March of this year, he was found guilty of computer fraud with the benefit of millions of dollars.
Senah was jailed for 46 months.
According to the U.S. Department of Justice, Senah will be expelled after serving a prison sentence.
The Russian Foreign Ministry harshly criticized Finland after the arrest and said that the man should not be extradited to the United States.
In the statement, Finland and the United States were criticised that the arrest was made in Finland without Russia knowing about the matter in advance.
According to Prosecutors, Senah had used a computer virus called Ebury, which infected tens of thousands of computers around the world.
With the help of the virus, usernames and passwords were fished.
Scammers then used the information to further control internet traffic, which was used to carry out click scams and spread spam.
Kenneth Blanco, acting director of the Justice Department's Crimes Division, says the incident shows that the U.S. is also capable of chasing cybercriminals operating in others.
“We have the ability and willingness to identify, find and bring them to justice,” Blanco said.
Edited at 20:58: Fixed the transcription of the name of the sentenced person.
Narco's third-season trailer: Pablo Escobar is out of the picture, so the Cali cartel takes the lead in Netflix's hit series
Even though Pablo Escobar's killer was revealed in the second season of Narcos, the Netflix series is definitely not over.
The streaming service has already confirmed that the third and fourth seasons of the series will air. The trailer for the third season has been unveiled and will present the new villains of the series.
In the third season, the U.S. narcotics police (DEA) has turned its attention to the Cali cartel, the world's richest drug trafficking organization.
The organization, led by four godfathers, operates “like the Fortune 500 company,” except that there’s a little more bribery and violence from the authorities.
Gilberto Rodriguez Orejuela (Damian Alcazar) leads the cartel, and Miguel Rodriguez Orejuela (Francisco Denis) is its brain. Pacho Herrera (Alberto Ammann) manages connections to Mexico, and Chepe Santacruz Londono (Pepe Rapazote) operates in New York.
In a recently unveiled trailer, Pedro Pascal makes a comeback as Javier Peñana, recruiting U.S. and Colombian cops to the war on drugs.
Matias Varela, Michael Stahl-David, Matt Whelan, Miguel Angel Silvestre, Kerry Bishe and Arturo Castro.
Narcos' third season begins on September 1st.
Kari Kola implements an exceptional work of light art in Paris
Joensuu-based lighting artist Kari Kola (pictured) will produce an exceptional work of light to celebrate the first Day of Light appointed by the UN in Paris next year on 16 May.
Kola makes a light artwork called Day and Night in Paris.
The themes of the work are light and darkness - day and night - the sun and the moon.
The work illuminates the UNESCO Headquarters Fontenoy building and the Globe monument in front of it.
In addition to light, I want to emphasize the darkness.
Today, the light pollution of millions of cities is a significant problem that affects the well-being of all of us.
With the help of my art, I can make the things that I feel are important visible in a concrete way, says lighting artist Kari Kola about her work.
I am well honored by the invitation to participate in the first International Day of the Day of Light and to bring light art as part of the theme day, Kola continues.
Kari Kola participated in the opening ceremony of the Year of Light in 2015, when he illuminated UNESCO Headquarters.
I feel very privileged to be able to recreate my art at the UNESCO Headquarters, Kari Kola says.
The work may yet expand significantly.
This will be communicated later in the coming autumn, Kola continues.
The aim of the International Day of Light is to highlight the importance of light for humanity and to increase people's awareness of the fields of science, art, culture and sustainable development.
The first Day of the Light will be celebrated at Unesco's headquarters in the center of Paris on 16 May.
Two men fought in shopping centre's parking garage in Espoo
Two men had a quarrel in the shopping center Iso Omena's parking garage in Espoo on Wednesday morning.
According to the parties, the dispute was sparked when the other party accidentally drove in a prohibited direction of travel.
There was a verbal quarrel between the drivers.
The driver, who was driving in a prohibited direction, sweaped at the driver of the other car, as a result of which his car took off, colliding with the batter's car.
He then knocked the car into the hood.
The man has admitted that he caused the damage and wronged him.
Timo Soini's article: "After that, there was a flood"
Minister for Foreign Affairs Timo Soini published a blog post on Thursday, titled with a question mark.
The writing is short, only two sentences long.
In my time, the party grew from zero to the governing party of thirty-seven MPs.
After that there was a flood, now everything is floating, Soini writes.
Soini's text contains references to 17th-century France.
King Louis XV or his mistress Madame de Pompadour are known to have used the Flood parable.
Depending on the interpretation, the books of history have been left with the phrases "The Flood after us" or "the Flood after me".
According to the United Nations, democracy in Afghanistan depends on the political role of women
The elections will only be representative if all members of society, including women, were able to vote in them, the head of the U.N. embassy in Afghans said on Wednesday.
Nicholas Haysom, the interim head of the U.N. relief embassy, stated that hundreds of women will be nominated in provincial council elections, three women will run for vice president, and more than 30 percent of newly registered voters will be women.
“The role of women cannot be overemphasized,” Haysom said in a statement on Wednesday.
Elections can only be genuinely representative and credible if women participate fully in them and are involved in all parts of the electoral process.
Afghan Provincial Council Council and presidential elections will be held on 5.4.2017.
Threats have been made by the Taliban at the same time as international forces are moving into an advisory role.
This year's elections are the first time when power is peacefully transferred from one democratically elected government to another.
Haysom said the women's contribution will strengthen Afghan democracy.
According to Afghanistan's Independent Election Commission, 1.2 million of the 3.6 million registered voters are women.
Friday is rainy and windy
Friday will bring plenty of rain to the south.
In the Helsinki metropolitan area, the weather is rainy and quite windy.
In the afternoon, the cloud cover cracks and can develop thunderous showers.
It'll be clearer and cleaner tonight.
The weather front will bring heavy precipitation to the southern and central parts of the country, thundery may also occur in the south.
In the evening in the southearth it is southwest windy and clearer, a few rain showers may still come.
The afternoon temperature in the rain is about fifteen, on the south-west coast when the more uniform rain has stopped close to twenty degrees.
In the north, cloudiness varies and there will be local afternoon showers.
During Friday and Saturday, low pressure dominates the weather in the southern and central parts of the country, and rains are common.
In the north, the rains will be minimal.
On Saturday in the southern and central parts of the country it rains in many places, thundress also occurs.
The rain also extends to parts of Kainuu.
In the north, cloudiness is abundant in the afternoon and there will be individual showers.
On the southern coast there is wind from the southwest, in the central and northern parts of the country the airflow is on the eastern side.
On the north-eastern side of the British Isles, there is a strong low-pressure, with the associated weather front bringing heavy precipitation to the southern part of Fennoscandia.
In western and central Europe, there is a western southwestern airflow and there will be some showers.
In southern Europe, the weather is sunny and hot, and the temperature rises above 40°C in places.
The easternmost part of the Mediterranean has thundery showers.
Flooding certain kinds of tourists a threat to the region's culture
Authorities are considering limiting the number of tourists in Venice.
The rage of the locals is particularly focused on the form of tourism represented by cruise ships.
Italy's Minister of Culture calls it "eat and flee" tourism: it's only a few hours at the destination and we're rushing through the sights in large numbers.
Venice is suffocated by quick day trips to tourists to the city.
It is difficult to get around the narrow streets and the fine squares are so crowded with visitors that the inhabitants of Venice can no longer fit in.
They have been forced to move further and further away from the historic center, while homes have been replaced.
The New York Times and the Guardian write about the problems of tourism in Venice.
Authorities are considering restrictions on the number of tourists.
They call the current phenomenon low-quality tourism - low-quality tourism.
When you arrive on a large cruise ship, two or three hours are given.
“You’re following the flag-holding guide from Piazzale Roma to Rialto Bridge and St. Markus’s market and you’ll turn back,” Italian Culture Minister Dario Franceschini said.
The beauty of Italian cities lies not only in architecture, but also in the activities of place, shops and workshops.
We must preserve this part of the identity of the cities.
St. Mark's Square is also one of the most popular places to visit in Venice's historic centre.
On the edge of the square lies the Church of St. Markus.
The indignation of the locals is mainly directed at cruise ships.
There are only 50 000 locals living in the historic centre of Venice.
In 1951, there were approximately 175 000 inhabitants.
In July, an estimated 2 000 Venetians demonstrated against mass tourism.
According to them, tourism has weakened their quality of life, destroys the environment and drives residents away.
Approximately 2 000 inhabitants leave each year.
If this continues, after a few years there will only be tourists in Venice.
It would be a social, anthropological and historical catastrophe, says Carlo Belframe, who hosted the demonstration.
The equation is difficult, as the vessels bring the incomes it desperately needs to Venice and create a need for numerous jobs in different sectors.
In addition to limiting the number of tourists, the solution is planned that cruisers would go further away from the historic centre.
Luciano Bortot, who lives in Venice, thinks that the situation would certainly change if Veneto's administrative district, where Venice is also located, could have more power to decide its own affairs - if not full autonomy.
Venice is visited every year by about 20-30 million tourists.
Are you driving towards Kuopio?
Bad traffic jams on the motorway
On the motorway between Kuopio and Siilinjärvi, there has been traffic jams of kilometres during Thursday afternoon's return traffic.
Retreading of the motorway will be carried out in the area, which will slow down traffic, says Savon Sanomat.
There is a speed limit of 50 km/h instead of the usual speed of 120 km/h.
However, even in the 50-kilometre restricted area, traffic had progressed at practically a walking pace on Thursday afternoon.
On Thursday afternoon, roadworks proceeded on the Siilinjärvi side at the Revelation statue, i.e. the Golden Capricorn.
I get along well without such attention
The 23-year-old runner has had to notice the curses of publicity.
Amalie Iuel has published stunning photographs of herself, for example, in the landscapes of the Grand Canyon.
Danish-Norwegian Amalie Iuel has emerged as a popular athlete in her current home country of Norway, not least because in March she broke the 26-year-old Norway's record in the 400-meter race by orbiting during the track lap of 51:81.
A month later, he improved the 400-meter hurdle at 55:38 of Norway's hurdles record.
Soon after his record-breaking records, Iuel discovered that celebrity and publicity have their downsides.
Men from all over the world began to send images of their genital organs, apparently, in the hope that the young runner would become interested in them.
Iuel was forced to close his Snapchat account from outsiders.
“I got a lot of ‘disturbances’ from strangers, so I had to turn my account private,” Iuel told VG magazine.
In an interview with VG, Iuel did not want to specify exactly what kind of images he received.
You can probably guess, Iuel laughed.
It's nice to get feedback after the run, but I'll get along well without that kind of attention.
Iuel was born in Denmark in 1994.
His mother is Danish, his father is Danish-Norwegian.
Iuel's family moved to Norway when Amalie was 2 years old.
When Amalie was 12 years old, the family first moved to Namibia because of their father's work, then to Pakistan.
It was followed by Dubai, Thailand and the United States.
Iuel spent four years as a university athlete (University of Southern California) in Los Angeles, from where he returned to Norway.
In June 2015, he changed Danish citizenship to Norwegian citizenship.
In Norway, Iuel trains under the guidance of Leif Olav Alnes, Karsten Warholm and Elisabeth Slettum.
Norway is much more open to discussing than in the United States.
In the United States, coaches don't ask for anything but just give orders.
In Norway, the coach has a lot more time to be personally involved with the athlete, Iuel told VG.
Amalie Iuel holds Norway's records in the 400 meters and the 400-meter hurdles.
A foot injury put snowboarder Janne Korpi's entire career at stake - now participating in the World Championships: "It's nice when you don't get anywhere"
Now it's been nice to count when nothing happened!
Janne Korpi, 31, one of Finland's most successful snowboarders, has been able to really enjoy her beloved sport again this season.
Last summer, the continuation of his career was at stake.
Korpi's stress injury had forced Korpi on the operating table in January, and the foot didn't want to heal.
My hand had taken a hit on the race slope.
In the summer, I didn't know what I was going to do, whether the snowboarding would continue or not.
The sponsorships were interrupted and many things were open, Korpi says.
But that's how it's said that things tend to work out.
The leg healed, the sponsor extended the contract for two years, and the blood began to draw to the slope again.
The decision was easy after that.
At the latest, I knew it when I was in the snow for the first time.
I was like, damn, it's so much fun again, let's do a couple more seasons.
Korpi didn't get to count properly until September-October, but after a long recession, it's been a big thing to train painless.
Only a few races have been toured by Korpi.
It doesn't matter. It doesn't matter.
Competitions have never been the most important aspect of snowboarding.
I've never counted because of one race.
I think it's frustrating to aim for four years in one race, and then you'll have a fever that week.
That's what I can't think, Korpi says.
Today Korpi competes in the halfpipe of Spain's World Cup in Sierra Nevada.
Even thoughous individual races don't keep him involved in snowboarding, he's always igniting to compete in tough company.
According to Korpi, the valuation of competitions organized by the International Ski Federation has increased in recent years, even though some of the snowboarders chose Norway's X-Games to be held at the same time.
It’s an important race, the visibility is big.
However, in my opinion, appreciation always depends on the circumstances and the level of the competition.
Korpi is the oldest athlete of Finland's 12-piece team in Sierra Nevada.
The team's youngest, 17-year-old Rene Rinnekangas and Emmi Parkkisenniemi, are 14 years younger than Korpe.
It's nice to keep an eye out with your younger friends.
I remember when everything was new and was so sickly excited about everything.
It also brings new energy to myself, Korpi thinks.
I'll be able to keep up somehow on the slopes, but otherwise I won't, that's how much there's power in my friends!
Against the background of his boarding career, Janne Korpi has also built a different kind of sports career.
The son of directing legend Pekka Korpi is addicted to trotting sports: the rider has his own trotting horses, for whom he makes training programs.
When I'm on a trip all the time, I don't have time to be there anymore.
But every day, I look at least for the trotting results and usually some departures, Korpi says.
According to Korpi, trotting and trotting coaching are a potential career plan when a snowboarding career comes to an end.
I won't plan that far, but it's at least a probable option.
I feel that it's like I'm studying it in addition to snowboarding.
I'm trying to stay up to date and develop, maybe I'll drive some departures in the summer.
If it's my future profession, then it's not hard to start when you're already inside the sport.
Trotting also includes an Elitloppet race cruise organised with presenter Jaajo Linnonmaa.
The duo has rented a cruise ship, which will head to the Elitloppet race in Stockholm at the end of May.
Korpi and Linnonmaa decided to revive the old tradition.
The cruise has sold well, and people have been thrilled.
We are already at the point where there will be no bankruptcy.
We still have a few months left, let's hope that the ship will be filled," Korpi says excitedly.
Attendo's nursing home project in Kitee has surprised everyone
Attendo Esteri is the name of a new 41-seat elderly care home to be built in Kitee.
A building of more than 1,700 square metres of square metres will probably be completed next summer on the other side of Terveystie road next door to the health centre.
A common cause for concern in Kitee is the number of customers in the new care unit, as many private nursing homes suffer from customer shortages during Siun sote.
Anne Flink, Regional Director of Care Services at Attendo, commented that there will be more demand in Kitee in the future, not only for alternatives, but also for intensified service housing.
All kinds of services are needed, and when there are alternatives, freedom of choice also works better, Flink says.
Last year, Attendo bought Kanervikkola and Eerika private nursing homes from Kitee.
The transaction concerned the business, but there is reportedly a five-year lease agreement for the properties.
Flink says Attendo doesn't have an acute need to move operations away from these facilities.
More on the subject later today.
GMB is concerned about McVitie’s brand’s Scottish factory’s continued operations
The trade union is seeking confirmation from McVitie’s invention manufacturer’s executives for long-term commitment to production at the Glasgow plant.
According to GMB's Scottish department, the company has not announced its plans for the future of the Tollcross plant.
Hobnobs and Rich Tea biscuits and other products have more than 500 employees.
Pladis, which owns the McVitie’s brand, said it had invested more than five million pounds in the plant in recent years.
The company added that employees and trade union representatives will ‘certainly be the first’ if there are any changes.
In a letter addressed to the factory manager, GMB urges the company ‘responsing that no funding has been allocated to modernise production lines’.
Drew Duffy of the GMB trade union finds it extremely worrying that the factory manager doesn't have clear information about Tollcross's business needs and future investment plans.
‘Families have worked here from generation to generation, and the workforce is heavily concentrated in the local community and the metropolitan area of Glasgow. The factory is a significant source of employment in the area of high unemployment and low livelihoods.
From the point of view of jobs, wages and livelihoods, as well as Brexit, rising living costs and the continuous reduction in production, eastern Glasgow desperately needs successful McVitie’s production.”
According to Pladis' spokesperson, the factory manager had only received a trade union letter the day before, and the company responds directly to the letter because it appreciates continuous interaction with employees and trade union representatives.
“We had a regular meeting with employees and GMB’s representatives at Tollcross two months ago, when we openly discussed a number of issues.
Among other things, we discussed with employees that, like other companies operating in a highly competitive and precavious market, we are constantly evaluating all of our businesses and offices to maintain our competitiveness.”
According to a spokesperson, the company has invested more than 5 million pounds in recent years in the Tollcross factory, especially its production lines and operational improvement.
“We are aware of Tollcross’s long history as a production facility, and we will certainly be the first to tell employees and trade union representatives if we intend to make changes to the plant’s operations.”
Did PSG warn Barcelona about Neymar's transfer three years ago?
LaLiga may regret their interest in Marco Verratti
Paris Saint-Germain won't give up Neymar's run after paying a 189-million-pound transfer to terminate the contract, but did PSG warn Barcelona of a record-breaking transfer three years ago?
The sensational move is the most talked-about incident of the summer. France's Ligue 1 football team is ready to break the world record for correction amounts. The previous record was less than halves lower: it was created when Paul Pogba returned to Manchester United with an 89-million-pound convocation.
After the postponement, Neymar will become the world's most expensive footballer and also the world's highest-paid professional athlete, as PSG will offer him more than 500 pounds a weekly salary after taxes.
Barcelona is known to be outraged by PSG's actions and the way Neymar has forcibly left the club. According to a source close to Barcelona, the club has been taking very good care of Neymar since he arrived in Santos in 2013.
Did PSG's president warn about this move three years ago?
When Manchester City announced that they were interested in Blaise Matuid in 2014, Nasser Al-Khelaifi warned clubs looking for PSG's best players.
“I’m not worried because they know that if they’re looking for our players, we’re going to become their opponent and we’re going to get in touch with their players,” Al-Khelaifi said.
“We respect all clubs.
But if they're looking for our players, they’ll have to face the consequences.”
Barcelona seems to have done just that: it expressed its interest in PSG's midfielder Marco Compared earlier this summer.
PSG made it clear that it wouldn't let the Italian leave under any circumstances, and it may well be that Barcelona will lose their star player Neymar.
