‘NOW’: Trump’s phone calls were fabricated – White House spokesman: ‘I wouldn’t say it was a lie’
“The White House acknowledges that two of U.S. President Donald Trump’s phone calls never happened,” the New York Times reported.
The phone calls incensed by Trump with both the Mexican president and the leader of the scout organization have turned out to be invented.
White House spokeswoman Sarah Huckabee Sanders confirmed that there had been no calls.
He said Trump didn't lie.
According to Sanders, a number of scout leaders spoke to Trump in person, and with Mexican President Enrique Pena Nieto, Trump had held talks at the G20 in Hamburg.
I wouldn't say it's a lie, it's a pretty harsh accusation.
Those conversations took place, they didn't just happen on the phone, but face-to-face," Sanders reasoned.
Speech to the scouts provoked disapproval
On Monday, Trump said the Mexican president called him and praised Trump's success in eradicating illegal immigration.
According to Trump, Pena Nieto reported that few will come across Mexico's southern border anymore, as they know a trip to the U.S. border anyway.
On Wednesday, the Mexican government issued a statement saying there was no such call.
On Tuesday, Politico released a complete version of Trump's interview with The Wall Street Journal.
In it, Trump praised his speech at a major meeting of the National Scouting Association in West Virginia.
“I got a call from the Boy Scout leader,” he said it was the finest speech they’ve ever had.
“They were very grateful,” Trump said.
On Wednesday, the scout agency said it was unaware of any of its executives' phone call to Trump.
According to the statement, Michael Surbaugh, the head of the organization, in which he had apologized to Trump's speech from the scouts, speaks for himself.
Trump's speech at the Scout's meeting aroused a lot of surprise, as it was exceptionally political in relation to the nature of the event.
Among other things, he praised the results of last autumn's presidential elections.
